dding them:\n\nLet me write them aligned properly:\n\n        631,529,940\n      +831,901,572\n      -------------\n\nLet me add each column starting from the right.\n\nStarting with the ones place (rightmost digit):\n\n0 + 2 = 2. Write 2. No carryover.\n\nTens place: 4 + 7 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds place: 9 (from first number) + 5 (from second) = 14, plus 1 carried over = 15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place: 9 + 1 = 10, plus 1 = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands place: 2 + 0 = 2, plus 1 = 3. Write 3, carryover 0.\n\nHundred thousands place:5 (from first number) +9 (from second) =14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nMillions place:1 (from first number) +1 (from second) =2, plus carryover 1 =3. Write 3, carryover 0.\n\nTen millions place:3 +3 =6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\nHundred millions place:6 +8 =14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nFinally, the billions place: the carryover 1. Since both numbers have 9 digits, adding gives a 10-digit number starting with 1.\n\nSo putting it all together:\n\nThe digits from right to left are:\n\nOnes:2, tens:1, hundreds:5, thousands:1, ten thousands:3, hundred thousands:4, millions:3, ten millions:6, hundred millions:4, and the final carryover 1 in the billions place.\n\nThus, the number is 1,463,431,512.\n\nWait, but the user's answer is 1,463,431,812. Hmm, so where did I go wrong?\n\nWait, let me check the thousands place again. Let's recount each step carefully.\n\nFirst number breakdown:\n\n631,529,940:\n\nBreaking down each digit from right:\n\nPosition 1 (ones):0\n\nPosition2 (tens):4\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9\n\nPosition4 (thousands):9\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):2\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):5\n\nPosition7 (millions):1\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):6\n\nSecond number:831,901,572:\n\nPosition1 (ones):2\n\nPosition2 (tens):7\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):5\n\nPosition4 (thousands):1\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):0\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):9\n\nPosition7 (millions):1\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):8\n\nNow, let's go step by step:\n\nPosition1 (ones):0+2=2. Correct.\n\nPosition2 (tens):4+7=11. 1 written, carryover1. Correct.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9 (first number) +5 (second number) =14, plus carryover1=15. So 5 written, carryover1. Correct.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):9 (first) +1 (second) =10, plus carryover1=11. So 1 written, carryover1. Correct.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):2 +0=2, plus carryover1=3. So 3 written, no carryover. Correct.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):5 +9=14. 4 written, carryover1. Correct.\n\nPosition7 (millions):1 (first) +1 (second)=2, plus carryover1=3. 3 written, no carryover. Correct.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3 +3=6. 6 written, no carryover. Correct.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):6 +8=14. 4 written, carryover1.\n\nSo the total is 1 (carryover) followed by the digits: 4 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions),3 (millions),4 (hundred thousands),3 (ten thousands),1 (thousands),5 (hundreds),1 (tens),2 (ones). So that's 1,463,431,512.\n\nBut the user's answer is 1,463,431,812. Hmm, so there's a discrepancy in the thousands place. Let me check the thousands place again.\n\nPosition4 (thousands place):\n\nFirst number: thousands digit is 9 (since in 631,529,940, the thousands digit is the 9 in ...9940. The thousands place is the fourth digit from the right. Let's count:\n\n631,529,940:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n...940 is the last three digits (hundreds, tens, ones: 9 (hundreds),4 (tens),0 (ones). Then the thousands place is the next digit to the left, which is the first 9 in ...29940. So yes, 9 in thousands place.\n\nSecond number:831,901,572.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n...572 is the last three digits (5 (hundreds),7 (tens),2 (ones). Then thousands digit is the next to the left: the 1 in ...1572 (since 831,901,572: the thousands place is the 1 in the ...01,572. Wait, let's see:\n\n831,901,572:\n\nBreaking into parts: 831 (millions), 901 (thousands), 572 (units). So the thousands digit is the third digit from the right in the thousands group. Wait, maybe better to count:\n\nThe number is 8 3 1 , 9 0 1 , 5 7 2.\n\nSo the thousands digit is the first digit of the thousands group, which is 9? Wait, no:\n\nWait, commas separate every three digits from the right. So:\n\n831,901,572:\n\n- The first comma separates the millions: 831 (millions).\n\n- The next comma separates the thousands: 901 (thousands).\n\n- The last three digits are 572 (units).\n\nTherefore, the thousands digit is the first digit of the thousands group, which is 9 (from 901). Wait, so thousands place is 9?\n\nWait, no. Wait, the thousands place is the fourth digit from the right. Let's count digits:\n\nNumber: 8 3 1 9 0 1 5 7 2.\n\nPositions from the right (ones is position1):\n\nPosition1:2 (ones)\n\nPosition2:7 (tens)\n\nPosition3:5 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:1 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:0 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:9 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:3 (millions)\n\nPosition8:1 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:8 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, so the thousands place (position4) is the fourth digit from the right, which is the '1' in the number 831,901,572. So yes, the thousands digit is 1. Therefore, when adding the thousands place:\n\nFirst number's thousands digit is 9, second is 1, so 9+1=10 plus carryover1 (from previous step which was position3's carryover) gives 11. So that's correct. So the thousands digit in the result is 1, with a carryover of 1 to the next (ten thousands) place.\n\nSo the thousands digit is 1, so in the final number, the thousands place is 1. Therefore, the number should have 1 in thousands place.\n\nBut in the user's answer, 1,463,431,812, the thousands digit is 1 (since 431,812 is part of it). Let me see:\n\nWait, the user's answer is 1,463,431,812. Breaking it down:\n\n1,463,431,812:.\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:4\n\nTen millions:6\n\nMillions:3\n\nHundred thousands:4\n\nTen thousands:3\n\nThousands:1\n\nHundreds:8\n\nTens:1\n\nOnes:2\n\nWait, so the hundreds place is 8 here, but according to my calculation, the hundreds place was 5. That's where the error is. So my calculation had the hundreds digit as 5, but user's answer has 8 in hundreds place.\n\nHmm, so that must mean I made a mistake in the hundreds place addition.\n\nLet me recheck the hundreds place.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds place):\n\nFirst number's hundreds digit:9 (from 631,529,940's breakdown, the hundreds digit is the third from the right, which is 9 in ...9940. So yes, 9.\n\nSecond number's hundreds digit:5 (from 831,901,572's breakdown, the hundreds digit is 5 in ...572. So yes, 5.\n\nAdding those:9+5=14, plus the carryover from the previous step (tens place carryover1) gives 15. So the hundreds digit is 5 with a carryover of 1. So that's correct.\n\nSo in the user's answer, the hundreds digit is 8, which suggests they might have added differently. Alternatively, maybe I messed up the positions.\n\nWait, let me look at the user's answer again: 1,463,431,812. Let's parse each digit:\n\nBreaking down the answer:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:2 (matches)\n\nTens:1 (matches my calculation)\n\nHundreds:8 (mine was 5, so discrepancy here)\n\nThousands:1 (matches)\n\nTen thousands:3 (matches)\n\nHundred thousands:4 (matches)\n\nMillions:3 (matches)\n\nTen millions:6 (matches)\n\nHundred millions:4 (matches)\n\nBillions:1 (matches)\n\nSo the problem is at the hundreds place. How did the user get 8 there?\n\nLet me check the hundreds place again. Maybe I miscounted the digits.\n\nWait, let's re-express both numbers with their digits clearly:\n\nFirst number:631,529,940.\n\nBreaking down digit by digit from left to right:\n\nDigit 1:6 (hundred millions)\n\nDigit2:3 (ten millions)\n\nDigit3:1 (millions)\n\nDigit4:5 (hundred thousands)\n\nDigit5:2 (ten thousands)\n\nDigit6:9 (thousands)\n\nDigit7:9 (hundreds)\n\nDigit8:4 (tens)\n\nDigit9:0 (ones)\n\nWait a second! Wait a minute! Here's the mistake! Oh no, I think I made an error in digit positions earlier.\n\nWait, when I listed the digits from left to right, I might have miscounted the positions. Let me redo this.\n\nFirst number: 631,529,940.\n\nBreaking it down digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the left:\n\n1st digit:6 (hundred millions place)\n\n2nd digit:3 (ten millions)\n\n3rd digit:1 (millions)\n\n4th digit:5 (hundred thousands)\n\n5th digit:2 (ten thousands)\n\n6th digit:9 (thousands)\n\n7th digit:9 (hundreds)\n\n8th digit:4 (tens)\n\n9th digit:0 (ones)\n\nTherefore, the hundreds place is the 7th digit from the left, which is 9. Correct.\n\nSecond number:831,901,572.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n1st digit:8 (hundred millions)\n\n2nd digit:3 (ten millions)\n\n3rd digit:1 (millions)\n\n4th digit:9 (hundred thousands)\n\n5th digit:0 (ten thousands)\n\n6th digit:1 (thousands)\n\n7th digit:5 (hundreds)\n\n8th digit:7 (tens)\n\n9th digit:2 (ones)\n\nTherefore, the hundreds digit is the 7th digit, which is 5. So adding 9 (first) +5 (second) =14 plus carryover gives 15. So the hundreds digit is 5 with a carryover. So my previous calculation was correct.\n\nBut the user's answer has 8 in hundreds place. So where did that come from?\n\nWait, maybe the user added the thousands place incorrectly?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made a mistake in another digit.\n\nWait, let's try adding the numbers another way, maybe column-wise:\n\nLet me write them out aligned:\n\n        631,529,940\n      +831,901,572\n      -------------\nLet's add column by column from right:\n\nStarting with the ones column (units):\n\n0 + 2 = 2. Write 2.\n\nTens column:4 +7=11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds column:9 +5=14 plus 1 is 15. Write 5, carryover1.\n\nThousands column:9 (from first number) +1 (from second) =10 plus 1 =11. Write 1, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands column:2 +0 =2 +1 =3. Write3.\n\nHundred thousands column:5 +9=14. Write4, carryover1.\n\nMillions column:1 +1=2 plus 1 =3. Write3.\n\nTen millions column:3 +3=6.\n\nHundred millions column:6 +8=14. Write4, carryover1.\n\nFinally, the carryover1 becomes