final_output:  ["815,412,933? Wait, but the user said they think the answer is 815,112,933. Hmm, there's a discrepancy here. Let me check again.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the places. Let me write it out more carefully, digit by digit.\n\nLet's break down each digit position:\n\nNumber1: 4 1 7 2 6 4 4 2 2\n\nNumber2: 3 9 8 1 4 8 5 1 1\n\nWait, actually, let me write them with commas to see the places better.\n\nNumber1: 417,264,422 → digits from right: positions 1 to 9:\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 2\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 2\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):4\n\nPosition4 (thousands):4\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):2\n\nPosition7 (millions):7\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):1\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):4\n\nWait, actually, commas are placed every three digits. So 417,264,422 is 4 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 2 (ones). Wait, maybe I got the positions wrong earlier.\n\nWait, let me parse each number digit by digit properly:\n\nFirst number: 417264422.\n\nBreaking down:\n\nDigits (from left to right):\n\n1. 4 (hundred millions place)\n\n2. 1 (ten millions)\n\n3. 7 (millions)\n\n4. 2 (hundred thousands)\n\n5. 6 (ten thousands)\n\n6. 4 (thousands)\n\n7. 4 (hundreds)\n\n8. 2 (tens)\n\n9. 2 (ones)\n\nSecond number: 398148511.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n1. 3 (hundred millions)\n\n2. 9 (ten millions)\n\n3. 8 (millions)\n\n4. 1 (hundred thousands)\n\n5. 4 (ten thousands)\n\n6. 8 (thousands)\n\n7. 5 (hundreds)\n\n8. 1 (tens)\n\n9. 1 (ones)\n\nNow, adding digit by digit from right to left (starting at position 9, ones):\n\nPosition 9 (ones):\n\n2 (from first) +1 (second) = 3. Write 3, carryover 0.\n\nPosition8 (tens):\n\n2 +1 =3. Write 3, carryover 0.\n\nPosition7 (hundreds):\n\n4 +5 =9. Write 9, carryover 0.\n\nPosition6 (thousands):\n\n4 (first) +8 (second) =12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\n6 (first) +4 (second) =10. Plus carryover 1 →