ed millions).\n\nSecond number: 580,931,369. Breaking down:.\n- 9 (ones).\n- 6 (tens).\n- 3 (hundreds).\n- 1 (thousands).\n- 3 (ten thousands).\n- 9 (hundred thousands).\n- 0 (millions).\n- 8 (ten millions).\n- 5 (hundred millions).\n\nSo aligning them properly:.\n\n        9 0 8 8 3 5 4 1 6.\n      + 5 8 0 9 3 1 3 6 9.\n      --------------------.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):.\n\n1. Ones: 6 + 9 = 15 → write 5, carryover 1.\n2. Tens: 1 + 6 = 7, plus carryover 1 → 8. No carryover.\n3. Hundreds: 4 + 3 = 7 → write 7.\n4. Thousands: 5 + 1 = 6 → write 6.\n5. Ten thousands: 3 + 3 = 6 → write 6.\n6. Hundred thousands: 8 + 9 = 17 → write 7, carryover 1.\n7. Millions: 8 (from first) + 0 (from second) + 1 (carryover) = 9 → write 9.\n8. Ten millions: 0 + 8 = 8 → write 8.\n9. Hundred millions: 9 + 5 = 14 → write 4 and carryover 1. But since there's no higher place, that carryover becomes a new digit at the beginning.\n\nWait a second, when adding the hundred millions place, 9 +5 =14, so we need to add that 1 as a new digit in the billions place. So the total sum would have a 1 in the billions, then 4 in hundred millions, etc.\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the right, the digits we have are:.\n\nOnes:5, Tens:8, Hundreds:7, Thousands:6, Ten thousands:6, Hundred thousands:7, Millions:9, Ten millions:8, Hundred millions:4, plus the carried-over 1 in the billions place</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
