us 800,000,000 is 1,200,000,000. So that would actually be a 1 in the billions place and 2 in the hundred millions. Oh right! I forgot that adding those two hundred millions would exceed 999,999,999, so we need to consider the next digit. So the total sum would be over a billion. Let me recalculate that last part.\n\nStarting over step by step to make sure:.\n\nLet me write the numbers vertically:.\n\n      4 2 7 8 5 2 6 6 9.\n    + 8 5 4 7 2 4 4 3 5.\n    ----------------------.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nOnes: 9 + 5 = 14 → 4, carry 1.\n\nTens: 6 + 3 = 9 + 1 = 10 → 0, carry 1.\n\nHundreds: 6 + 4 = 10 +1 = 11 → 1, carry 1.\n\nThousands: 2 + 4 = 6 +1 =7 → 7, no carry.\n\nTen-thousands: 5 + 2 =7 → 7.\n\nHundred-thousands: 8 +7 =15 → 5, carry 1.\n\nMillions: 7 +4 =11 +1 =12 → 2, carry1.\n\nTen-millions: 2 +5 =7 +1=8 →8, no carry.\n\nHundred-millions:4 +8 =12 → here, since this is the highest digit, we can't just write 12. Wait, actually, the hundred millions place is the 9th digit from the right. When adding 4 (hundred millions) and 8 (hundred millions), their sum is 12 hundred million, which is 1,200,000,000. So that would be 2 in the hundred millions place and carry over 1 to a new billions place. Since the original numbers don't have a billions digit, this carry creates a new digit. So the total sum will have 10 digits. .\n\nTherefore, the final digits would be:.\n\nBillions place: 1 (from the carry).\n\nHundred millions: 2 (since 12-10=2)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
