ds place? Wait, let's check thousands place again.\n\nFirst number's thousands digit is 1 (since it's 160,261,934 \u2192 breakdown:\n\n160,261,934: The thousands place is the fourth digit from the right. Let's count:\n\nStarting from the right: 4 (ones),3 (tens),9 (hundreds),1 (thousands),6 (ten thousands),2 (hundred thousands),0 (millions),6 (ten millions),1 (hundred millions). Yes, thousands digit is 1.\n\nSecond number's thousands digit: 616,579,138 \u2192 breakdown:\n\nFrom right:8,3,1,9 (thousands),7 (ten thousands),5 (hundred thousands),6 (millions),1 (ten millions),6 (hundred millions). So thousands digit is 9.\n\nSo adding thousands digits:1+9=10 plus any carry?\n\nWait, when adding the thousands place, the previous carry was from hundreds place which was 1. So 1 (first) +9 (second) +1 (carry) =11. So thousands digit is 1, carryover 1. That part is correct.\n\nTen thousands place: first has 6 (ten thousands place), second has 7 (ten thousands). Adding 6+7+1 (carry) =14. So ten thousands digit is 4, carryover1. Correct.\n\nHundred thousands place: first has 2 (hundred thousands), second has5 (hundred thousands) plus carry 1: 2+5+1=8. So that's correct. \n\nMillions place: first has 0, second has6 \u21926. \n\nTen millions: 6 +1 =7. Hundred millions 1+6=7. \n\nSo the total is indeed 776,841,072. But the user's answer is 776,841,172. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait, maybe I made an error in the digit positions. Let me write the numbers without commas for clarity.\n\nFirst number: 160261934\n\nSecond number:616579138\n\nAdding digit by digit:\n\nStarting from right:\n\nPosition 1 (ones):4+8=12 \u21922, carry1\n\nPosition 2 (tens):3+3=6 +1=7 \u21927, no carry.\n\nWait a second! Wait, in my previous steps I thought the tens digits were 3 and 3. Let me confirm.\n\nFirst number's tens digit: the second digit from right in 160261934 is 3 (since it's ...934, so 3 in tens place). Second number's tens digit: in 616579138, the second from right is 3 (the number ends with 138, so tens is 3). So 3+3=6 plus the carryover 1 from ones makes 7. So that's correct. So no carryover here.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds): 9 (from first's hundreds) +1 (second's hundreds digit: third from right in 616579138 is 1 (since 138: hundreds is 1). So 9+1=10 \u21920, carry1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands): first number's thousands digit is 1 (fourth from right: 1 in 160261934 is the thousands place). Second's thousands digit is 9 (fourth from right in 616579138 is 9, since it's ...9138 \u2192 thousands is 9). So 1+9=10 plus carry 1 \u219211. So thousands place 1, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands): first's ten thousands is 6 (fifth from right in 160261934: let's count: 4 (1),3 (2),9 (3),1 (4),6 (5)). Second's ten thousands digit: fifth from right in 616579138 is 7 (since ...79138 \u2192 the fifth is 7). So 6+7 +1 (carry)=14 \u21924, carry1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands): first has 2 (sixth from right in 160261934 is 2). Second has 5 (sixth from right in 616579138 is 5). So 2+5 +1=8. Correct, no carry.\n\nPosition7 (millions): first has 0 (seventh from right in 160261934 is 0). Second has6 (seventh in 616579138 is 6). 0+6=6.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions): first has6 (eighth from right in 160... is 6). Second has1 (eighth from right in 616579138 is 1). 6+1=7.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions): first has1, second has6. Total 7.\n\nSo putting all together: 7 (hundred millions),7 (ten millions),6 (millions),8 (hundred thousands),4 (ten thousands),1 (thousands),0 (hundreds),7 (tens),2 (ones). Wait, that would be 776,841,072. But the user's answer is 776,841,172. There's a difference in the thousands place? Wait, the thousands digit here is 1, so the number would be 776,841,072. The user's answer has 1 in the thousands place and 7 in the hundreds? Wait no, let me see.\n\nWait, the digits are:\n\nFrom highest to lowest:\n\nHundred millions:7\n\nTen millions:7\n\nMillions:6\n\nHundred thousands:8\n\nTen thousands:4\n\nThousands:1\n\nHundreds:0\n\nTens:7\n\nOnes:2.\n\nSo