\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n3 +5 =8 +1 (carry)=9.\n\nSo the digits from position1 to position9 are:\n\n8 (ones), 8 (tens),1 (hundreds),7 (thousands),2 (ten thousands),9 (hundred thousands),7 (millions),2 (ten millions),9 (hundred millions).\n\nSo writing the number from highest to lowest (position9 to position1):\n\n9 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 7 (millions),9 (hundred thousands),2 (ten thousands),7 (thousands),1 (hundreds),8 (tens),8 (ones).\n\nWait, but that would be 927,927,188.\n\nBut the second method gave 927,297,188.\n\nHmm, so which is correct?\n\nWait, perhaps in the first method, the millions digit was miscalculated.\n\nWait in position7 (millions place):\n\nFirst number has 9 in millions, second has 8 in millions? Let me confirm.\n\nWait the first number is 369,074,769.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n3 (hundred millions),6 (ten millions),9 (millions),0 (hundred thousands),7 (ten thousands),4 (thousands),7 (hundreds),6 (tens),9 (ones).\n\nWait, perhaps I made an error in the breakdown of the first number's digits.\n\nWait 369,074,769.\n\nLet me parse each digit correctly:\n\nThe number is 3,6,9,0,7,4,7,6,9.\n\nFrom left to right:\n\nDigit 1 (hundred millions):3\n\nDigit2 (ten millions):6\n\nDigit3 (millions):9\n\nDigit4 (hundred thousands):0\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):7\n\nDigit6 (thousands):4\n\nDigit7 (hundreds):7\n\nDigit8 (tens):6\n\nDigit9 (ones):9.\n\nWait, no, perhaps my initial breakdown was wrong. Let's write the number with commas: 369,074,769.\n\nSo 369,074,769 is:\n\n3 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 9 (millions),\n\n0 (hundred thousands),7 (ten thousands),4 (thousands),\n\n7 (hundreds),6 (tens),9 (ones).\n\nTherefore, the millions digit (third digit from the left) is 9, but the hundred thousands place is 0, then ten thousands is 7, etc.\n\nSo when adding the millions place (position7 in the digit list), the first number has 9, and the second number's millions digit is 8 (since the second number is 558,222,419 \u2192 5 (hundred mi