dred millions)\n\nNow, let's re-add each position step by step:\n\nPosition1 (ones):4+7=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):3+1 +1=5 \u21925.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):2+8=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):7+7=14 +1=15 \u21925, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3+6=9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):3+2=5 +1=6 \u21926.\n\nPosition7 (millions):9+5=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):4+7=11 +1=12 \u21922, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):4+7=11 +1=12 \u21922, carryover1.\n\nNow, the carryover1 is added as the next digit (billions place).\n\nSo the digits from position10 down to 1 are:\n\nPosition10:1,\n\nPosition9:2,\n\nPosition8:2,\n\nPosition7:4,\n\nPosition6:6,\n\nPosition5:0,\n\nPosition4:5,\n\nPosition3:0,\n\nPosition2:5,\n\nPosition1:1.\n\nWait, but when you arrange the digits in order (billions down to ones):\n\n1 (billions),\n\n2 (hundred millions),\n\n2 (ten millions),\n\n4 (millions),\n\n6 (hundred thousands),\n\n0 (ten thousands),\n\n5 (thousands),\n\n0 (hundreds),\n\n5 (tens),\n\n1 (ones).\n\nSo that's 1,224,605,051.\n\nBut the step-by-step addition gave me 1,206,605,051. The discrepancy is in the ten millions and hundred millions place.\n\nAh! Wait a second. In the digit-by-digit method, when adding the ten millions place (position8):\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is 4 (since the first number is 449,337,234: 4 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 9 (millions)...).\n\nSecond number's ten millions digit is 7 (since 757,267,817 has 7 in the ten millions place).\n\nSo adding those:4 +7 =11, plus the carryover1 from the millions place gives 12. So that's 2 with carryover1. So ten millions place is 2.\n\nHundred millions place (position9): first number's digit is4, second's is7. 4+7=11 plus carryover1 gives 12 \u21922, carryover1.\n\nSo the hundred millions digit is 2, and carryover1 to the billions.\n\nBut in the step-by-step addition, the hundred millions place would be 