dred thousands).\n\nPosition7:5 (millions).\n\nPosition8:7 (ten millions).\n\nPosition9:7 (hundred millions).\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nPosition1:4+7=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nPosition2:3+1 +1=5 →5, carryover0.\n\nPosition3:2+8=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nPosition4:7+7=14 +1=15 →5, carryover1.\n\nPosition5:3+6=9 +1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nPosition6:3+2=5 +1=6 →6, carryover0.\n\nPosition7:9+5=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nPosition8:4+7=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nPosition9:4+7=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nNow the carryover1 becomes the next digit (position10:1).\n\nSo the digits from position10 down to 1 are:\n\n1 (position10),\n\n2 (position9),\n\n2 (position8),\n\n4 (position7),\n\n6 (position6),\n\n0 (position5),\n\n5 (position4),\n\n0 (position3),\n\n5 (position2),\n\n1 (position1).\n\nWait, hold on. Position7: the digit from the millions place (position7) is 4, so that's the millions place. So the digit positions:\n\nPosition10: 1 (billions).\n\nPosition9:2 (hundred millions).\n\nPosition8:2 (ten millions).\n\nPosition7:4 (millions).\n\nPosition6:6 (hundred thousands).\n\nPosition5:0 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition4:5 (thousands).\n\nPosition3:0 (hundreds).\n\nPosition2:5 (tens).\n\nPosition1:1 (ones).\n\nTherefore, the number is 1,224,605,051.\n\nBut the user's proposed answer is 1,206,685,051. Hmm, so there's a difference here. That means I must have made a mistake in my calculation or the user's answer is incorrect.\n\nWait, let me verify with another method: adding the two numbers numerically:\n\n449,337,234\n\n+757,267,817\n\nLet's add them step by step:\n\nStart with the first number: 449,337,234.\n\nAdding 757,267,817:\n\nBreakdown:\n\n449,337,234 + 700,000,000 = 1,149,337,234.\n\nThen add 57,267,817 to that:\n\n1,149,337,234 + 50,000,000 = 1,199,337,234.\n\nThen add 7,267,817:\n\n1,199,337,234 + 7,000,000 = 1,206,337,234.\n\nThen add 267,817:\n\n1,206,337,234 + 200,000 = 1,206,537,234.\n\nThen add 67,817:\n\n1,206,537,234 + 60,000 = 1,206,597,234.\n\nThen 7,817:\n\n1,206,597,234 + 7,817 = 1,206,605,051.\n\nWait a minute! Wait, this is different. So here, adding step by step gives me 1,206,605,051. But earlier, my digit-by-digit addition gave me 1,224,605,051. There's a conflict here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let me check the step-by-step addition again carefully.\n\nOriginal numbers: 449,337,234 and 757,267,817.\n\nFirst method (digit-wise):\n\nGot 1,224,605,051.\n\nSecond method (breaking down addition):\n\n449,337,234 +757,267,817.\n\nBreaking into parts:\n\n757,267,817 = 700,000,000 + 50,000,000 + 7,000,000 + 200,000 + 60,000 + 7,000 + 800 + 10 +7.\n\nAdding step by step:\n\nStart with 449,337,234.\n\nAdd 700,000,000: 449,337,234 + 700,000,000 = 1,149,337,234.\n\nAdd 50,000,000: 1,149,337,234 +50,000,000 = 1,199,337,234.\n\nAdd 7,000,000: 1,199,337,234 +7,000,000 = 1,206,337,234.\n\nAdd 200,000: 1,206,337,234 +200,000 = 1,206,537,234.\n\nAdd 60,000: 1,206,537,234 +60,000 = 1,206,597,234.\n\nAdd 7,000: 1,206,597,234 +7,000 = 1,206,604,234.\n\nAdd 800: 1,206,604,234 +800 = 1,206,605,034.\n\nAdd 10: 1,206,605,034 +10 = 1,206,605,044.\n\nAdd 7: 1,206,605,044 +7 =1,206,605,051.\n\nSo that gives 1,206,605,051.\n\nWait, so this contradicts my first method's result of 1,224,605,051. So clearly, I made a mistake in the first digit-by-digit calculation.\n\nLooking back at the first method:\n\nLet me check the positions again. Maybe I miscounted the digits in the positions.\n\nWait let me re-examine the digit-by-digit addition:\n\nFirst number (449,337,234):\n\nPositions 1-9:\n\n1:4 (ones)\n\n2:3 (tens)\n\n3:2 (hundreds)\n\n4:7 (thousands)\n\n5:3 (ten thousands)\n\n6:3 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:9 (millions)\n\n8:4 (ten millions)\n\n9:4 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number (757,267,817):\n\n1:7 (ones)\n\n2:1 (tens)\n\n3:8 (hundreds)\n\n4:7 (thousands)\n\n5:6 (ten thousands)\n\n6:2 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:5 (millions)\n\n8:7 (ten millions)\n\n9:7 (hundred millions)\n\nNow, let's re-add each position step by step:\n\nPosition1 (ones):4+7=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):3+1 +1=5 →5.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):2+8=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):7+7=14 +1=15 →5, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3+6=9 +1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):3+2=5 +1=6 →6.\n\nPosition7 (millions):9+5=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):4+7=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):4+7=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nNow, the carryover1 is added as the next digit (billions place).\n\nSo the digits from position10 down to 1 are:\n\nPosition10:1,\n\nPosition9:2,\n\nPosition8:2,\n\nPosition7:4,\n\nPosition6:6,\n\nPosition5:0,\n\nPosition4:5,\n\nPosition3:0,\n\nPosition2:5,\n\nPosition1:1.\n\nWait, but when you arrange the digits in order (billions down to ones):\n\n1 (billions),\n\n2 (hundred millions),\n\n2 (ten millions),\n\n4 (millions),\n\n6 (hundred thousands),\n\n0 (ten thousands),\n\n5 (thousands),\n\n0 (hundreds),\n\n5 (tens),\n\n1 (ones).\n\nSo that's 1,224,605,051.\n\nBut the step-by-step addition gave me 1,206,605,051. The discrepancy is in the ten millions and hundred millions place.\n\nAh! Wait a second. In the digit-by-digit method, when adding the ten millions place (position8):\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is 4 (since the first number is 449,337,234: 4 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 9 (millions)...).\n\nSecond number's ten millions digit is 7 (since 757,267,817 has 7 in the ten millions place).\n\nSo adding those:4 +7 =11, plus the carryover1 from the millions place gives 12. So that's 2 with carryover1. So ten millions place is 2.\n\nHundred millions place (position9): first number's digit is4, second's is7. 4+7=11 plus carryover1 gives 12 →2, carryover1.\n\nSo the hundred millions digit is 2, and carryover1 to the billions.\n\nBut in the step-by-step addition, the hundred millions place would be 4 (from first number) +7 (second) =11, but that's part of the 700 million addition. Wait, perhaps the confusion is arising from different breakdowns.\n\nWait let me see: the step-by-step addition gives 1,206,605,051.\n\nBreaking down that number:\n\n1,206,605,051:\n\n- Billions:1.\n\n- Hundred millions:2.\n\n- Ten millions:0? Wait no.\n\nWait let's parse 1,206,605,051 properly with commas:\n\n1,206,605,051.\n\nIn the US numbering system, commas are placed every three digits starting from the right.\n\nSo:\n\n1,206,605,051 → breaks down as:\n\n1 (billion),\n\n206 (millions),\n\n605 (thousands),\n\n051 (units).\n\nWait, actually, commas are placed as:\n\n1,206,605,051 → 1,206,605,051.\n\nSo:\n\n- The first comma after the billions: 1,\n\n- Then 206 million,\n\n- Then 605 thousand,\n\n- Then 051.\n\nSo the number is 1,206,605,051.\n\nBreaking into digits:\n\nPosition10 (billions):1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):2.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):0? Wait, no. Wait let's see:\n\nThe number is 1,206,605,051.\n\nBreaking down each digit from right to left:\n\nPosition1 (ones):1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):5.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):0.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):5.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0.\n\nPosition7 (millions):6.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):0.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):2.\n\nPosition10 (billions):1.\n\nWait, that can't be. Wait let me write it out digit by digit:\n\nThe number is:\n\n1 (billions place),\n\n2 (hundred millions),\n\n0 (ten millions),\n\n6 (millions),\n\n6 (hundred thousands),\n\n0 (ten thousands),\n\n5 (thousands),\n\n0 (hundreds),\n\n5 (tens),\n\n1 (ones).\n\nWait, that doesn't align. Let me write the number as:\n\n1,206,605,051.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- Billions: 1.\n\n- Millions: 206.\n\n- Thousands: 605.\n\n- Units: 051.\n\nSo:\n\n- The digits are:\n\n1 (billion),\n\n2 (hundred millions),\n\n0 (ten millions),\n\n6 (millions),\n\n6 (hundred thousands),\n\n0 (ten thousands),\n\n5 (thousands),\n\n0 (hundreds),\n\n5 (tens),\n\n1 (ones).\n\nWait, so the ten millions digit is 0?\n\nBut in the digit-by-digit addition, we had the ten millions place as 2. So this discrepancy is because of an error in one of the methods.\n\nWait, where did I go wrong?\n\nLet me re-examine the digit-by-digit method:\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is4 (from 449,337,234: 4 in the hundred millions, 4 in ten millions, 9 in millions).\n\nSecond number's ten millions digit is7 (from 757,267,817: 7 in hundred millions, 7 in ten millions, 5 in millions).\n\nSo adding the ten millions digits:4 +7 =11, plus carryover1 from position7 (millions place addition).\n\nWait, let's retrace the carryovers step by step.\n\nStarting from position7 (millions place):\n\nFirst number's millions digit:9.\n\nSecond number's millions digit:5.\n\nSo 9+5=14. So we write down4 and carryover1.\n\nSo the carryover to position8 (ten millions) is1.\n\nTherefore, position8:4+7=11 +1 (carryover) =12. So 2, carryover1.\n\nSo ten millions digit is2, not 0. So in the final number, the ten millions place should be 2.\n\nBut in the step-by-step addition method, the ten millions place ended up as0?\n\nWait, in the step-by-step addition, when I broke down the numbers:\n\nFirst number is 449,337,234.\n\nSecond number is 757,267,817.\n\nAdding them:\n\n449,337,234 + 757,267,817.\n\nLet me write them out in expanded form:\n\n449,337,234 = 400,000,000 + 40,000,000 + 9,000,000 + 300,000 + 30,000 + 7,000 + 200 + 30 +4.\n\n757,267,817 = 700,000,000 + 50,000,000 +7,000,000 + 200,000 +60,000 +7,000 +800 +10 +7.\n\nAdding these term by term:\n\nBillions:\n\n400M +700M = 1,100,000,000.\n\nHundred millions:\n\n40M +50M =90,000,000.\n\nTen millions:\n\n9M +7M =16,000,000.\n\nMillions:\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, the first number's ten millions digit is4 (40,000,000) and the second's is7 (70,000,000).\n\nWait, actually, the breakdown should be:\n\nFirst number:\n\n- 4 (hundred millions): 400,000,000.\n\n- 4 (ten millions): 40,000,000.\n\n- 9 (millions):9,000,000.\n\nSecond number:\n\n-7 (hundred millions):700,000,000.\n\n-7 (ten millions):70,000,000.\n\n-5 (millions):5,000,000.\n\nSo adding the hundred millions:400M +700M =1,100M.\n\nTen millions:40M +70M =110M.\n\nMillions:9M +5M =14M.\n\nThen:\n\nHundred thousands:\n\nFirst number:3 (hundred thousands)=300,000.\n\nSecond:2 (hundred thousands)=200,000 → total 500,000.\n\nTen thousands:\n\nFirst:3 (ten thousands)=30,000.\n\nSecond:6 (ten thousands)=60,000 → total 90,000.\n\nThousands:\n\nFirst:7,000 + second:7,000 =14,000.\n\nHundreds:\n\nFirst:200 + second:800=1,000.\n\nTens:\n\nFirst:30 + second:10=40.\n\nOnes:\n\n4+7=11.\n\nNow adding all together:\n\nBillions:1,100,000,000.\n\nHundred millions:110,000,000 (wait, ten millions place is 110M, which is actually 100M +10M).\n\nWait, this is getting confusing. Let me sum all components:\n\nTotal = (1,100,000,000) + (40M+70M =110M) → Wait, no, actually:\n\nWait, the total should be:\n\nBillions:1,100,000,000 (from 400+700).\n\nTen millions:40M+70M =110,000,000.\n\nMillions:9M+5M=14,000,000.\n\nHundred thousands:300k+200k=500,000.\n\nTen thousands:30k+60k=90,000.\n\nThousands:7k+7k=14,000.\n\nHundreds:200+800=1,000.\n\nTens:30+10=40.\n\nOnes:4+7=11.\n\nNow adding all these:\n\nStart with 1,100,000,000.\n\nAdd 110,000,000 → total 1,210,000,000.\n\nAdd 14,000,000 →1,224,000,000.\n\nAdd 500,000 →1,224,500,000.\n\nAdd 90,000 →1,224,590,000.\n\nAdd 14,000 →1,224,604,000.\n\nAdd 1,000 →1,224,605,000.\n\nAdd 40 →1,224,605,040.\n\nAdd 11 →1,224,605,051.\n\nAh! So this confirms the digit-by-digit result:1,224,605,051.\n\nBut the step-by-step addition earlier had a different result. Where did I go wrong there?\n\nWait in the step-by-step addition earlier, I broke down the second number as 700M +50M +7M +200k +60k +7k +800 +10 +7.\n\nWait let's recalculate that step-by-step:\n\nStarting with first number:449,337,234.\n\nAdd 700,000,000 → total 1,149,337,234.\n\nThen add 50,000,000 →1,149,337,234 +50,000,000 =1,199,337,234.\n\nThen add 7,000,000 →1,199,337,234 +7,000,000 =1,206,337,234.\n\nWait here's the mistake! The 7,000,000 should be part of the millions place. But adding 7 million to 1,199,337,234:\n\n1,199,337,234 +7,000,000 =1,206,337,234. That's correct.\n\nThen add the remaining parts:\n\n200,000 →1,206,337,234 +200,000 =1,206,537,234.\n\n60,000 →1,206,537,234 +60,000 =1,206,597,234.\n\n7,000 →1,206,597,234 +7,000 =1,206,604,234.\n\n800 →1,206,604,234 +800 =1,206,605,034.\n\n10 →1,206,605,034 +10 =1,206,605,044.\n\n7 →1,206,605,044 +7 =1,206,605,051.\n\nWait, but according to this, the total is 1,206,605,051, but the digit-by-digit and component-wise addition give 1,224,605,051.\n\nThere's a conflict here. The mistake must be in the breakdown of the second number.\n\nWait, let's look at the second number again:757,267,817.\n\nBreaking into components:\n\n757,267,817.\n\n- 700,000,000 (hundred millions).\n\n- 50,000,000 (ten millions).\n\n-7,000,000 (millions).\n\n-200,000 (hundred thousands).\n\n-60,000 (ten thousands).\n\n-7,000 (thousands).\n\n-800 (hundreds).\n\n-10 (tens).\n\n-7 (ones).\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nSo adding to the first number:\n\nFirst number:449,337,234.\n\nAdding 700M gives 1,149,337,234.\n\nAdding 50M →1,199,337,234.\n\nAdding7M →1,206,337,234.\n\nAdding200k →1,206,537,234.\n\nAdding60k →1,206,597,234.\n\nAdding7k →1,206,604,234.\n\nAdding800 →1,206,605,034.\n\nAdding10 →1,206,605,044.\n\nAdding7 →1,206,605,051.\n\nBut this contradicts the digit-wise addition.\n\nWait, this must mean that in my digit-wise approach I made an error.\n\nWait, let me check the digit-wise addition again:\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is4 (from 449,337,234 → the ten millions place is the second digit from the left in the 449 million part, which is 4).\n\nSecond number's ten millions digit is7 (from 757,267,817 → the second digit in 757 million is 5? Wait, no.\n\nWait, hold on, the second number is 757,267,817.\n\nBreaking down the digits:\n\nThe number is 7 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 7 (millions). Wait, that's incorrect.\n\nWait, wait a minute! This is the key mistake!\n\nThe second number is 757,267,817.\n\nLet me parse its digits properly:\n\n757,267,817.\n\nBreaking down the digits:\n\nStarting from the left:\n\n- The first three digits:7 (hundred millions),5 (ten millions),7 (millions).\n\nWait, 757 million.\n\nYes, so 7 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 7 (millions).\n\nTherefore, the ten millions digit is5, not7.\n\nOh no! Here's the error. I incorrectly assumed the ten millions digit was7, but it's actually5.\n\nThat's the mistake!\n\nSo the second number's ten millions digit is5, not7.\n\nThis changes everything.\n\nLet me correct this:\n\nThe second number is757,267,817.\n\nBreaking down digit by digit from right to left (positions1-9):\n\nPosition1:7 (ones).\n\nPosition2:1 (tens).\n\nPosition3:8 (hundreds).\n\nPosition4:7 (thousands).\n\nPosition5:6 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition6:2 (hundred thousands).\n\nPosition7:5 (millions).\n\nPosition8:5 (ten millions).\n\nPosition9:7 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, let me write the number as:\n\n7 (hundred millions),\n\n5 (ten millions),\n\n7 (millions),\n\n2 (hundred thousands),\n\n6 (ten thousands),\n\n7 (thousands),\n\n8 (hundreds),\n\n1 (tens),\n\n7 (ones).\n\nWait, commas are placed as 757,267,817.\n\nBreaking into digits:\n\n7 (hundred millions),\n\n5 (ten millions),\n\n7 (millions),\n\n2 (hundred thousands),\n\n6 (ten thousands),\n\n7 (thousands),\n\n8 (hundreds),\n\n1 (tens),\n\n7 (ones).\n\nSo the ten millions digit is5, not7.\n\nThat was the mistake earlier. I had incorrectly taken the ten millions digit as7 instead of5.\n\nSo correcting that:\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is4.\n\nSecond number's ten millions digit is5.\n\nTherefore, in the digit-by-digit addition:\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n4 (first) +5 (second) + any carryover.\n\nWait let's redo the digit-by-digit addition with the correct digits.\n\nFirst number (449,337,234):\n\nPositions1-9:\n\n1:4,\n\n2:3,\n\n3:2,\n\n4:7,\n\n5:3,\n\n6:3,\n\n7:9,\n\n8:4,\n\n9:4.\n\nSecond number (757,267,817):\n\nPositions1-9:\n\n1:7,\n\n2:1,\n\n3:8,\n\n4:7,\n\n5:6,\n\n6:2,\n\n7:5,\n\n8:5,\n\n9:7.\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nPosition1:4+7=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nPosition2:3+1 +1=5 →5.\n\nPosition3:2+8=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nPosition4:7+7=14 +1=15 →5, carryover1.\n\nPosition5:3+6=9 +1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nPosition6:3+2=5 +1=6 →6.\n\nPosition7:9+5=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nPosition8:4+5=9 +1 (carryover)=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nPosition9:4+7=11 +1 (carryover)=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nPosition10:1 (carryover).\n\nSo the digits from position10 down to 1 are:\n\nPosition10:1,\n\nPosition9:2,\n\nPosition8:0,\n\nPosition7:4,\n\nPosition6:6,\n\nPosition5:0,\n\nPosition4:5,\n\nPosition3:0,\n\nPosition2:5,\n\nPosition1:1.\n\nThus, the number is 1,206,605,051.\n\nAh! Now this matches the step-by-step addition's result. So the mistake was in the initial digit-by-digit analysis where I incorrectly assumed the second number's ten millions digit was7 instead of5.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is1,206,605,051.\n\nWait but the user's answer was1,206,685,051. Hmm, close but not the same.\n\nWait let me check the correct digits again.\n\nWait let's re-express the final digits after correction:\n\nPosition10:1 (billions).\n\nPosition9:2 (hundred millions).\n\nPosition8:0 (ten millions).\n\nPosition7:4 (millions).\n\nPosition6:6 (hundred thousands).\n\nPosition5:0 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition4:5 (thousands).\n\nPosition3:0 (hundreds).\n\nPosition2:5 (tens).\n\nPosition1:1 (ones).\n\nSo the number is:\n\n1 (billions),\n\n2 (hundred millions),\n\n0 (ten millions),\n\n4 (millions),\n\n6 (hundred thousands),\n\n0 (ten thousands),\n\n5 (thousands),\n\n0 (hundreds),\n\n5 (tens),\n\n1 (ones).\n\nSo written with commas:1,206,605,051.\n\nThe user's answer was1,206,685,051. So the difference is in the hundred thousands place:6 vs8.\n\nLooking back at the position6:\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands place):\n\nFirst number's digit is3.\n\nSecond number's digit is2.\n\nAdding them:3+2=5, plus any