s correctly.\n\nWait, 856,564,531.\n\nLet's parse it digit by digit from left to right:\n\nDigit positions (starting from the right, but let's do left for clarity):\n\nBillions place: 8 (since 856 million is 8 hundred millions, so 8 is the hundred millions place, not billions. Wait, maybe I need to clarify the exact places.\n\nWait, 856,564,531 is:\n\nDigit 1 (leftmost): 8 \u2192 hundred millions place (since 800,000,000).\n\nDigit 2:5 \u2192 ten millions place (50,000,000).\n\nDigit3:6 \u2192 millions place (6,000,000).\n\nDigit4:5 \u2192 hundred thousands (500,000).\n\nDigit5:6 \u2192 ten thousands (60,000).\n\nDigit6:4 \u2192 thousands (4,000).\n\nDigit7:5 \u2192 hundreds (500).\n\nDigit8:3 \u2192 tens (30).\n\nDigit9:1 \u2192 ones (1).\n\nSimilarly, the second number 643,645,481:\n\nDigit1:6 \u2192 hundred millions (600,000,000).\n\nDigit2:4 \u2192 ten millions (40,000,000).\n\nDigit3:3 \u2192 millions (3,000,000).\n\nDigit4:6 \u2192 hundred thousands (600,000).\n\nDigit5:4 \u2192 ten thousands (40,000).\n\nDigit6:5 \u2192 thousands (5,000).\n\nDigit7:4 \u2192 hundreds (400).\n\nDigit8:8 \u2192 tens (80).\n\nDigit9:1 \u2192 ones (1).\n\nSo when adding digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):\n\nDigit9 (ones):1+1=2 \u2714\ufe0f.\n\nDigit8 (tens):3+8=11 \u2192 1, carryover1.\n\nDigit7 (hundreds):5+4=9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nDigit6 (thousands):4+5=9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):6 (from first number) +4 (from second)=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nDigit4 (hundred thousands):5 (first) +6 (second)=11 +1=12 \u21922, carryover1.\n\nDigit3 (millions):6 (first) +3 (second)=9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nDigit2 (ten millions):5 (first) +4 (second)=9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nDigit1 (hundred millions):8 (first) +6 (second)=14 +1=15 \u21925, carryover1.\n\nNow, since there's a carryover of 1 after the hundred millions place, we need to add a new digit to the left, which is the billions place: 1.\n\nSo putting all together