lace:9 +6=15\n\nThousands place:4 +5=9\n\nHundreds place:1 +2=3\n\nTens place:0 +7=7\n\nOnes place:5 +0=5\n\nWait, but when adding, we have to consider the carryover from each column.\n\nWait, perhaps it\'s better to add step by step from the right, considering each digit column:\n\nLet me list each column from right (ones) to left (hundred millions):\n\nColumn 1 (Ones): 5 +0 =5. Write 5.\n\nColumn 2 (Tens):0 +7=7. Write 7.\n\nColumn3 (Hundreds):1 +2=3. Write3.\n\nColumn4 (Thousands):4 +5=9. Write9.\n\nColumn5 (Ten thousands):9 +6=15. Write5, carryover1.\n\nColumn6 (Hundred thousands):0 +1 +1 (carryover)=2. Write2.\n\nColumn7 (Millions):4 +7=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\nWait, here\'s a problem. Wait, the millions place:\n\nWait the millions column is the 4 (first number) and 7 (second number). 4+7=11. So we write 1 and carryover 1. So the millions digit is 1, with a carryover.\n\nThen Column8 (Ten millions):8 +5=13, plus the carryover1 →14. So write4, carryover1.\n\nColumn9 (Hundred millions):3 +9=12, plus carryover1 →13. So write3, carryover1.\n\nSince there are no more digits, the carryover1 becomes a new digit at the billions place:1.\n\nSo putting it all together:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nColumns 1-9:\n\n5 (ones),7 (tens),3 (hundreds),9 (thousands),5 (ten thousands),2 (hundred thousands),1 (millions),4 (ten millions),3 (hundred millions), plus the carried over 1 in billions.\n\nSo the total would be:\n\n1 (billions), 3 (hundred millions),4 (ten millions),1 (millions),2 (hundred thousands),5 (ten thousands),9 (thousands),3 (hundreds),7 (tens),5 (ones).\n\nWait, let me structure it properly:\n\nBillions place:1\n\nHundred millions:3\n\nTen millions:4\n\nMillions:1\n\nHundred thousands:2\n\nTen thousands:5\n\nThousands:9\n\nHundreds:3\n\nTens:7\n\nOnes:5\n\nSo combining these digits:\n\n1,341,259,375.\n\nWait but the user said the correct answer is 1,341,259,875. Hmm, so there\'s a discrepancy here. Where did I go wrong?\n\nWait let\'s check each column again c