its:\n\n875,916,498 has 9 digits:\n\nPosition breakdown:\n\nDigit 1 (units): 8\n\nDigit 2 (tens):9\n\nDigit3 (hundreds):4\n\nDigit4 (thousands):6\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):1\n\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):9\n\nDigit7 (millions):5\n\nDigit8 (ten millions):7\n\nDigit9 (hundred millions):8\n\nSecond number: 134,101,977 is also 9 digits:\n\nDigit1 (units):7\n\nDigit2 (tens):7\n\nDigit3 (hundreds):9\n\nDigit4 (thousands):1\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):0\n\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):1\n\nDigit7 (millions):4\n\nDigit8 (ten millions):3\n\nDigit9 (hundred millions):1\n\nSo adding each digit:\n\nStarting from digit1 (units):\n\n8 +7 =15 → 5, carryover 1\n\nDigit2 (tens):\n\n9 +7 +1 =17 →7, carryover1\n\nDigit3 (hundreds):\n\n4 +9 +1=14 →4, carryover1\n\nDigit4 (thousands):\n\n6 +1 +1=8 →8, no carryover.\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):\n\n1 +0 =1 →1, no carryover.\n\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):\n\n9 +1 =10 →0, carryover1.\n\nDigit7 (millions):\n\n5 +4 +1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nWait, here\'s an error! Previously I thought that in millions place (digit7), the first number has 5 and the second has 4. Adding 5 +4 +1 (carryover from previous) gives 10. So that\'s 0 with a carryover of 1. But in my first calculation I said millions place was 9, but that was a mistake. Wait, in the first calculation I might have messed up the digit positions.\n\nWait, let\'s retrace.\n\nDigit7 is millions place:\n\nFirst number\'s digit7 (millions place) is 5 (since the number is 875,916,498 → millions digit is the 5 in 875 million). Second number\'s digit7 is 4 (from 134,101,977 → 134 million, so millions digit is 4). So 5 +4 =9 plus any carryover?\n\nWait, after digit6 (hundred thousands):\n\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):\n\nFirst number has 9, second has 1, so 9+1=10, so we write 0 and carryover1 to the millions place (digit7). So digit7: 5+4 +1 (carryover) =10, so 0 and carryover1.\n\nAh! Here\'s where I messed up in my first calculation. Originally I thought the millions place after adding 5+3 (but that was wrong because the second number\'s millions digit is 4, not 3). Wait, the second number is 134,101,977. Breaking down:\n\n1 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 4 (millions), so yes, millions digit is 4. So the first calculation was wrong here.\n\nSo in digit7 (millions place):\n\n5 (from first) +4 (second) +1 (carryover) =10. So write 0, carryover1.\n\nThen moving to digit8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst number has7, second has3. 7+3 +1 (carryover) =11. So write 1 and carryover1.\n\nDigit9 (hundred millions):\n\nFirst number 8, second number 1. 8+1 +1 (carryover) =10. So write 0 and carryover1 to the billions place.\n\nSo the billions place is 1.\n\nSo putting all digits together:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nDigit1:5\n\nDigit2:7\n\nDigit3:4\n\nDigit4:8\n\nDigit5:1\n\nDigit6:0\n\nDigit7:0\n\nDigit8:1 (with carryover to next)\n\nDigit9:0 (from 8+1+1=10)\n\nPlus the carryover to billions place 1.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\n5 (units),7 (tens),4 (hundreds),8 (thousands),1 (ten thousands),0 (hundred thousands),0 (millions),1 (ten millions),0 (hundred millions), and 1 (billions). So the number is 1,009,018,475? Wait, let me arrange them properly.\n\nWait, the digits in order from billions down:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:0 (from digit9: which was the 10 in hundred millions, so digit9 is 0 with carryover, so the digit is 0, and the next digit (billions) is 1).\n\nTen millions:1 (digit8 is 1)\n\nMillions:0 (digit7)\n\nHundred thousands:0 (digit6)\n\nTen thousands:1 (digit5)\n\nThousands:8 (digit4)\n\nHundreds:4 (digit3)\n\nTens:7 (digit2)\n\nOnes:5 (digit1)\n\nSo putting that together:\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions?), wait no:\n\nWait, the digit positions:\n\nThe digits from highest to lowest:\n\nBillions (digit10):1\n\nHundred millions (digit9):0\n\nTen millions (digit8):1\n\nMillions (digit7):0\n\nHundred thousands (digit6):0\n\nTen thousands (digit5):1\n\nThousands (digit4):8\n\nHundreds (digit3):4\n\nTens (digit2):7\n\nOn