ndred millions:8+1=9 +1=10 →0, carry1\n\nBillions: carry1 →1\n\nSo the result is 1,010,018,475. So the correct answer is 1,010,018,475. The user\'s answer was 1,010,018,435, which has a 3 instead of 4 in the hundreds place. So either the user made a mistake in their calculation, or perhaps I messed up. Let me check the hundreds place again.\n\nHundreds place digits:\n\nFirst number\'s hundreds digit is 4 (from 875,916,498: the number ends with 498, so hundreds digit is 4).\n\nSecond number\'s hundreds digit is 9 (from 134,101,977: the last three digits are 977, so hundreds digit is 9).\n\nAdding 4 +9 =13, plus the carryover 1 from the tens place (which was 17, so carryover1). Wait, no: the tens place was 9+7+1=17 (the 1 is carryover from ones). So the carryover from tens to hundreds is 1. Wait, in the hundreds place: 4+9 +1 (carryover from tens) =14. So 4 in the hundreds place and carryover1. That\'s correct. So the hundreds digit is 4. So the user\'s answer must have an error there. Therefore, the correct answer is 1,010,018,475. The user\'s thought was close but had an error in the hundreds place.\n\nWait, the user wrote "I think the correct answer is: 1010018435" which is 1,010,018,435. Comparing with my result 1,010,018,475, the difference is in the tens and ones? Wait no, let me see:\n\nWait 1,010,018,435 vs 1,010,018,475. The last four digits: 435 vs 475. So the hundreds digit is 4 vs 4 (same), tens digit is 3 vs7, and ones is5 vs5. Wait, so actually, the tens digit is different. Wait, perhaps I made a mistake in the tens place?\n\nWait let me check the tens place again:\n\nTens digit:\n\nFirst number\'s tens digit is9 (from 875,916,498 → the tens digit is the second from the right, which is 9 in ...498).\n\nSecond number\'s tens digit is7 (from ...977 → tens digit is7).\n\nAdding 9+7 =16, plus any carryover? The ones place had a carryover of1. Wait:\n\nOnes place:8+7=15 → carryover1.\n\nTens place:9+7 +1 (carryover) =17 → so 7 in tens place, carryover1.\n\nSo