ions):7\n\nDigit3 (millions):6\n\nDigit4 (hundred thousands):2\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):4\n\nDigit6 (thousands):2\n\nDigit7 (hundreds):6\n\nDigit8 (tens):4\n\nDigit9 (ones):9.\n\nSecond number:644,562,764.\n\nDigit1 (hundred millions):6\n\nDigit2 (ten millions):4\n\nDigit3 (millions):4\n\nDigit4 (hundred thousands):5\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):6\n\nDigit6 (thousands):2\n\nDigit7 (hundreds):7\n\nDigit8 (tens):6\n\nDigit9 (ones):4.\n\nSo adding each corresponding digit:\n\nHundred millions:2+6=8, but with carryover from previous? Wait, when adding step by step, we start from the right. But when I did the breakdown earlier, I think I might have made a mistake in the place values when adding in parts. Let me try adding the two numbers using another method to cross-verify.\n\nLet me write them vertically:\n\n\u2003276,242,649\n\n+644,562,764\n\n-------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:9+4=13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nTens:4+6=10 +1=11. Write 1, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:6+7=13 +1=14. Write4, carryover1.\n\nThousands:2+2=4 +1=5. Write5, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands:4+6=10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:2+5=7 +1=8. Write8, no carryover.\n\nMillions:6 (from first number) +4 (second number) =10. Wait, wait, wait. Wait, the millions place. Let me check again.\n\nWait the first number's millions digit is the third digit from the left: 2 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 6 (millions). The second number's millions digit is 4 (hundred millions:6, ten millions:4, millions:4). So millions place digits are 6 and 4. So adding 6 +4 =10? Wait, but in the breakdown earlier I had 7+4 for millions? Wait no, perhaps I messed up the place values in the breakdown.\n\nWait let me clarify the place values:\n\nFirst number: 2 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 9 (ones).\n\nSecond number:6 (hundred millions),4 (ten millions),4 (millions),5 (hundred thousands),6 (ten thousands