wn with commas:\n\n697 (million), 954 (thousand), 550.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nDigit 1 (ones):0,\n\nDigit2 (tens):5,\n\nDigit3 (hundreds):5,\n\nDigit4 (thousands):4,\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):5,\n\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):9,\n\nDigit7 (millions):7,\n\nDigit8 (ten millions):9,\n\nDigit9 (hundred millions):6.\n\nSecond number:104,161,451.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n104 (millions), 161 (thousands), 451.\n\nSo digits:\n\nDigit1 (ones):1,\n\nDigit2 (tens):5,\n\nDigit3 (hundreds):4,\n\nDigit4 (thousands):1,\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):6,\n\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):1,\n\nDigit7 (millions):4,\n\nDigit8 (ten millions):0,\n\nDigit9 (hundred millions):1.\n\nSo when adding:\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\nFirst number:7 (millions digit),\n\nSecond number:4 (millions digit). So 7 +4 =11, plus the carry from previous (position6 which was carry1). Wait, let's go step by step again.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\nFirst number's digit7 is7,\n\nSecond's digit7 is4,\n\nplus the carryover from position6 (which was 1 from the hundred thousands place calculation).\n\nWait let's retrace the steps again:\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\nFirst digit:9,\n\nSecond digit:1,\n\nplus any carry? From position5's calculation:\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\n5 +6 =11, so carryover1 to position6.\n\nSo in position6:\n\n9 +1 =10 + carry1 (from position5's carryover) → total 11. So digit6 is1, carryover1.\n\nThen moving to position7 (millions):\n\nFirst digit7:7,\n\nSecond digit7:4,\n\nplus carryover1 from position6 → total 7+4+1=12 → so digit7 is2, carryover1.\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nThen position8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst digit8:9,\n\nSecond digit8:0,\n\nplus carry1 →9+0+1=10 → digit8 is0, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\nFirst digit9:6,\n\nSecond digit9:1,\n\nplus carry1 →6+1+1=8 → digit9 is8, no carryover.\n\nThus the total is 802,116,001. But the user's answer was 852,116,001. Hmm, so there's a mistake either in my calculation or the user's thought.\n\nWait, let me add the numbers 