o methods. Which is correct?\n\nWait, let me use a calculator to verify.\n\nLet me compute 366,520,298 +506,786,558.\n\n366,520,298 +506,786,558.\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\n366,520,298\n\n+506,786,558\n\n=?\n\n366,520,298 + 500,000,000 = 866,520,298.\n\nThen add 6,786,558 → 866,520,298 +6,786,558 = ?\n\n866,520,298 +6,000,000 = 872,520,298.\n\nThen +786,558 → 872,520,298 +786,558.\n\n872,520,298 +700,000 = 873,220,298.\n\nThen +86,558 → 873,220,298 +86,558 = 873,306,856.\n\nAh! So the correct answer is 873,306,856. So the digit-by-digit method must have an error. But where?\n\nWait in the digit-by-digit calculation above, when I did the step-by-step, I got 873,305,956. That must mean I made a mistake there.\n\nLet me re-examine the digit-by-digit again carefully.\n\nLet me list each digit's place for both numbers:\n\nFirst number (366,520,298):\n\n- Position1 (ones):8\n\n- Position2 (tens):9\n\n- Position3 (hundreds):2\n\n- Position4 (thousands):0\n\n- Position5 (ten thousands):2\n\n- Position6 (hundred thousands):5\n\n- Position7 (millions):6\n\n- Position8 (ten millions):6\n\n- Position9 (hundred millions):3\n\nSecond number (506,786,558):\n\n- Position1 (ones):8\n\n- Position2 (tens):5\n\n- Position3 (hundreds):5\n\n- Position4 (thousands):6\n\n- Position5 (ten thousands):8\n\n- Position6 (hundred thousands):7\n\n- Position7 (millions):6\n\n- Position8 (ten millions):0\n\n- Position9 (hundred millions):5\n\nWait a second! Hold on! I think I made a mistake in the hundreds place of the second number.\n\nWait, the second number is 506,786,558. Breaking it down:\n\n- Hundred millions:5.\n\n- Ten millions:0.\n\n- Millions:6.\n\n- Hundred thousands:7.\n\n- Ten thousands:8.\n\n- Thousands:6.\n\n- Hundreds:5.\n\n- Tens:5.\n\n- Ones:8.\n\nSo the hundreds digit (position3) is 5 (since the number is ...558). So in position3 (hundreds place), the second number has a 5, not 6 as I previously thought.\n\nWait! Oh no! That's the mistake. Earlier, I assumed that in the hundreds place of the second number was 6, but it's actually 5.\n\nWait, let me re-express the second number:\n\n506,786,558:\n\nBreaking down each digit from left to right (hundred millions to ones):\n\nPosition9:5 (hundred millions).\n\nPosition8:0 (ten millions).\n\nPosition7:6 (millions).\n\nPosition6:7 (hundred thousands).\n\nPosition5:8 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition4:6 (thousands).\n\nPosition3:5 (hundreds).\n\nPosition2:5 (tens).\n\nPosition1:8 (ones).\n\nAh! So in the hundreds place (position3), the second number has 5, not 6 as I had initially thought. That was the error.\n\nSo in my first digit-by-digit addition, I incorrectly assumed that the second number's hundreds digit was 6, but it's actually 5.\n\nLet me redo the digit-by-digit addition with the correct digits.\n\nStarting over with corrected digits:\n\nNumbers:\n\nFirst number: 3 6 6 5 2 0 2 9 8.\n\nSecond number:5 0 6 7 8 6 5 5 8.\n\nWait, let me list each digit's place correctly:\n\nPosition1 (ones):\n\nFirst:8, second:8 →8+8=16 →6 carry1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):\n\nFirst:9, second:5 →9+5=14 +1=15 →5 carry1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\nFirst:2, second:5 →2+5=7 +1=8 →8, no carry.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\nFirst:0, second:6 →0+6=6.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\nFirst:2, second:8 →2+8=10 →0 carry1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\nFirst:5, second:7 →5+7=12 +1=13 →3 carry1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\nFirst:6, second:6 →6+6=12 +1=13 →3 carry1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst:6, second:0 →6+0=6 +1=7.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\nFirst:3, second:5 →3+5=8.\n\nNow compiling the digits from position1 to position9 (right to left):\n\nPosition1:6.\n\nPosition2:5.\n\nPosition3:8.\n\nPosition4:6.\n\nPosition5:0.\n\nPosition6:3.\n\nPosition7:3.\n\nPosition8:7.\n\nPosition9:8.\n\nThus the number is:\n\nPosition9 (8), position8 (7), position7 (3), position6 (3), position5 (0), position4 (6), position3 (8), position2 (5), position1 (6).\n\nSo that's 8 7 3 3 0 6 8 5 6 → 873,306,856.\n\nAh! There we go