d 11, so we write down 1 and carryover 1. So the hundreds digit is 1, and the carryover affects thousands place. The thousands place becomes 5+2+1=8.\n\nContinuing:\n\nTen thousands: 6+1=7\n\nHundred thousands:0+7=7\n\nMillions:2+0=2\n\nTen millions:0+4=4\n\nHundred millions:7+1=8.\n\nSo putting all digits together from highest to lowest:\n\nHundred millions:8\n\nTen millions:4\n\nMillions:2\n\nHundred thousands:7\n\nTen thousands:7\n\nThousands:8\n\nHundreds:1\n\nTens:9\n\nOnes:6\n\nSo the number is 8 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands),7 (ten thousands),8 (thousands),1 (hundreds),9 (tens),6 (ones).\n\nWhich translates to 842,778,196.\n\nWait, but the user\'s answer was 842,778,190. So there\'s a discrepancy in the last digit. Hmm, where did I go wrong?\n\nWait let me check the hundreds place again. The first number\'s hundreds digit is 6, second is 5. 6+5=11. So we write 1 in hundreds place, carryover 1. So the hundreds digit is 1, not 11. Then thousands place: first number\'s thousands digit is 5, second is 2. 5+2 +1 (carryover)=8. That\'s correct.\n\nTen thousands: first has 6, second has1 → 7.\n\nHundred thousands: first 0, second7 →7.\n\nMillions: first 2, second 0 →2.\n\nTen millions: first 0, second4 →4.\n\nHundred millions:7+1=8.\n\nSo the total is 842,778,196. But the user says the correct answer is 842,778,190. There\'s a difference of 6. So maybe I made a mistake in the ten thousands place?\n\nWait let me check each digit again:\n\nFirst number breakdown:\n\n702,065,632 → digits from right:\n\nPosition 1 (ones):2\n\n2:3 (tens)\n\n3:6 (hundreds)\n\n4:5 (thousands)\n\n5:6 (ten thousands)\n\n6:0 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:2 (millions)\n\n8:0 (ten millions)\n\n9:7 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number 140,712,564:\n\nPosition1:4 (ones)\n\n2:6 (tens)\n\n3:5 (hundreds)\n\n4:2 (thousands)\n\n5:1 (ten thousands)\n\n6:7 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:0 (millions)\n\n8:4 (ten millions)\n\n9:1 (hundred millions)\n\nAdding each place:\n\n