lions).\n\nWait, maybe I should list each digit from right to left with their positions:.\n\nPosition 1 (units): 0.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):6.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):2.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):0.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):8.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0.\n\nPosition7 (millions):3.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):1.\n\nSecond number:773,801,542.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\nPosition1 (units):2.\n\nPosition2 (tens):4.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):5.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):0.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):8.\n\nPosition7 (millions):3.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):7.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):7.\n\nWait, but the second number is 773,801,542. Let me confirm:.\n\n773,801,542:.\n\nDigits from right:.\n\nPosition1: 2 (units).\n\nPosition2:4 (tens).\n\nPosition3:5 (hundreds).\n\nPosition4:1 (thousands).\n\nPosition5:0 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition6:8 (hundred thousands).\n\nPosition7:3 (millions).\n\nPosition8:7 (ten millions).\n\nPosition9:7 (hundred millions).\n\nYes. So now adding each position:.\n\nStarting from Position1:.\n\n0 +2 =2 (no carryover).\n\nPosition2:6 +4=10. So write 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition3:2 +5=7 plus the carryover 1 makes 8. No carryover.\n\nPosition4:0 +1=1. No carryover.\n\nPosition5:8 +0=8.\n\nPosition6:0 +8=8.\n\nPosition7:3 (from first) +3 (second)=6.\n\nPosition8:3 +7=10. So here we get 10, so write 0 and carryover 1.\n\nPosition9:1 +7=8 plus the carryover 1 makes 9.\n\nWait, let me check again:.\n\nWait the positions for the first number's Position9 is 1 (hundred millions), and the second's is 7. Adding those gives 1+7=8, plus the carryover from Position8 which was 1 (from the 3+7=10 in Position8), so 8+1=9. So the hundred millions place is 9.\n\nPutting all together:.\n\nStarting from Position1 (units) to Position9 (hundred millions):.\n\nPositions:.\n\n1:2.\n\n2:0 (with carryover 1 to next).\n\n3:8.\n\n4:1.\n\n5:8.\n\n6:8.\n\n7:6.\n\n8:0 (with carryover 1 to next).\n\n9:9.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:.\n\nPosition1:2, Position2:0, Position3:8, Position4:1, Position5:8, Position6:8, Position7:6, Position8:0, Position9:9.\n\nWait, but arranging them properly, the sum would be:.\n\nStarting from the highest digit (hundred millions place):.\n\n9 (hundred millions).\n\nthen ten millions:0 (from Position8).\n\nmillions:6.\n\nhundred thousands:8.\n\nten thousands:8.\n\nthousands:1.\n\nhundreds:8.\n\ntens:0.\n\nunits:2.\n\nWait, perhaps better to write the number as:.\n\n9 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions?), Wait no. Let me think again.\n\nWait the positions are:.\n\nPosition9: 9 (hundred millions).\n\nPosition8: 0 (ten millions).\n\nPosition7:6 (millions).\n\nPosition6:8 (hundred thousands).\n\nPosition5:8 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition4:1 (thousands).\n\nPosition3:8 (hundreds).\n\nPosition2:0 (tens).\n\nPosition1:2 (units).\n\nSo the number is:.\n\n9 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 2 (units)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
