millions),\n\n5 (ten millions),\n\n1 (millions),\n\n7 (hundred thousands),\n\n5 (ten thousands),\n\n1 (thousands),\n\n4 (hundreds),\n\n9 (tens),\n\n9 (ones). Yes, that\'s correct.\n\nSo when adding the hundreds place (digit3 from the right):\n\nFirst number\'s hundreds digit is 2,\n\nSecond number\'s hundreds digit is 4,\n\nSo 2+4=6 plus carryover from the tens place (which was 1, but wait, let\'s see.\n\nWait, let\'s go step by step again.\n\nStarting over:\n\nOnes place (digit1):\n\n1 +9=10 → 0, carryover1.\n\nTens place (digit2):\n\n4 +9=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nHundreds place (digit3):\n\n2 (first) +4 (second)=6 +1 (carryover)=7 → no carryover.\n\nThousands place (digit4):\n\n3 (first) +1 (second)=4 →4, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands (digit5):\n\n5 +5=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands (digit6):\n\n6 +7=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nMillions (digit7):\n\n5 (first) +1 (second)=6 +1=7 →7, no carryover.\n\nTen millions (digit8):\n\n3 (first) +5 (second)=8 →8.\n\nHundred millions (digit9):\n\n3 +1=4.\n\nSo the digits are indeed 4 8 7 4 0 4 7 4 0. Wait, no. Wait digit7 is millions, which is 7. Let me list all digits from left to right (highest to lowest):\n\nHundred millions:4,\n\nTen millions:8,\n\nMillions:7,\n\nHundred thousands:4,\n\nTen thousands:0,\n\nThousands:4,\n\nHundreds:7,\n\nTens:4,\n\nOnes:0.\n\nSo the number is 487,404,740. But the user\'s answer was 487,404,700. So there\'s a difference in the hundreds place: 7 vs 0. Hmm, so where did I go wrong?\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in digit7 (millions place). Let\'s check that.\n\nFirst number\'s millions digit: the millions place in 335,653,241. Let\'s see:\n\n335,653,241:\n\nThe digits are:\n\n3 (hundred millions),\n\n3 (ten millions),\n\n5 (millions),\n\n6 (hundred thousands),\n\n5 (ten thousands),\n\n3 (thousands),\n\n2 (hundreds),\n\n4 (tens),\n\n1 (ones).\n\nSo millions digit is 5.\n\nSecond number\'s millions digit is the millions place in 151,751,499: 1 (since it\'s 151, so the first three