om position2 (which was 1). Wait, position2 was the hundreds place (digit2). Let me check:.\n\nWait position2 (hundreds place):.\n\nFirst number's digit2 is 4 (from 117,921,429: the hundreds digit is 4).\n\nSecond number's digit2 is7 (from 586,403,747: the hundreds digit is7).\n\nSo 4+7=11, so we write 1 and carryover1 to position3 (thousands place).\n\nTherefore, in position3 (thousands):.\n\n1 (from first number) + 3 (from second) + 1 (carryover) = 5. So that's correct.\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):2+0=2, no carryover.\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):9 (first) +4 (second) =13 →3, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (millions):7 +6 =13 plus carryover1 →14 →4, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):1 +8 =9 plus carryover1 →10 →0, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):1 +5 =6 plus carryover1 →7.\n\nSo the digits from position8 down to 0 are:.\n\n7 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions),4 (millions),3 (hundred thousands),2 (ten thousands),5 (thousands),1 (hundreds),7 (tens),6 (units).\n\nWait, so putting them together:.\n\nHundred millions:7, ten millions:0, millions:4 → so that's 704, then next is hundred thousands:3 → 704,3, then ten thousands:2 → 704,32, thousands:5 → 704,325, then hundreds:1 →704,3251? Wait no, need to think in terms of places.\n\nActually, the number is:.\n\nDigits:.\n\nPosition8:7.\n\nPosition7:0.\n\nPosition6:4.\n\nPosition5:3.\n\nPosition4:2.\n\nPosition3:5.\n\nPosition2:1.\n\nPosition1:7.\n\nPosition0:6.\n\nSo combining them as a number: 7 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions),4 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands),5 (thousands),1 (hundreds),7 (tens),6 (ones).\n\nSo writing that out:</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
