final_output:  ['1,671,598,383? Wait, but the user says they think the correct answer is 1,621,598,383. Hmm, so there\'s a discrepancy here. I must have made a mistake in my calculation.\n\nLet me check again step by step. Let me try adding the numbers again more carefully:\n\nFirst number: 786,240,044.\n\nSecond number:885,358,339.\n\nAdding them:\n\nStart from the right:\n\nOnes place: 4 +9 =13. Write 3, carryover1.\n\nTens place:4 +3=7 +1=8. Write8, carryover0.\n\nHundreds place:0 +3=3. Write3.\n\nThousands place:0 +8=8. Write8.\n\nTen thousands place:4 +5=9. Write9.\n\nHundred thousands place:2 +3=5. Write5.\n\nMillions place:6 +5=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\nTen millions place:8 +8=16 +1=17. Write7, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions place:7 +8=15 +1=16. Write6, carryover1.\n\nNow the carryover1 becomes the next digit (billions place):1.\n\nPutting all the digits together from right to left:\n\nOnes:3, tens:8, hundreds:3, thousands:8, ten thousands:9, hundred thousands:5, millions:1, ten millions:7, hundred millions:6, billions:1.\n\nSo the number is 1,671,598,383.\n\nBut the user\'s answer is 1,621,598,383. There\'s a difference in the ten millions place. Wait, let\'s see:\n\nWait when adding the ten millions place (the 8th digit from the right), which is the ten millions place. The first number\'s ten millions digit is 8 (since 786,240,044 is 7 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 6 (millions)...). The second number\'s ten millions digit is 8 (from 885,358,339 which is 8 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 5 (millions)...). So adding those two digits:8 +8=16, plus the carryover from the millions place (which was 1 from 6+5=11). So 16 +1=17. So that gives 7 in the ten millions place and carryover1. So the ten millions digit is7, so the hundred millions place would be 7+8 +1? Wait, the hundred millions place: first number\'s digit is7, second\'s is8. So 7+8=15 plus the carryover1 from the ten millions addition (which was the 1 from the ten millions\' total 17? Wa