), 3 (ten millions), 1 (millions). So 6 (millions) +1 (millions) = 7? Wait, but there's a carryover from the hundred thousands place which was 1. So 6 (millions) +1 (millions) +1 (carryover) = 8? So that's correct, so millions place is 8, carryover 0.\n\nThen Ten millions place: first number has 2, second has 3. 2+3 =5, plus any carryover? No, so 5.\n\nHundred millions place: first has 2, second has 6. 2+6=8.\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the right, the digits are:.\n\nOnes:6.\n\nTens:1.\n\nHundreds:6.\n\nThousands:3.\n\nTen thousands:8.\n\nHundred thousands:5.\n\nMillions:8.\n\nTen millions:5.\n\nHundred millions:8.\n\nWait, so the total sum would be 8 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands?), wait, maybe I need to list the digits in order.\n\nWait, let me structure it properly.\n\nStarting from the highest place, which is hundred millions. The sum's digits from left to right (hundred millions down to ones):.\n\nHundred millions: 2 +6 =8.\n\nTen millions: 2 +3 =5.\n\nMillions:6 +1 =7 + carryover? Wait, no, the millions place addition was 6 +1 (from the numbers) plus the carryover from hundred thousands.\n\nWait, let me retrace step by step:.\n\nLet me go through each digit step by step again carefully.\n\nStarting from the right (position 0 is ones):.\n\nPosition 0 (ones): 2 +4 =6. Write down 6. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition 1 (tens):4 +7 =11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 2 (hundreds):2 +3 =5 +1 (carryover) =6. Write 6. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition 3 (thousands):4 +9 =13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 4 (ten thousands):1 +6 =7 +1 (carryover) =8. Write 8. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition 5 (hundred thousands):6 +9 =15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 6 (millions):6 +1 =7 +1 (carryover) =8. Write 8. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition 7 (ten millions):2 +3 =5. Write 5. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition 8 (hundred millions):2 +6 =8. Write 8. Carryover 0.\n\nSo putting all the written digits together from highest to lowest:.\n\nHundred millions 