econd number). So 7,000.\n\nHundreds: 800 +600=1,400.\n\nTens:80+20=100.\n\nUnits:8+0=8.\n\nAdding all those:\n\n1,323,000,000 (from hundred millions, ten millions, millions) plus 800,000 (hundred thousands) → 1,323,800,000.\n\nAdding 11,000 (ten thousands) → 1,323,811,000.\n\nAdding 7,000 (thousands) → 1,323,818,000.\n\nAdding 1,400 (hundreds) → 1,323,819,400.\n\nAdding 100 (tens) → 1,323,819,500.\n\nAdding 8 (units) →1,323,819,508.\n\nHmm, but according to digit-by-digit addition earlier, the total was 1,323,828,508. So there\'s a discrepancy here of 9,000.\n\nAh! I see the mistake now. In the breakdown approach, I might have missed a component. Let me check again:\n\nWait, the first number has 0 in the thousands place, but the second number\'s thousands place is 7 (from 481,117,620: the thousands digit is the fourth from the right, which is 7). So thousands component is 7,000. Correct.\n\nWait, but the first number also has digits in the ten thousands place. Wait, the first number\'s ten thousands digit is 1 (from 842,710,888 → the fifth digit from the right is 1, so ten thousands place is 10,000). Second number\'s ten thousands digit is 1 (from 481,117,620 → fifth digit is 1). So 10,000 +1,000? Wait no, ten thousands place is 10,000 each. Wait, the first\'s ten thousands digit is 1, so 1 *10,000 =10,000. The second\'s ten thousands digit is 1, so 1*10,000=10,000. Wait, that would make 20,000. Wait, I think I made a mistake here.\n\nAh! Here\'s the error. In the breakdown approach, I said the second number\'s ten thousands digit is 1 (from 481,117,620\'s fifth digit from the right is 1). But actually, the second number\'s ten thousands digit is 1 (yes), so the first number\'s ten thousands is 10,000, second is also 10,000 → total 20,000. But in my breakdown earlier, I wrote 10,000 +1,000 =11,000. That was incorrect. The second number\'s ten thousands digit is 1 (so 10,000), not 1,000. So that\'s where the mistake is.\n\nLet me correct that:\n\nTen thousands place