→9+9=18, plus carryover1 (from position5's carryover) →19, so we wrote 9 and carried over 1 to position7.\n\nAh! So for position7 (millions):\n\n9 (from first) +4 (second) +1 (carryover) =14 → so that's 4 in millions place, carryover1.\n\nThen position8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst number:1 (ten millions place?), second number's ten millions is 0.\n\nSo 1 +0 + carryover1 (from millions place) → 2, no carryover.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n3 +4 =7, no carryover.\n\nTherefore, putting it all together:\n\nHundred millions:7\n\nTen millions:2\n\nMillions:4\n\nHundred thousands:9\n\nTen thousands:1\n\nThousands:9\n\nHundreds:2\n\nTens:8\n\nOnes:4.\n\nSo the number is 724,919,284.\n\nBut the user thought the answer was 704,919,284. Hmm, where's the mistake?\n\nWait, perhaps I made an error in the millions place calculation. Let's check again:\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\nFirst number's millions digit is 9 (from 319,925,668: the millions digit is the third digit from left, which is 9 in 319, so yes).\n\nSecond number's millions digit is 4 (from 404,993,616: the third digit is 4, so yes).\n\nSo 9 +4 =13, plus the carryover 1 from position6 (which was 19, so carried 1) → total 14. So millions digit is 4, carryover1.\n\nThen ten millions place:\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is 1, second is 0. So 1+0 +1 (carryover) =2. So ten millions is 2.\n\nWait, so the hundred millions is 3 +4=7, ten millions is 2, millions is 4.\n\nSo the first three digits are 7 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 4 (millions). So the number starts with 724..., which would be 724 million, so 724,919,284. \n\nBut the user's answer is 704,919,284. So why the discrepancy?\n\nAh! Wait, maybe I miscounted the positions. Let me check the breakdown again.\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the positions. Let me write both numbers vertically aligned:\n\nFirst number: 3 1 9 9 2 5 6 6 8\n\nPositions (from left to right):\n\nHundred millions:3\n\nTen millions:1\n\nMillions:9\n\nHundred thousands:9\n\nTen thousands:2\n\nThousands:5\n\nHundreds:6\n\nTens:6\n\nOnes:8\n\nSecond number:4 0 4 9 9 3 6 1 6\n\nPositions:\n\nHundred millions:4\n\nTen millions:0\n\nMillions:4\n\nHundred thousands:9\n\nTen thousands:9\n\nThousands:3\n\nHundreds:6\n\nTens:1\n\nOnes:6\n\nNow adding column by column from right (ones) to left (hundred millions):\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones):\n\nOnes:8 +6=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nTens:6+1=7 +1=8 →8, no carryover.\n\nHundreds:6+6=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nThousands:5+3=8 +1=9 →9, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands:2+9=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:9+9=18 +1=19 →9, carryover1.\n\nMillions:9 (first) +4 (second) =13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nTen millions:1 (first) +0 (second) +1=2 →2, no carryover.\n\nHundred millions:3 +4=7 →7.\n\nSo the result is 7 2 4 9 1 9 2 8 4 → 724,919,284.\n\nBut the user thought the answer was 704,919,284. So where did they get the 0 in the ten millions place?\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the millions place calculation?\n\nWait, millions place is the fourth digit from the right? Or is it the fourth digit from the left?\n\nWait, maybe the confusion comes from how we count the places.\n\nLet me think again:\n\nThe first number is 319,925,668. Breaking down:\n\n- 3 (hundred millions)\n\n- 1 (ten millions)\n\n- 9 (millions)\n\n- 9 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 2 (ten thousands)\n\n- 5 (thousands)\n\n- 6 (hundreds)\n\n- 6 (tens)\n\n- 8 (ones)\n\nSecond number:404,993,616.\n\n-4 (hundred millions)\n\n-0 (ten millions)\n\n-4 (millions)\n\n-9 (hundred thousands)\n\n-9 (ten thousands)\n\n-3 (thousands)\n\n-6 (hundreds)\n\n-1 (tens)\n\n-6 (ones)\n\nAdding the millions place (third digit from the left):\n\nFirst number's millions digit is 9, second's is4. So 9+4=13, plus carryover1 from hundred thousands (which was 19 → carryover1) → total 14. So millions digit is4, carryover1 to ten millions.\n\nTen millions place: first's ten millions is1, second's is0. 1+0 +1=2. So ten millions is2.\n\nHence the ten millions digit is 2, not