,738.\n\nBut the user's answer was 1,516,370,738. Hmm, so there's a mistake here. Let me check again.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up position7 (millions place). Let me check:\n\nPosition7 (millions place):\n\nFirst number's millions digit is 4 (since 924,581,297 \u2192 millions is the 4th digit from the left? Wait, let me confirm the breakdown again.\n\nWait, perhaps my digit positions were miscounted. Let me re-express each number digit by digit properly.\n\nFirst number: 924,581,297.\n\nBreaking down each digit from left (billions) to right (ones):\n\nDigit1 (hundred millions):9\n\nDigit2 (ten millions):2\n\nDigit3 (millions):4\n\nDigit4 (hundred thousands):5\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):8\n\nDigit6 (thousands):1\n\nWait, wait, no, that's not right. Wait, commas are placed every three digits. So 924,581,297 is:\n\nBillions: 9 (since 900,000,000)\n\nHundred millions:9? Wait, no. Let's parse correctly:\n\nThe number is 924,581,297. The commas are after every three digits starting from the right. So:\n\nFirst comma separates millions: 924,581,297 \u2192 924 (million), 581 (thousand), 297.\n\nSo breaking down:\n\nDigit1 (hundred millions place):9 (since 900,000,000)\n\nDigit2 (ten millions):2 (20,000,000)\n\nDigit3 (millions):4 (4,000,000)\n\nDigit4 (hundred thousands):5 (500,000)\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):8 (80,000)\n\nDigit6 (thousands):1 (1,000)\n\nDigit7 (hundreds):2 (200)\n\nDigit8 (tens):9 (90)\n\nDigit9 (ones):7.\n\nSimilarly, the second number:671,789,441.\n\nBreaking down:\n\nDigit1 (hundred millions):6\n\nDigit2 (ten millions):7\n\nDigit3 (millions):1\n\nDigit4 (hundred thousands):7\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):8\n\nDigit6 (thousands):9\n\nDigit7 (hundreds):4\n\nDigit8 (tens):4\n\nDigit9 (ones):1.\n\nSo now, when adding each column from right to left (starting with ones):\n\nPosition1 (ones):7+1=8.\n\nPosition2 (tens):9+4=13 \u21923, carry1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):2+4=6 +1=7.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):1+9=10 \u21920, carry1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):8+8=16 +1=17 \