tion8):0+5=5.\n\nHundred millions (position9):9+9=18 \u21928 with carryover1.\n\nBillions place:1.\n\nSo the result is 1,856,690,748. But the user's answer is 1,856,690,248. The difference is in the hundreds place (7 vs 2) and the thousands place (0 vs 0?), but looking at the user's answer:\n\nWait, user's answer: 1,856,690,248. Let's parse this number:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n1,856,690,248 \u2192 commas split as 1,856,690,248. Wait, commas are placed every three digits from the right.\n\nSo it's 1,856,690,248 \u2192 which would be 1 (billions), 856 (millions), 690 (thousands), 248 (units). Wait, but that would be 1,856,690,248 \u2192 1,856,690,248.\n\nWait, the thousands digit would be the '0' in 690, so thousands place is0, which matches. The hundreds digit in the units part is '2', which is the problem. In my calculation, the hundreds digit is7, but in the user's answer it's2. So where is this discrepancy coming from?\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the hundreds place. Let me check again.\n\nHundreds place (Digit3):\n\nFirst number: hundreds digit is8 (from 842 \u2192 the hundreds is8).\n\nSecond number: hundreds digit is9 (from 906 \u2192 hundreds is9).\n\n8 +9 =17 \u2192 7 with carryover1. So hundreds digit should be7. So in the final number, the hundreds place is7. But in the user's answer, it's2. That suggests an error in the user's answer, unless I miscalculated.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I miscounted the digits. Let me write both numbers again:\n\nFirst number: 906,088,842 \u2192 let's write all digits:\n\nDigits (from right to left):\n\nPosition1:2 (ones)\n\nPosition2:4 (tens)\n\nPosition3:8 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:8 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:8 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:0 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:6 (millions)\n\nPosition8:0 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:9 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number:950,601,906:\n\nPosition1:6 (ones)\n\nPosition2:0 (tens)\n\nPosition3:9 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:1 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:0 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:6 (hun