ber: 420,625,701.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\nDigit positions (from right):.\n\n1 (ones): 1.\n\n2 (tens): 0.\n\n3 (hundreds):7.\n\n4 (thousands):5.\n\n5 (ten thousands):2.\n\n6 (hundred thousands):6.\n\n7 (millions):0.\n\n8 (ten millions):2.\n\n9 (hundred millions):4.\n\nSecond number: 234,951,434.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\nDigit positions (from right):.\n\n1 (ones):4.\n\n2 (tens):3.\n\n3 (hundreds):1.\n\n4 (thousands):4.\n\n5 (ten thousands):5.\n\n6 (hundred thousands):9.\n\n7 (millions):3.\n\n8 (ten millions):4.\n\n9 (hundred millions):2.\n\nWait, so when adding, let's align each digit properly:.\n\nLet me write both numbers with their digits in each place:.\n\nFirst number (420,625,701):.\n\n- Hundred millions: 4.\n\n- Ten millions: 2.\n\n- Millions: 0.\n\n- Hundred thousands:6.\n\n- Ten thousands:2.\n\n- Thousands:5.\n\n- Hundreds:7.\n\n- Tens:0.\n\n- Ones:1.\n\nSecond number (234,951,434):.\n\n- Hundred millions:2.\n\n- Ten millions:3.\n\n- Millions:4.\n\n- Hundred thousands:9.\n\n- Ten thousands:5.\n\n- Thousands:1.\n\n- Hundreds:4.\n\n- Tens:3.\n\n- Ones:4.\n\nWait, I think I made a mistake in the breakdown earlier. Let me check the second number again:.\n\n234,951,434:.\n\nBreaking down digit by digit from left to right:.\n\nDigits: 2 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions),4 (millions),9 (hundred thousands),5 (ten thousands),1 (thousands),4 (hundreds),3 (tens),4 (ones).\n\nWait, that's conflicting with my previous breakdown. Let me count properly:.\n\nThe number 234,951,434:.\n\n- The first comma separates the millions: 234, so the first three digits are 2 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 4 (millions). .\n\nThen the next three digits are 951: 9 (hundred thousands),5 (ten thousands),1 (thousands).\n\nThen the last three digits: 434: 4 (hundreds),3 (tens),4 (ones).\n\nSo yes, the breakdown is correct.\n\nTherefore, when adding:.\n\nLet me write each digit place by place:.\n\nStarting from the ones:.\n\nOnes place:.\n\nFirst number:1, Second:4 → 1+4=5. No carryover.\n