illions digit is 8 (since 768, so the third digit is 8). So 5 +8 =13, plus the carryover from the hundred thousands place (which was 1 from the 18 sum). Wait, wait, the hundred thousands place (digit6) was 9+9=18, so carryover1 to the millions place. So 5+8=13 plus 1 is 14. So millions digit is 4 with carryover1. That's correct.\n\nThen the ten millions place (digit8):\n\nFirst number: ten millions digit is 5 (second digit from left: 8-5-...).\n\nSecond number's ten millions digit is 6 (second digit in 7-6-...). So 5 +6 =11 plus the carryover1 from millions place (14's carryover1) \u2192 12. So ten millions digit is 2, carryover1. Correct.\n\nHundred millions place (digit9):\n\nFirst:8, second:7 \u21928+7=15 plus carryover1 \u219216. So 6 with carryover1. Correct.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:6\n\nTen millions:2\n\nMillions:4\n\nHundred thousands:8\n\nTen thousands:4\n\nThousands:0\n\nHundreds:3\n\nTens:1\n\nUnits:8\n\nSo the number is 1,624,840,318. But the user says their answer is 1,624,810,318. The difference is in the hundred thousands and ten thousands places.\n\nWait in the user's answer, the digits after millions are 810, but according to my calculation, it should be 840. So maybe they added incorrectly somewhere.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made a mistake in digit6 (hundred thousands place):\n\nWait first number's hundred thousands digit is 9 (from 855,939,091 \u2192 the 9 in the hundred thousands place).\n\nSecond number's hundred thousands digit is 9 (from 768,901,227 \u2192 the 9 in the hundred thousands place). 9+9=18. So that's 8 with carryover1. Correct.\n\nSo that part is right. Then the next digit (millions) is 5+8 +1 =14. So millions digit is 4, carryover1. Correct.\n\nHmm, so perhaps the user's answer is wrong, and the correct answer is 1,624,840,318?\n\nWait let me do the addition using another method to verify.\n\nLet me add the two numbers:\n\n855,939,091\n\n+768,901,227\n\n= ?\n\nLet's add them step by step:\n\nFirst