final_output:  ["1,624,840,318? Wait, wait, let me see.\n\nWait, let me write the digits in order from highest to lowest (left to right):\n\nThe digits after each step:\n\nStarting from the leftmost digit (after carryover):\n\nThe carryover1 is the first digit (billions place), then the Digit9 result (6), then Digit8 result (2), Digit7 (4), Digit6 (8), Digit5 (4), Digit4 (0), Digit3 (3), Digit2 (1), Digit1 (8).\n\nWait, let me list all digits step by step:\n\nAfter processing all digits:\n\n- The final carryover is 1 (billions place).\n\n- Then the hundred millions place was 16, so wrote 6, carryover1.\n\nWait, actually, the digits from highest to lowest (left to right):\n\nThe digits we wrote from each place (starting from digit9 to digit1):\n\nWait, perhaps I need to reverse the order. Let me think:\n\nEach digit we computed from right to left (digits 1 to 9), but the final number is written left to right (starting from the highest place to the lowest). So the digits we have are:\n\n- The carryover1 is the highest digit (billions place).\n\n- Then the result from digit9 (hundred millions place) was 6.\n\n- Then digit8 (ten millions place) was 2.\n\n- digit7 (millions) was4.\n\ndigit6 (hundred thousands):8.\n\ndigit5 (ten thousands):4.\n\ndigit4 (thousands):0.\n\ndigit3 (hundreds):3.\n\ndigit2 (tens):1.\n\ndigit1 (units):8.\n\nSo putting them all together from left to right:.\n\n1 (billions), 6 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 8 (units).\n\nSo the number is 1,624,840,318.\n\nWait, but the user's answer was 1,624,810,318. Hmm, there's a discrepancy here. Let me check where I might have gone wrong.\n\nWait let's recalculate step by step again carefully.\n\nLet me write both numbers vertically aligned:\n\nFirst number: 855,939,091\n\nSecond number:768,901,227\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet me write them aligned properly:\n\n         855,939,091\n\n       + 768,901,227\n\n      