tens):6,\n\nDigit3 (hundreds):4,\n\nDigit4 (thousands):5,\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):9,\n\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):4,\n\nDigit7 (millions):3,\n\nDigit8 (ten millions):9,\n\nDigit9 (hundred millions):5,\n\nDigit10 (billions): none, since it's 9 digits total.\n\nWait, actually, 593,495,461 has 9 digits, so the highest place is hundred millions (digit9).\n\nSimilarly, the second number:411,150,634 is also 9 digits.\n\nSo when adding, the maximum digit position is 9, and the carryover can create a 10th digit.\n\nSo in the ten millions place (position8):\n\nFirst number:9,\n\nSecond number:1,\n\nTotal:10 →0 with carryover1.\n\nHundred millions (position9):\n\nFirst:5,\n\nSecond:4,\n\nPlus carryover: 1,\n\nTotal:10 →0 and carryover1.\n\nThus the billions place (position10) is 1.\n\nSo the final sum is 1,004,646,095.\n\nBut the user's initial thought was 5,004,646,095, which is five times higher. That suggests maybe they added an extra digit somewhere.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made a mistake in the millions place.\n\nLet me check position7 (millions):\n\nFirst number's millions digit is3 (since 593 million... so the millions digit is the third digit from the left: 5 (hundred millions),9 (ten millions),3 (millions).\n\nSecond number's millions digit is1 (from 411, so 4 (hundred millions),1 (ten millions),1 (millions). Wait, the second number is 411,150,634. Breaking down:\n\n411 (million), so the millions digit is the third digit: 4 (hundred millions),1 (ten millions),1 (millions).\n\nSo position7 (millions place) digits are 3 and1 →3+1=4. That's correct.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\nFirst number:4,\n\nSecond number:1,\n\nTotal:5? Wait, wait, first number's hundred thousands digit: let's see.\n\nFirst number: 593,495,461.\n\nBreaking down the hundred thousands place: the fourth digit from the right (since the thousands are at position4, ten thousands position5, hundred thousands position6).\n\nWait, maybe confusion in the positions. Let me try another approach,