d thousands),5 (ten thousands),5 (thousands),9 (hundreds),6 (tens),7 (ones). That\'s 9 digits, correct.\n\nWait, when adding hundred millions place:\n\nFirst number\'s hundred millions digit is 4, second is 8. 4+8=12, so that\'s correct. So the hundred millions place would be 2 with a carryover 1 to the billions.\n\nBut when adding ten millions place: first number\'s ten millions is 1, second\'s ten millions is 8. 1+8=9. So that\'s 9 in the ten millions place. Then the millions place: first\'s millions is7, second\'s millions is0. 7+0=7. So that\'s correct.\n\nWait, but where does the 95 come from in the user\'s answer?\n\nWait let\'s recalculate the total step by step, perhaps I messed up a place.\n\nLet me write the addition step by step digit by digit:\n\nLet me write both numbers with their digits:\n\nNumber1: 4 1 7 3 4 4 2 4 0\n\nNumber2:8 8 0 0 5 5 9 6 7\n\nWait, actually, perhaps I misaligned the digits. Let me check:\n\nWait, maybe the first number is 417,344,240. So breaking down:\n\nBillions? No, it\'s 417 million, so up to hundred millions. Let me write each digit position properly:\n\nLet me write both numbers in terms of each digit from the right (starting at position 1):\n\nNumber1: positions 1-9:\n\n1:0 (ones)\n\n2:4 (tens)\n\n3:2 (hundreds)\n\n4:4 (thousands)\n\n5:4 (ten thousands)\n\n6:3 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:7 (millions)\n\n8:1 (ten millions)\n\n9:4 (hundred millions)\n\nNumber2: positions 1-9:\n\n1:7 (ones)\n\n2:6 (tens)\n\n3:9 (hundreds)\n\n4:5 (thousands)\n\n5:5 (ten thousands)\n\n6:0 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:0 (millions)\n\n8:8 (ten millions)\n\n9:8 (hundred millions)\n\nAdding each column:\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 0+7=7\n\nPosition 2 (tens):4+6=10 → 0, carry 1\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):2+9=11 +1=12 → 2, carry1\n\nPosition4 (thousands):4+5=9 +1=10 →0, carry1\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):4+5=9 +1=10 →0, carry1\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):3+0=3 +1=4 →4, no carry\n\nPosition7 (millions):7+0=7\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):1+8=9\n\nPosition9 (hund