 to avoid confusion. Let's list each digit from right to left (starting at ones place):.\n\nFirst number: 894,402,416.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\nPosition (from right): 1 (ones): 6.\n\n2 (tens): 1.\n\n3 (hundreds): 4.\n\n4 (thousands): 2.\n\n5 (ten thousands): 0.\n\n6 (hundred thousands):4.\n\n7 (millions):4.\n\n8 (ten millions):9.\n\n9 (hundred millions):8.\n\nSecond number: 349,230,377.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\nPosition (from right): 1 (ones):7.\n\n2 (tens):7.\n\n3 (hundreds):3.\n\n4 (thousands):0.\n\n5 (ten thousands):3.\n\n6 (hundred thousands):2.\n\n7 (millions):9.\n\n8 (ten millions):4.\n\n9 (hundred millions):3.\n\nWait, let me confirm that again. Let's take 349,230,377:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nDigit 1: 7 (ones).\n\nDigit 2: 7 (tens).\n\nDigit 3: 3 (hundreds).\n\nDigit 4: 0 (thousands).\n\nDigit 5: 3 (ten thousands).\n\nDigit 6: 2 (hundred thousands).\n\nDigit 7: 9 (millions).\n\nDigit 8: 4 (ten millions).\n\nDigit 9: 3 (hundred millions). Yes, that's correct.\n\nNow, adding each column starting from the right:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):.\n\n6 (from first number) +7 (second) =13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):.\n\n1 (first) +7 (second) +1 (carry) =9. No carryover.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):.\n\n4 (first) +3 (second)=7. So 7, no carryover.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):.\n\n2 (first) +0 (second) =2. Write 2, no carryover.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):.\n\n0 (first) +3 (second)=3. Write 3, no carryover.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):.\n\n4 (first) +2 (second)=6. Write 6.\n\nPosition 7 (millions):.\n\n4 (first) +9 (second)=13. So write 3, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):.\n\n9 (first) +4 (second) +1 (carryover)=14. So write 4, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):.\n\n8 (first) +3 (second) +1 (carryover)=12. So write 2, carryover 1. But since that's the highest place, we need to add the 1 as a new digit to the left. So the total becomes 1 2 followed by the rest?.\n\nWait, let me go step by step again carefully.\n\nWait, let's list all the positions with their sums:.\n\n1. Ones: 6 +7 =13 → 3, carryover 1.\n\n2. Tens:1 +7 +1=9 →9, carryover 0.\n\n3. Hundreds:4+3=7 →7, carryover 0.\n\n4. Thousands:2+0=2 →2, carryover 0.\n\n5. Ten thousands:0+3=3 →3, carryover 0.\n\n6. Hundred thousands:4+2=6 →6, carryover 0.\n\n7. Millions:4+9=13 →3, carryover 1.\n\n8. Ten millions:9+4 +1 (carry)=14 →4, carryover 1.\n\n9. Hundred millions:8+3 +1 (carry)=12 →2, carryover 1.\n\nNow, since there are no more digits, the final carryover 1 becomes the next digit (billions place). So the total digits from right to left are:.\n\nPositions 1-9 plus the carryover:.\n\nBillions place (position 10):1.\n\nHundred millions:2.\n\nTen millions:4.\n\nMillions:3.\n\nHundred thousands:6.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nThousands:2.\n\nHundreds:7.\n\nTens:9.\n\nOnes:3.\n\nWait, but arranging them properly, the digits from the highest to lowest (left to right):.\n\nStarting with the billions place (1), then hundred millions (2), ten millions (4), millions (3), hundred thousands (6), ten thousands (3), thousands (2), hundreds (7), tens (9), ones (3)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
