nds), 3 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 2 (ones).\n\nWait, let's see:.\n\nWait, after adding, the digits are:.\n\nStarting from the ones (position0):.\n\n2 (ones).\n\n9 (tens).\n\n2 (hundreds).\n\n3 (thousands).\n\n4 (ten thousands).\n\n8 (hundred thousands).\n\n5 (millions).\n\n4 (ten millions).\n\n4 (hundred millions).\n\nplus the carryover 1 (billions).\n\nSo the final number is 1 (billions), 4 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 2 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
