(ones).\n\nThus the total is 847,482,940? Wait but the user's answer was 847,482,044. Hmm, discrepancy here. Let me check again.\n\nWait perhaps I messed up the positions in the millions and beyond.\n\nWait, let me write the numbers vertically aligned properly.\n\nFirst number: 4 5 8 5 7 1 1 7 1\n\nSecond number:3 8 8 9 1 0 8 6 9\n\nWait, actually, maybe the positions beyond thousands are getting mixed up. Let me write each number's digits with their place values clearly:\n\nFirst number (458,571,171):\n\n- Hundred millions:4\n\n- Ten millions:5\n\n- Millions:8\n\n- Hundred thousands:5\n\n- Ten thousands:7\n\n- Thousands:1\n\n- Hundreds:1\n\n- Tens:7\n\n- Ones:1\n\nSecond number (388,910,869):\n\n- Hundred millions:3\n\n- Ten millions:8\n\n- Millions:8\n\n- Hundred thousands:9\n\n- Ten thousands:1\n\n- Thousands:0\n\n- Hundreds:8\n\n- Tens:6\n\n- Ones:9\n\nNow adding each column from right (ones) to left (hundred millions):\n\nOnes:1+9=10 →0, carryover1\n\nTens:7+6=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1\n\nHundreds:1+8=9 +1 (carryover?) Wait wait, the hundreds place for first number is 1, second is 8?\n\nWait no, in the hundreds place, first number's hundreds digit is 1 (since the number is ...1 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 1 (ones). The second number's hundreds digit is 8 (from ...8 (hundreds), 6 (tens),9 (ones). Wait, so hundreds place: first number has 1, second has 8. So 1 +8 =9, plus any carryover?\n\nWait, the hundreds place comes after the tens place which had a carryover of 1. Wait, let's track again step by step:\n\nStarting over:\n\nOnes:\n\n1 +9 =10 → 0, carryover1.\n\nTens:\n\n7 (first) +6 (second)=13 +1 (carryover) =14 →4, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:\n\n1 (first's hundreds) +8 (second's hundreds)=9 +1 (carryover)=10 → 0 here? Wait, no:\n\nWait first number's hundreds digit is 1, second's hundreds digit is 8. So 1+8=9, plus the carryover 1 from the tens place (which was the 1 from the 14 in the tens). Wait, yes, so 9+1=10. Therefore, hundreds place would be 0 and carryover1 again.\n\nWait that's different from my previous calculation! Oh no, I see where I messed up earlier.\n\nSo I think I made an error in the hundreds place calculation before. Let me recalculate all steps with this correction.\n\nStarting over:\n\nOnes place:1+9=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nTens:7+6=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:1 (first) +8 (second) =9 +1 (carryover) =10 → write 0, carryover1.\n\nThousands place: first number's thousands digit is 1, second's is 0 (wait, second number's thousands digit is 0? Let me confirm.\n\nWait the second number is 388,910,869. Breaking down:\n\n- The thousands place: looking at 388,910,869, the thousands digit is the fourth from the right. Let's count:\n\n...8 (ten thousands?), 0 (thousands?), 8 (hundreds), 6 (tens),9 (ones). Wait maybe I messed up the breakdown.\n\nWait 388,910,869:\n\nBreaking down each digit:\n\nBillions place: none.\n\nHundred millions:3\n\nTen millions:8\n\nMillions:8\n\nHundred thousands:9\n\nTen thousands:1\n\nThousands:0\n\nHundreds:8\n\nTens:6\n\nOnes:9\n\nYes, so thousands digit is 0. First number's thousands digit is 1 (from 458,571,171: the thousands digit is the fourth from the right, which is 1).\n\nSo thousands place:1 (first) +0 (second) =1 + carryover1 (from hundreds place) → 2. So thousands place is 2, no carryover.\n\nWait, let me confirm:\n\nAfter hundreds place gave a carryover of 1, so thousands place is 1+0 +1=2.\n\nSo thousands digit is 2, no carryover.\n\nMoving on:\n\nTen thousands place: first number has 7, second has1 →7+1=8. No carryover.\n\nHundred thousands place: first number's hundred thousands digit is5, second is9 →5+9=14. So write 4, carryover1.\n\nMillions place: first's millions digit is8, second's is8 →8+8=16 +1 (carryover)=17 →7, carryover1.\n\nTen millions place: first's ten millions is5, second's is8 →5+8=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions place:4 +3=7 +1=8, no carryover.\n\nNow compiling all digits from highest to lowest:\n\nHundred millions:8\n\nTen millions:4\n\nMillions:7\n\nHundred thousands:4\n\nTen thousands:8\n\nThousands:2\n\nHundreds:0\n\nTens:4\n\nOnes:0\n\nWait, so the number is 847,482,040? But the user's answer was 847,482,044. Hmm. Wait, where's the discrepancy?\n\nWait let's check the ten thousands place again. The ten thousands place for first number is7, second is1, so 7+1=8, that's correct.\n\nThousands place was 1 (first) +0 (second) +1 (carryover from hundreds) =2, so that's right.\n\nHundreds place was 1+8=9 +1 (carryover from tens) =10 → 0 with carryover1.\n\nTens place:7+6=13 +1=14 →4 and carryover1.\n\nOnes:10 →0 carryover1.\n\nMillions place:8+8=16 +1 (from hundred thousands carryover?) Wait, let me go step by step again for the higher places:\n\nAfter hundred thousands place:\n\nHundred thousands:5+9=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nSo millions place digits are 8 (first) and8 (second). Adding 8+8=16 + carryover1 =17 →7 with carryover1.\n\nTen millions place:5 (first) +8 (second)=13 +1=14 →4 and carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:4+3=7 +1=8.\n\nSo putting it all together:\n\nHundred millions:8\n\nTen millions:4\n\nMillions:7\n\nHundred thousands:4\n\nTen thousands:8\n\nThousands:2\n\nHundreds:0\n\nTens:4\n\nOnes:0\n\nSo the number is 847,482,040. But the user's answer was 847,482,044. There's a difference in the last two digits. Wait, the user's answer has 044 at the end, but according to my calculation, it's 040.\n\nHmm, so where did I go wrong?\n\nWait let me check the hundreds place again. The hundreds digit:\n\nFirst number's hundreds place is1, second is8. So 1+8=9 plus the carryover from the tens place (which was 1 from the tens' calculation). So 9+1=10, so hundreds digit is 0, carryover1 to thousands place. That's correct.\n\nThousands place: first is1, second is0. 1+0=1 plus carryover1=2. So thousands digit is2, no carryover. So thousands place is 2, so the thousands digit is 2, not 9 as I thought in my first incorrect calculation.\n\nWait so the thousands digit is 2, not 9. So when building the number, the