final_output:  ["1,009,774,760? Wait, but the user said they think it's 1,009,974,760. Let me check again.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the steps. Let me do the addition step by step digit by digit properly, perhaps I messed up a place.\n\nLet me write both numbers with commas for clarity:\n\nFirst number: 137,931,054\n\nSecond number:871,843,706\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet's write them aligned:\n\n      137,931,054\n    +871,843,706\n    --------------\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):\n\n4 +6 =10 → 0 carryover 1.\n\nTens: 5 +0 +1=6 → 6, no carryover.\n\nHundreds:0 +7 =7 →7.\n\nThousands:1 +3 =4 →4.\n\nTen thousands:3 +4 =7 →7.\n\nHundred thousands:9 +8 =17 →7, carryover 1.\n\nMillions:7 +1 +1=9 →9.\n\nTen millions:3 +8 =11 →1, carryover 1.\n\nHundred millions:1 +8 =9 + carryover 1 →10 → so here, the hundred millions place becomes 0 and carryover 1 to the billions place.\n\nBillions place: 1 (from carryover).\n\nPutting all together:\n\nBillions:1, hundred millions:0, ten millions:1, millions:9, hundred thousands:7, ten thousands:7, thousands:4, hundreds:7, tens:6, ones:0.\n\nWait that would be 1,009,774,760. But the user's answer is 1,009,974,760. Hmm, discrepancy in the hundred thousands place? Let me check again.\n\nWait, let's re-express the numbers digit by digit:\n\nFirst number: 1 3 7 9 3 1 0 5 4\n\nWait, maybe I miscounted the digits. Let me count each number's digits properly.\n\nFirst number: 137,931,054\n\nBreaking down each digit from left to right:\n\n1 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions),7 (millions),9 (hundred thousands),3 (ten thousands),1 (thousands),0 (hundreds),5 (tens),4 (ones).\n\nSecond number:871,843,706\n\n8 (hundred millions),7 (ten millions),1 (millions),8 (hundred thousands),4 (ten thousands),3 (thousands),7 (hundreds),0 (tens),6 (ones).\n\nWait, so when adding:\n\nLet me list each digit's place:\n\nBillions: none, so 0.\n\nHundred millions: 1 vs 8.\n\nTen millions:3 vs7.\n\nMillions:7 vs1.\n\nHundred thousands:9 vs8.\n\nTen thousands:3 vs4.\n\nThousands:1 vs3.\n\nHundreds:0 vs7.\n\nTens:5 vs0.\n\nOnes:4 vs6.\n\nWait, so when adding:\n\nStarting from the ones:\n\nOnes:4+6=10, write 0, carryover 1.\n\nTens:5+0 +1=6. Write 6, carryover 0.\n\nHundreds:0+7=7. Write 7.\n\nThousands:1+3=4. Write4.\n\nTen thousands:3+4=7. Write7.\n\nHundred thousands:9+8=17 →7, carryover1.\n\nMillions:7 (first) +1 (second)=8 + carryover1 →9. Write9.\n\nTen millions:3 (first) +7 (second)=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:1+8=9 + carryover1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nBillions: carryover1.\n\nPutting it all together:\n\nBillions:1, hundred millions:0, ten millions:0 (from 3+7=10?), wait wait. Wait, the ten millions place:\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is 3, second's is7. So 3+7=10, so that gives 0 in ten millions and carryover1 to hundred millions.\n\nBut hundred millions place is 1 (first) +8 (second) +1 (carryover from ten millions)=10, so that's 0 in hundred millions, carryover1 to billions.\n\nThus the digits would be:\n\nBillions place:1,\n\nHundred millions:0,\n\nTen millions:0,\n\nMillions:9,\n\nHundred thousands:7,\n\nTen thousands:7,\n\nThousands:4,\n\nHundreds:7,\n\nTens:6,\n\nOnes:0.\n\nSo the number is 1,009,774,760.\n\nBut the user thought it was 1,009,974,760. Hmm, so there's a discrepancy in the hundred thousands place. Wait, let me check the hundred thousands again.\n\nHundred thousands place: first number's hundred thousands digit is9, second's is8. 9+8=17. So 7 written, carryover1. That part is correct. So the hundred thousands digit is7. So the number would have 7 there. But in the user's answer, it's 974... which would be 9 in the millions? Wait the user's answer is 1,009,974,760. Let me parse that:\n\n1,009,974,760:\n\nBillions:1, hundred millions:0, ten millions:0, millions:9, hundred thousands:9? Wait, maybe I messed up the places.\n\nWait let me structure the final number:\n\nThe sum is 1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 0 (ones). So written as 1,009,774,760.\n\nBut the user's answer is 1,009,974,760. So the difference is in the hundred thousands place (7 vs9) and possibly another place?\n\nWait perhaps I made a mistake in the millions place. Let's check millions place again:\n\nMillions digit for first number is7, second is1. Adding them plus carryover from hundred thousands (which was 1). So 7+1 +1=9. That's correct. So millions digit is9.\n\nHundred thousands is7 (from 9+8=17). So the millions and hundred thousands digits are 9 and7 respectively, making the millions place 9 and the hundred thousands 7, so the number is 1,009,774,760.\n\nThe user's answer has 974 in the middle, implying 9 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands),4 (ten thousands?), but ten thousands was 7. Wait, perhaps the user's answer is wrong? Or did I miscalculate.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I messed up the ten thousands place. Ten thousands place: first has3, second has4. 3+4=7. So that's correct. So the ten thousands is7.\n\nWait, so putting all together:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n0 (ones)\n\n6 (tens)\n\n7 (hundreds)\n\n4 (thousands)\n\n7 (ten thousands)\n\n7 (hundred thousands)\n\n9 (millions)\n\n0 (ten millions)\n\n0 (hundred millions)\n\n1 (billions)\n\nWait, but the order is from billions down:\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions),9 (millions),7 (hundred thousands),7 (ten thousands),4 (thousands),7 (hundreds),6 (tens),0 (ones).\n\nSo that would be written as 1,009,774,760.\n\nThe user's answer is 1,009,974,760. The difference is in the hundred thousands place (7 vs9) and the millions place (9 vs9, so that's okay). So the user might have added the hundred thousands place as 9+8+1? Wait no, the hundred thousands digits were 9 and8, so 9+8=17, so 7 with carryover1. The carryover goes to millions place. So that's correct.\n\nHmm, perhaps the user made an error. Alternatively, maybe I made a mistake in the ten millions place?\n\nWait let's go step