ut the user\'s answer is 1,544,152,944. Hmm, so where did I go wrong?\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the digit positions. Let me try adding the numbers using another method:\n\nFirst number: 689,268,179\n\nSecond number: 854,884,265\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet me add digit by digit again, but this time track each step meticulously.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):\n\n1. Units place: 9 +5 =14 → 4, carryover 1.\n\n2. Tens place:7 +2 =9 +1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\n3. Hundreds place:1 +4=5 +0=5? Wait, no, the first number\'s hundreds digit is 1, second\'s is4. 1+4=5, plus carryover 1? Wait, no, the carryover from previous step was 1, but in step 2, after tens place, we had a carryover of 1. So step3: 1+4 +0 (since carryover was already handled in step2). Wait, no, the carryover from step2 is added to step3\'s total.\n\nWait, let me clarify:\n\nEach step:\n\nStep1 (units): sum is 14 → record 4, carryover1.\n\nStep2 (tens): 7+2 =9, plus carryover1 →10 → record0, carryover1.\n\nStep3 (hundreds):1 (from first number) +4 (from second) =5, plus carryover1 →6 → record6, carryover0.\n\nStep4 (thousands): first number\'s thousands digit is8, second\'s is8 →8+8=16, plus carryover0 →16 → record6, carryover1.\n\nStep5 (ten thousands): first\'s ten thousands is6, second\'s is8 →6+8=14, plus carryover1 →15 → record5, carryover1.\n\nStep6 (hundred thousands): first\'s hundred thousands is2, second\'s is8 →2+8=10, plus carryover1 →11 → record1, carryover1.\n\nStep7 (millions): first\'s millions digit is9, second\'s is4 →9+4=13, plus carryover1 →14 → record4, carryover1.\n\nStep8 (ten millions): first\'s ten millions is8, second\'s is5 →8+5=13, plus carryover1 →14 → record4, carryover1.\n\nStep9 (hundred millions): first\'s hundred millions is6, second\'s is8 →6+8=14, plus carryover1 →15 → record5, carryover1.\n\nFinally, the carryover1 is added as the next digit (billions).\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:5\n\nTen millions:4\n\nMillions:4\n\nHundred thousands:1