 millions).\nDigit3:0 (millions).\nDigit4:1 (hundred thousands).\nDigit5:8 (ten thousands).\nDigit6:8 (thousands).\nDigit7:0 (hundreds).\nDigit8:7 (tens).\nDigit9:0 (ones).\n\nWait, so when written as 390,188,070, the digits are:.\n\n3 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 0 (ones). So the digits from right to left (ones to hundred millions) would be:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):0.\n\nPosition2 (tens):7.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):0.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):8.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):8.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):0.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):9.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):3.\n\nTherefore, when adding to the first number:.\n\nFirst number's digits from right:.\n\nPosition1:2.\n\nPosition2:2.\n\nPosition3:7.\n\nPosition4:4.\n\nPosition5:7.\n\nPosition6:1.\n\nPosition7:0.\n\nPosition8:4.\n\nPosition9:9.\n\nNow adding each position:.\n\nPosition1: 2 +0 =2.\n\nPosition2:2 +7=9.\n\nPosition3:7 +0=7.\n\nPosition4:4 +8=12 → write 2, carryover 1.\n\nPosition5:7 +8=15 +1 (carryover)=16 → write 6, carryover 1.\n\nPosition6:1 +1=2 +1 (carryover)=3 → write 3, no carryover?.\n\nWait, let me check:.\n\nWait Position5: 7 (from first number) +8 (second) =15, plus carryover 1 → total 16. So write 6, carryover 1 to next position (position6).\n\nPosition6: first number has 1, second has1. So 1+1=2, plus the carryover 1 → total 3. So write 3, no carryover.\n\nPosition7: first has0, second has0 (since the second number's millions place is 0). Wait, the second number's position7 (millions) is 0 (from digit7 in their breakdown above). So 0 +0 =0. No carryover.\n\nPosition8: first number has4 (ten millions), second has9 (ten millions). 4 +9=13 → write 3, carryover 1.\n\nPosition9: first has9 (hundred millions), second has3 (hundred millions). 9+3=12 plus carryover 1 → 13. So write 3 and carryover 1.\n\nWait, but since we are at the highest position (hundred millions), the carryover would create a new digit (billions place). So the total would be 1 in billions, 3 in hundred millions, etc.\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nPositions 1-9 (from ones to hundred millions):.\n\nPositions:.\n\n1:2.\n\n2:9.\n\n3:7.\n\n4:2 (with carryover 1 to next).\n\n5:6 (with carryover1).\n\n6:3.\n\n7:0.\n\n8:3 (with carryover1).\n\n9:3 (plus carryover1 from previous? Wait, let me retrace.\n\nWait let's go step by step again with all positions:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 2 +0 =2.\n\nPosition2 (tens):2+7=9.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):7+0=7.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):4+8=12 → write 2, carryover 1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):7+8=15 +1 (carryover) →16 → write6, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):1+1=2 +1 (carryover) →3 → write3, no carryover.\n\nPosition7 (millions):0+0=0 → write0, no carryover.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):4 +9=13 → write3, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):9+3=12 +1 (carryover) →13 → write3, carryover1.\n\nNow, since we've reached the highest digit (position9, hundred millions), the carryover1 becomes a new digit in the billions place (position10).\n\nSo the total digits are now 10 digits: billions place has 1, followed by the others.\n\nSo the sum digits from right to left (positions1 to 10):.\n\nPosition10 (billions):1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):3.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3.\n\nPosition7 (millions):0.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):3.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):2.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):7.\n\nPosition2 (tens):9.\n\nPosition1 (ones):2.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to write the final number as:.\n\nStarting from the billions place down:.\n\n1 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 2 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
