ver1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\nFirst number:7, second:8 →7+8=15. Add carryover1 →16. So digit6, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\nFirst number:4, second:4 →4+4=8. Add carryover1 →9. So digit9, carryover0.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\nFirst number:6, second:1 →6+1=7. Add carryover0 →7. So digit7, no carryover.\n\nWait, but when I added the hundred millions place (position9), it's 6+1=7. So the digits would be:.\n\nStarting from the right, the digits we have are:.\n\nPosition1:8.\n\nPosition2:1.\n\nPosition3:9.\n\nPosition4:3.\n\nPosition5:9.\n\nPosition6:6.\n\nPosition7:6 (from 15+1=16?).\n\nWait, let me redo Position7 again:.\n\nPosition7 (millions place):.\n\nFirst number's millions digit is 7 (since first number's breakdown was 6 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 7 (millions)), so that's correct. The second number's millions digit is 8 (from 1 (hundred millions),4 (ten millions),8 (millions)). .\n\nSo 7+8=15. Then add the carryover from position6's addition. .\n\nPosition6 was hundred thousands place: first number has 9, second 7, so 9+7=16. Then carryover1 from that. So in position6, we had 16 → digit6, carryover1 to next position (position7). .\n\nTherefore, position7's total is 15 (7+8) plus the carryover1 →16. So that gives digit6 and carryover1 to position8.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions): first number is4, second is4. 4+4=8 + carryover1 →9. So digit9, carryover0.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):6+1=7, plus no carryover. So 7.\n\nPutting all digits together from position9 to position1:.\n\nPosition9:7, position8:9, position7:6, position6:6, position5:9, position4:3, position3:9, position2:1, position1:8.\n\nSo writing them in order from highest to lowest place value (left to right):.\n\n7 (hundred millions),9 (ten millions),6 (millions),6 (hundred thousands),9 (ten thousands),3 (thousands),9 (hundreds),1 (tens),8 (ones).\n\nWait, let me confirm the order again. .\n\nPosition9 is the hundred millions place, so first digit is 7.\n\nThen 