right (1 to 9):\n\nPosition 1: 0 (ones)\n\n2:3 (tens)\n\n3:4 (hundreds)\n\n4:5 (thousands)\n\n5:3 (ten thousands)\n\n6:5 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:6 (millions)\n\n8:3 (ten millions)\n\n9:3 (hundred millions)\n\nWait that's different. So the thousands digit (position4) is5. The ten thousands digit (position5) is3, hundred thousands (position6)=5.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition, the thousands place was 3 (from 7+5+1=13). Wait, there's a contradiction here.\n\nAh! Here's the mistake.\n\nWait in the digit-by-digit calculation, when we added the thousands place (position4):\n\nWe had 7 (first number) +5 (second) +1 (carryover) =13. So the thousands digit should be 3, with a carryover of1. But in the final number as written, the thousands digit is 5. That's the error.\n\nWait, so where did I go wrong in the digit-by-digit breakdown?\n\nWait, let's retrace:\n\nAfter adding all digits, the digits from right to left are:\n\nPosition1:0,\n\n2:4,\n\n3:5,\n\n4:3,\n\n5:3,\n\n6:5,\n\n7:6,\n\n8:3,\n\n9:3.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\n0 (position1),4 (2),5 (3),3 (4),3 (5),5 (6),6 (7),3 (8),3 (9).\n\nSo writing them in order from left to right (hundred millions to ones):\n\nPosition9:3,\n\nPosition8:3,\n\nPosition7:6,\n\nPosition6:5,\n\nPosition5:3,\n\nPosition4:3,\n\nPosition3:5,\n\nPosition2:4,\n\nPosition1:0.\n\nSo the number is 3 (hundred millions),3 (ten millions),6 (millions),5 (hundred thousands),3 (ten thousands),3 (thousands),5 (hundreds),4 (tens),0 (ones). \n\nWait, so the thousands digit is 3 (position4), hundred thousands is5 (position6), etc.\n\nSo the number is 336,533,540.\n\nAh! There we go. I must have miscounted the digits when I thought the thousands digit was5. So the digit-by-digit method actually gives 336,533,540, which matches the other method. So my earlier confusion was due to a miscount in the digit positions.\n\nSo the correct answer is 336,533,540. But the user claims the correct answer is 336,533,740. There's a difference 