s correctly.\n\nWait, let's parse each number properly.\n\nFirst number: 649,360,478.\n\nBreaking it down into parts:\n\n649,360,478.\n\nCommas are placed as thousand separators, so it's 649,360,478 \u2192 649 (millions), 360 (thousands), 478.\n\nWait actually, standard comma placement is every three digits from the right. So 649,360,478 is 649,360,478 \u2192 649 million, 360 thousand, 478.\n\nWait so:\n\n- The digits from left to right are:\n\n6 (hundred millions place),\n\n4 (ten millions),\n\n9 (millions),\n\n3 (hundred thousands),\n\n6 (ten thousands),\n\n0 (thousands),\n\n4 (hundreds),\n\n7 (tens),\n\n8 (ones).\n\nWait that's different from what I thought earlier. So the ten thousands digit is the fifth digit from the left: 6 (position5).\n\nWait let me write the number as 6 4 9 3 6 0 4 7 8.\n\nPositions (from left to right):\n\n1: 6 (hundred millions),\n\n2:4 (ten millions),\n\n3:9 (millions),\n\n4:3 (hundred thousands),\n\n5:6 (ten thousands),\n\n6:0 (thousands),\n\n7:4 (hundreds),\n\n8:7 (tens),\n\n9:8 (ones).\n\nSimilarly for the second number:922,516,150 \u2192 9 2 2 5 1 6 1 5 0.\n\nSo positions:\n\n1:9 (hundred millions),\n\n2:2 (ten millions),\n\n3:2 (millions),\n\n4:5 (hundred thousands),\n\n5:1 (ten thousands),\n\n6:6 (thousands),\n\n7:1 (hundreds),\n\n8:5 (tens),\n\n9:0 (ones).\n\nTherefore, when adding, the ten thousands place (position5 from the left, which is the 5th digit) for first number is 6, second is 1. So adding those gives 6 +1 =7. So that's correct.\n\nWait but in my previous calculation when I listed the digits from the right (position1 to position9), the ten thousands place was position5 from the right, which corresponds to position5 from the left? Wait, no. Let me clarify.\n\nWait, the confusion arises because when we number positions from the right, the place values are different. Let me try a different approach.\n\nLet me write both numbers with their digit places clearly:\n\nFirst number: 649,360,478.\n\nDigit places (from left to right):\n\n- Hundred millions:6,\n\n- Ten millions:4,\n\n- Millions:9,\n\n- Hundred thousands:3,\n\n- Ten thousands:6,\n\n- Thousands:0,\n\n- Hundreds:4,\n\n- Tens:7,\n\n- Ones:8.\n\nSecond number:922,516,150.\n\nDigit places:\n\n- Hundred millions:9,\n\n- Ten millions:2,\n\n- Millions:2,\n\n- Hundred thousands:5,\n\n- Ten thousands:1,\n\n- Thousands:6,\n\n- Hundreds:1,\n\n- Tens:5,\n\n- Ones:0.\n\nNow, adding each corresponding place starting from the right (ones place):\n\nOnes:8+0=8.\n\nTens:7+5=12 \u2192 2, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:4+1=5 +1=6, carryover0.\n\nThousands:0+6=6.\n\nHundred thousands:3+5=8.\n\nTen thousands:6+1=7.\n\nMillions:9+2=11 \u2192 1, carryover1.\n\nTen millions:4+2=6 +1 (carryover) =7.\n\nHundred millions:6+9=15 \u21925, carryover1.\n\nThen the carryover1 becomes the billions place.\n\nSo the total is 1 (billions), 5 (hundred millions),7 (ten millions),7 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands?), wait wait:\n\nWait let's list each place's result:\n\nBillions:1.\n\nHundred millions:5 (from 6+9 carryover).\n\nTen millions:7 (4+2 +1 carryover).\n\nMillions:1 (from 9+2's 11).\n\nHundred thousands:8 (3+5).\n\nTen thousands:7 (6+1).\n\nThousands:6 (0+6).\n\nHundreds:6 (4+1 + carryover? Wait, hundreds place was 4 +1 (from second number's hundreds digit?) Wait, let's go again step by step.\n\nWait let me list all the places with their sums:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes (place1):8+0=8.\n\nTens (place2):7+5=12 \u2192 2, carry1.\n\nHundreds (place3):4 (first) +1 (second)=5 +1 (carry)=6.\n\nThousands (place4):0 +6=6.\n\nHundred thousands (place5):3 (first) +5 (second)=8.\n\nWait no, hundred thousands is place5 from the right? Wait, perhaps the places are getting mixed up.\n\nAlternatively, let me list each place's digits:\n\nOnes: place1:8+0.\n\nTens: place2:7+5.\n\nHundreds: place3:4+1.\n\nThousands: place4:0+6.\n\nTen thousands: place5:6 (from first number's ten thousands digit) +1 (second's ten thousands digit) \u21926+1=7.\n\nHundred thousands: place6: