9 +5=14. Write4, carryover1.\n\n8. Tens:4 +6=10. Write0, carryover1.\n\n9. Ones:3 +1=4 + carryover1? Wait no, the carryover from the tens place is added to the hundreds? Wait no. Wait, the ones place addition is 3+1=4, and there is no carryover from the previous (tens) place yet. Wait, actually, the carryover from the tens place (which is the previous digit when moving left) is added to the next digit.\n\nWait, when adding from left to right, but carryover would be handled differently. Maybe it\'s better to add right to left with carries.\n\nAlternatively, let me try again using the right to left approach, digit by digit with proper place values:\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):\n\nOnes:3 +1 =4 → 4.\n\nTens:4 +6=10 → write0, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:9 +5=14 +1=15 →5, carryover1.\n\nThousands:4 +0=4 +1=5 →5, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands:7 +5=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:6 +0=6 +1=7 →7, no carryover.\n\nMillions:5 +5=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nTen millions:1 +4=5 +1=6 →6, no carryover.\n\nHundred millions:2 +9=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nThen the final carryover1 is added as a new digit (billions place).\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\n4 (ones),\n\n0 (tens),\n\n5 (hundreds),\n\n5 (thousands),\n\n2 (ten thousands),\n\n7 (hundred thousands),\n\n0 (millions),\n\n6 (ten millions),\n\n1 (hundred millions),\n\nand the final 1 (billions).\n\nSo arranging from left to right (billions, hundred millions, etc.):\n\n1 (billions),\n\n1 (hundred millions),\n\n6 (ten millions),\n\n0 (millions),\n\n7 (hundred thousands),\n\n2 (ten thousands),\n\n5 (thousands),\n\n5 (hundreds),\n\n0 (tens),\n\n4 (ones).\n\nWait, let\'s see:\n\nStarting from the highest digit, which is the billions place (the final carryover1).\n\nThen the next digits are the hundred millions place (1), ten millions (6), millions (0), hundred thousands (7), ten thousands (2), thousands (5), hundreds (5), tens (0), ones (4).\n\nSo putting them together: 1,160,725,504.\n\nAh! There we go. That matches the user\'s answer of 1,160,735,504? Wait no, the user said 1,160,735,504 but my result here is 1,160,725,504.\n\nHmm, so there must be a miscalculation. Let me check again.\n\nWait in the millions place: when adding the millions digits:\n\nFirst number\'s millions digit is 5 (since 215,674,943 is 215 million... so the millions place is the third digit from the left: 2 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions),5 (millions). Second number\'s millions digit is 5 (from 945,050,561 which is 945 million, so millions digit is the third digit: 9 (hundred millions),4 (ten millions),5 (millions). So 5 +5 =10. So that\'s correct, which gives a 0 in millions place with carryover1.\n\nThen the hundred thousands place: first number has 6 (from 215,674,943: the fourth digit after hundred millions is 6, which is hundred thousands place). Second number\'s hundred thousands digit is 0 (since 945,050,561 has 0 in the fourth digit). So 6+0 +1 (carryover) =7. So that\'s correct, resulting in 7 in hundred thousands place, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands place: first number has 7 (fifth digit), second number has5 (fifth digit). 7+5=12. So write 2, carryover1. Correct.\n\nThousands place: first number\'s thousands digit is4 (sixth digit?), second\'s is0 (sixth digit). So 4+0+1 (carryover)=5. Correct.\n\nHundreds place: first has9 (seventh digit?), second has5 (seventh digit). 9+5=14 →4 with carryover1.\n\nTens place: first has4 (eighth digit?), second has6 (eighth digit). 4+6=10 →0 with carryover1.\n\nOnes place: 3 +1=4 plus the carryover? Wait no, the carryover from tens place is added to the hundreds place. Wait the process is adding each column right to left, so:\n\nStarting from ones:\n\nOnes:3+1=4, no carryover.\n\nTens:4+6=10, so 0 with carryover1 to hundreds.\n\nHundreds:9+5 +1=15 →5 carryover1.\n\nThousands:4+0 +1=5, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands:7+5=12 →2 carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:6+0 +1=7.\n\nMillions:5+5=10 →0 carryover1.\n\nTen millions:1+4 +1=6 (since carryover from millions place).\n\nHundred millions:2+9=11 →1 carryover1.\n\nBillions: carryover1.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\nOnes:4,\n\nTens:0,\n\nHundreds:5,\n\nThousands:5,\n\nTen thousands:2,\n\nHundred thousands:7,\n\nMillions:0,\n\nTen millions:6,\n\nHundred millions:1,\n\nBillions:1.\n\nSo written left to right: 1 (billions), 1 (hundred millions),6 (ten millions),0 (millions),7 (hundred thousands),2 (ten thousands),5 (thousands),5 (hundreds),0 (tens),4 (ones).\n\nThus the number is 1,160,725,504.\n\nBut the user\'s answer is 1,160,735,504. There\'s a discrepancy in the thousands place. Wait, let me check the thousands place again.\n\nWait in the thousands place: first number\'s thousands digit is 4 (from 215,674,943: the number is 215,674,943. Breaking down:\n\n215,674,943:\n\n- 215 (millions part),\n\n- 674 (thousands part),\n\n- 943 (units).\n\nWait so the thousands digit is the fourth digit from the right in the millions section? Maybe my digit breakdown was wrong.\n\nWait let\'s parse the numbers properly:\n\nFirst number: 215,674,943.\n\nBreaking into digits:\n\nDigit positions (from right, 1 to 9):\n\n1:3 (ones)\n\n2:4 (tens)\n\n3:9 (hundreds)\n\n4:4 (thousands)\n\n5:7 (ten thousands)\n\n6:6 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:5 (millions)\n\n8:1 (ten millions)\n\n9:2 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number:945,050,561.\n\nDigits from right:\n\n1:1 (ones)\n\n2:6 (tens)\n\n3:5 (hundreds)\n\n4:0 (thousands)\n\n5:5 (ten thousands)\n\n6:0 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:4 (millions)\n\n8:5 (ten millions)\n\n9:9 (hundred millions)\n\nSo when adding the thousands place (4th digit from the right):\n\nFirst number\'s thousands digit is4, second number\'s thousands digit is0.\n\nAdding 4 +0 =4, plus any carryover from the hundreds place.\n\nWait the hundreds place (3rd digit):\n\nFirst:9, second:5 →9+5=14 → carryover1.\n\nSo thousands place:4+0 +1 (carryover from hundreds) =5. That\'s correct.\n\nTen thousands place (5th digit):\n\n7 (first) +5 (second) =12. So 2 with carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands (6th digit):\n\n6 +0 +1 (carryover) =7.\n\nMillions (7th digit):\n\n5 (first) +4 (second) =9? Wait wait, 5+4?\n\nWait first number\'s millions digit is5 (since 215,674,943 has the millions digit as the seventh digit from the right, which is 5). Second number\'s millions digit is4 (from 945,050,561, the millions digit is the seventh digit from the right, which is 4? Wait let\'s see:\n\nWait second number:945,050,561.\n\nBreaking down:\n\nFrom the right:\n\n1:1,\n\n2:6,\n\n3:5,\n\n4:0,\n\n5:5,\n\n6:0,\n\n7:4,\n\n8:5,\n\n9:9.\n\nSo the millions digit is the seventh from the right, which is 4 (since 945,050,561 is 945 million, so the millions digit is the third digit from the left in the millions group? Wait maybe I\'m overcomplicating.\n\nAlternatively, the millions place is the 7th digit from the right in a 9-digit number.\n\nFirst number\'s millions digit (7th from right):\n\nPosition7 (counting from right 1 to9):\n\nPosition1:3,\n\n2:4,\n\n3:9,\n\n4:4,\n\n5:7,\n\n6:6,\n\n7:5,\n\n8:1,\n\n9:2.\n\nYes, so 5.\n\nSecond number\'s millions digit (7th from right):\n\nPosition7:4.\n\nThus, 5+4 =9. Wait but earlier I thought it was 5+5=10. Wait where did that come from? Wait I must have messed up earlier steps.\n\nAh! Here\'s the mistake! In my earlier step when adding the millions place, I incorrectly said first number\'s millions digit was5 and second\'s was5, but actually the second number\'s millions digit is4. So that was an error.\n\nLet me recalculate:\n\nStarting over with correct digits:\n\nAdding each column from right to left with correct digits:\n\nOnes:3+1=4.\n\nTens:4+6=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:9+5=14 +1=15 →5, carryover1.\n\nThousands:4+0=4 +1=5 →5, carryover0.\n\nTen thousands:7+5=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:6+0=6 +1=7 →7, carryover0.\n\nMillions:5 +4=9 →9, no carryover.\n\nTen millions:1 (from first number) +5 (from second number)=6 →6, no carryover.\n\nHundred millions:2 +9=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nBillions place:1 (carryover).\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\nOnes:4,\n\nTens:0,\n\nHundreds:5,\n\nThousands:5,\n\nTen thousands:2,\n\nHundred thousands:7,\n\nMillions:9,\n\nTen millions:6,\n\nHundred millions:1,\n\nBillions:1.\n\nSo arranging from left to right:\n\n1 (billions),\n\n1 (hundred millions),\n\n6 (ten millions),\n\n9 (millions),\n\n7 (hundred thousands),\n\n2 (ten thousands),\n\n5 (thousands),\n\n5 (hundreds),\n\n0 (tens),\n\n4 (ones).\n\nSo the total is 1,169,725,504.\n\nWait but now this conflicts with the previous step where I thought the millions place was 5+4=9. So that\'s where the confusion was. Earlier when I thought the second number\'s millions digit was5, but it\'s actually4.\n\nTherefore, the correct sum is 1,169,725,504.\n\nBut the user says the answer is 1,160,735,504. Hmm, so there must be another mistake.\n\nWait let\'s try adding the numbers directly using another method:\n\n215,674,943\n\n+945,050,561\n\n= ?\n\nLet me add 215,674,943 + 945,050,561:\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\nFirst, 215,674,943 + 900,000,000 = 1,115,674,943.\n\nThen add 45,050,561:\n\n1,115,674,943 +45,050,561.\n\nBreakdown:\n\n1,115,674,943 +40,000,000 =1,155,674,943.\n\nThen +5,050,561:\n\n1,155,674,943 +5,050,561 =1,160,725,504.\n\nAh! Here\'s the correct total:1,160,725,504.\n\nWait, so why the discrepancy? Because in the digit-by-digit approach, I messed up the millions place.\n\nWait let\'s re-examine the digit breakdown.\n\nWait the first number is 215,674,943.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- 200,000,000 (hundred millions)\n\n- 15,000,000 (ten millions)\n\n- 674,000 (thousands)\n\n- 943 (units)\n\nSecond number:945,050,561 is:\n\n-900,000,000 (hundred millions)\n\n-45,000,000 (ten millions)\n\n-50,000 (hundred thousands)\n\n-561 (units)\n\nWait adding:\n\n200M +900M =1,100M.\n\n15M +45M =60M → total so far 1,160M.\n\n674,000 +50,000 =724,000.\n\n943 +561 =1,504.\n\nSo total is 1,160,000,000 +724,000 +1,504 =1,160,725,504.\n\nAh, so that\'s the correct answer. So why did the digit-by-digit approach give a different result?\n\nBecause in the digit-by-digit approach earlier, I incorrectly added the millions place as 5+5 instead of 5+4.\n\nWait let\'s re-examine the digit-by-digit steps with correct digits:\n\nFirst number\'s millions digit is 5 (from 215,674,943: the millions digit is the third digit from the left, which is 5).\n\nSecond number\'s millions digit is 4 (from 945,050,561: the millions digit is the third digit from the left, which is 4).\n\nTherefore, millions place:5 +4 =9, so no carryover. That\'s correct.\n\nThen the ten millions place: first number\'s ten millions digit is1, second\'s is4 →1+4=5. No carryover.\n\nHundred millions:2+9=11 →1 with carryover1.\n\nSo the digits would be:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:1,\n\nTen millions:5,\n\nMillions:9,\n\nHundred thousands:6+0=6,\n\nTen thousands:7+5=12 →2 with carryover1,\n\nWait wait, let\'s do the digit-by-digit again properly.\n\nStarting from the right (ones):\n\nPosition1 (ones):3+1=4.\n\nPosition2 (tens):4+6=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9+5=14+1=15 →5, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):4+0=4 +1=5, no carryover.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):7+5=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):6+0=6 +1=7, no carryover.\n\nPosition7 (millions):5+4=9, no carryover.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):1+4=5, no carryover.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):2+9=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nFinal carryover1 to billions.\n\nThus, digits from right to left:\n\n4,0,5,5,2,7,9,5,1,1.\n\nArranged left to right (billions first):\n\n1 (billions),\n\n1 (hundred millions),\n\n5 (ten millions),\n\n9 (millions),\n\n7 (hundred thousands),\n\n2 (ten thousands),\n\n5 (thousands),\n\n5 (hundreds),\n\n0 (tens),\n\n4 (ones).\n\nWait, that would be 1,159,725,504? No, let me list them properly:\n\nBillions digit:1,\n\nHundred millions:1,\n\nTen millions:5,\n\nMillions:9,\n\nHundred thousands:7,\n\nTen thousands:2,\n\nThousands:5,\n\nHundreds:5,\n\nTens:0,\n\nOnes:4.\n\nSo the number is 1,159,725,504