 So adding those:.\n\n9 (first) +5 (second) +1 (carryover) =15 → 5 with carryover1.\n\nTen millions place:.\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is 3, second's ten millions is 5.\n\nSo 3 +5 +1 (carryover) =9. No carryover here.\n\nHundred millions place:.\n\nFirst number's hundred millions is 9, second's is1.\n\n9 +1 =10. So here, we write 0 and carryover1. But since this is the highest place, the carryover becomes a new digit at the beginning.\n\nSo putting all together:.\n\nStarting from the right, the digits are:.\n\nOnes:6.\n\nTens:5.\n\nHundreds:1.\n\nThousands:2.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nHundred thousands:9.\n\nMillions:5.\n\nTen millions:9.\n\nHundred millions:0 (from 9+1=10, so 0 with carryover1).\n\nThen the carryover1 becomes the next digit (billions place).\n\nSo the total digits would be:.\n\nBillions place:1.\n\nHundred millions:0.\n\nTen millions:9.\n\nMillions:5.\n\nHundred thousands:9.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nThousands:2.\n\nHundreds:1.\n\nTens:5.\n\nOnes:6.\n\nSo combining all these digits from left to right:.\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions),5 (millions),9 (hundred thousands),3 (ten thousands),2 (thousands),1 (hundreds),5 (tens),6 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
