      "text": "1,075,377,863? Wait, but the user's answer was 1,075,377,867. Hmm, there's a discrepancy here. Let me check again.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the calculation steps. Let me go through again carefully.\n\nLet me write each digit step by step with their place values.\n\nFirst number: 299,615,329.\n\nBreaking down each digit from right (ones) to left (hundred millions):\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 9\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 2\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds): 3\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):5\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):1\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):6\n\nPosition 7 (millions):9\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):9\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):2\n\nSecond number:775,762,534.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):4\n\nPosition2:3\n\nPosition3:5\n\nPosition4:2\n\nPosition5:6\n\nPosition6:7\n\nPosition7:5\n\nPosition8:7\n\nPosition9:7\n\nNow, adding each position:\n\nStarting with Position 1:\n\n9 +4 =13 \u2192 3, carryover 1.\n\nPosition2: 2 +3 =5 +1=6 \u2192 6, no carryover.\n\nPosition3:3 +5=8 \u21928.\n\nPosition4:5 +2=7 \u21927.\n\nPosition5:1 +6=7 \u21927.\n\nPosition6:6 +7=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nPosition7:9 +5=14 +1 (carry) =15 \u21925, carryover1.\n\nPosition8:9 +7=16 +1 (carry)=17 \u21927, carryover1.\n\nPosition9:2 +7=9 +1 (carry)=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nNow, after all positions, the carryover is 1, so we add that as a new digit at the front.\n\nThus, the digits from the first (position1) to the last (position9 plus the carryover) are:\n\nPosition1:3\n\nPosition2:6\n\nPosition3:8\n\nPosition4:7\n\nPosition5:7\n\nPosition6:3\n\nPosition7:5\n\nPosition8:7\n\nPosition9:0\n\nPlus the carryover 1, so the total digits are:\n\n1 (billions place), followed by 0 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 3 (ones).\n\nWait, let me arrange them in order from highest to lowest place:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:0\n\nTen millions:7\n\nMillions:5\n\nHundred thousands:3\n\nTen thousands:7\n\nThousands:7\n\nHundreds:8\n\nTens:6\n\nOnes:3\n\nSo the number is 1,075,377,863.\n\nBut the user's answer was 1,075,377,867. Hmm, that's different. So where did I go wrong?\n\nWait, let me check the Position7 again. Position7 is millions place.\n\nFirst number's millions digit:9 (since in the breakdown earlier, Position7 for the first number is 9).\n\nSecond number's millions digit:5 (from the second number's Position7:5).\n\nSo 9+5=14, plus the carryover from Position6 (which was 13, so carryover1). Wait, Position6 was 6+7=13, so that carryover is 1. So adding to Position7:\n\n9+5=14, plus carryover1 gives 15. So the digit is 5, carryover1. That's correct.\n\nPosition8: ten millions place.\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is9 (Position8 of first number).\n\nSecond number's ten millions digit is7 (Position8 of second number).\n\n9+7=16, plus the carryover1 makes 17. So digit 7, carryover1. Correct.\n\nPosition9: hundred millions place.\n\nFirst number:2, second:7 \u2192 2+7=9, plus carryover1 \u219210. So 0 with carryover1. Correct.\n\nSo the total is 1,075,377,863. But the user's answer was 1,075,377,867. So there's a difference in the last digit. Wait, let me check Position1 again.\n\nPosition1: ones place: first number's 9 + second's 4 =13. So 3. That's correct. So the last digit should be 3, but the user's answer ends with 7. Wait, the user's answer is 1075377867. Let's parse that number:\n\nBreaking down 1,075,377,867:\n\nOnes digit:7. Hmm, so that's different from my calculation. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait, maybe I miscounted the digits in the original numbers. Let me check the original numbers again.\n\nFirst number:299,615,329.\n\nLet me count the digits: 2 9 9 6 1 5 3 2 9 \u2192 9 digits. So that's correct.\n\nSecond number:775,762,534. Let's count:7 7 5 7 6 2 5 3 4 \u2192 9 digits. Correct.\n\nWait, perhaps I made an error in the positions for the second number. Let me re-examine the breakdown.\n\nSecond number:775,762,534.\n\nBreaking do