seem correct. The only possibility is that the breakdown method missed a carryover somewhere.\n\nWait the breakdown method\'s total is1,685,545,828, which is less than the digit-wise result by3,000. That implies that in the breakdown, the remaining parts were added to give545,828 instead of548,828.\n\nWait wait a second! Wait in the breakdown\'s remaining addition:\n\nFirst number\'s remaining part is521,299.\n\nSecond\'s remaining part is24,529.\n\nAdding those:\n\n521,299 +24,529.\n\nLet me compute this again carefully:\n\n521,299\n\n+  24,529\n\n------------\n\nAdding units:9+9=18 →8, carry1.\n\nTens:9+2=11 +1=12 →2, carry1.\n\nHundreds:2+5=7 +1=8 →8, carry0.\n\nThousands:1+4=5 →5.\n\nTen thousands:2+2=4.\n\nHundred thousands:5+0=5.\n\nTotal is545,828. That\'s correct.\n\nSo the digit-wise total must be wrong. Wait but how?\n\nWait let me use a calculator to compute the actual sum:\n\n748,521,299 +937,024,529.\n\nLet me add 700,000,000 +900,000,000 =1,600,000,000.\n\n48,000,000 +37,000,000 =85,000,000 → total so far1,685,000,000.\n\nNow the remaining parts:\n\n521,299 +24,529 =545,828.\n\nAdding to1,685,000,000 gives1,685,545,828.\n\nSo the correct answer is1,685,545,828.\n\nTherefore, the digit-wise addition must have an error.\n\nWait where?\n\nLet me re-express the digit-wise addition:\n\nPosition6 (millions):\n\nFirst number\'s millions digit is8 (from 748,521,299 → the millions digit is8).\n\nSecond number\'s millions digit is7 (from937,024,529 → millions digit is7).\n\n8+7=15 →5, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):\n\nFirst\'s ten millions digit is4 (from748,521,299 → ten millions is4).\n\nSecond\'s ten millions digit is3 (from937,024,529 → ten millions is3).\n\n4+3+1 (carry)=8 → correct.\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):\n\nFirst\'s7, second\'s9 →16 →6, carry1.\n\nPosition9:1.\n\nSo the millions digit is5, ten millions is8, hundred millions is6.\n\nThus the number is1,685,548,828.\n\nWait but according to the breakdown, it should be1,685,545,828. There