nSimilarly 303,837,752 is 3,0,3,8,3,7,7,5,2.\n\nSo adding digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):\n\nPosition 1 (ones):\n\n7 (from first) + 2 (from second) = 9. Correct.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):\n\n7 +5 =12. So 2 with carryover 1.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):\n\n5 +7 =12 plus 1 is 13. So 3, carryover 1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\n4 +3 =7 plus 1=8. No carryover.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\n0 +8 =8.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\n3 +3=6.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait position6: first number's hundred thousands place is the 6th digit from the right.\n\nWait let me list all positions properly:\n\nLet me write both numbers with positions from right (starting at 1):\n\nFirst number: 104304577\n\nDigits from right (position 1 to 9):\n\nPosition 1:7\n\n2:7\n\n3:5\n\n4:4\n\n5:0\n\n6:3\n\n7:4\n\n8:0\n\n9:1\n\nSecond number: 303837752\n\nDigits from right (position1 to9):\n\nPosition1:2\n\n2:5\n\n3:7\n\n4:7\n\n5:3\n\n6:8\n\n7:3\n\n8:0\n\n9:3\n\nWait now adding each position:\n\nPosition1:7+2=9\n\nPosition2:7+5=12 (write 2, carry1)\n\nPosition3:5+7=12 +1=13 (3, carry1)\n\nPosition4:4+7=11 +1=12 (2, carry1)\n\nWait, hold on, position4: first number's digit is 4 (from first number's position4), second number's position4 is7 (since second number's position4 is digit at thousands place, which is 7 in 303837752: let me see:\n\nWait 303,837,752: the thousands digit is the 4th from the right: 7 (since the number is 303,837,752: breaking down:\n\n303,837,752:\n\n- 2 (ones)\n\n-5 (tens)\n\n-7 (hundreds)\n\n-7 (thousands)\n\n-3 (ten thousands)\n\n-8 (hundred thousands)\n\n-3 (millions)\n\n-0 (ten millions)\n\n-3 (hundred millions)\n\nWait, perhaps my earlier breakdown was wrong. Let me parse the second number properly:\n\nSecond number: 303,837,752\n\nBreaking down each digit:\n\nHundred millions: 3\n\nTen millions:0\n\nMillions:3\n\nHundred thousands:8\n\nTen thousands:3\n\nThousands:7\n\nHundreds:7\n\nTens:5\n\nOnes:2\n\nWait so written out as digits from left to right:\n\n3 (hundred millions),0 (ten millions),3 (millions),8 (hundred thousands),3 (ten thousands),7 (thousands),7 (hundreds),5 (tens),2 (ones).\n\nTherefore, the digits from right (position1 to9):\n\nPosition1 (ones):2\n\nPosition2 (tens):5\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):7\n\nPosition4 (thousands):7\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):8\n\nPosition7 (millions):3\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):0\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):3\n\nFirst number:104,304,577\n\nDigits from left:\n\n1 (hundred millions),0 (ten millions),4 (millions),3 (hundred thousands),0 (ten thousands),4 (thousands),5 (hundreds),7 (tens),7 (ones).\n\nSo from right:\n\nPosition1 (ones):7\n\nPosition2 (tens):7\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):5\n\nPosition4 (thousands):4\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):0\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):3\n\nPosition7 (millions):4\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):0\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):1\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nPosition1:7+2=9\n\nPosition2:7+5=12 (2, carryover1)\n\nPosition3:5+7=12 +1=13 (3, carryover1)\n\nPosition4:4 (from first) +7 (from second) =11 +1 (carry)=12 (2, carryover1)\n\nPosition5:0 +3 =3 +1=4 (carryover0)\n\nPosition6:3+8=11 (1, carryover1)\n\nPosition7:4 (first) +3 (second)=7 +1=8 (carryover0)\n\nPosition8:0 +0=0\n\nPosition9:1 +3=4\n\nNow compiling the digits from position1 to9:\n\nPosition1:9\n\n2:2\n\n3:3\n\n4:2\n\n5:4\n\n6:1\n\n7:8\n\n8:0\n\n9:4\n\nSo the number is:\n\nPosition9 to1: 4 0 8 1 4 2 3 2 9\n\nWhich is 408,142,329.\n\nTherefore the correct answer is 408,142,329. The user thought it was 408,142,320. So the mistake is in the last digit. Wait in position1 (ones place), 7+2=9, so the final digit should be 9, not 0. Hence the user's answer is off by 9. Probably a typo or miscalculation in the ones place.\n\nSo the correct answer is 408,142,329.\n</think>\n\n",
