nes)\n\n2:4 (tens)\n\n3:5 (hundreds)\n\n4:4 (thousands)\n\n5:7 (ten thousands)\n\n6:2 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:9 (millions)\n\n8:3 (ten millions)\n\n9:6 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number (343,672,113):\n\nDigit positions:\n\n1:3 (ones)\n\n2:1 (tens)\n\n3:1 (hundreds)\n\n4:2 (thousands)\n\n5:7 (ten thousands)\n\n6:6 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:3 (millions)\n\n8:4 (ten millions)\n\n9:3 (hundred millions)\n\nNow adding each digit:\n\nPosition 1:9+3=12 \u2192 2, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 2:4+1=5 +1=6.\n\nPosition3:5+1=6 \u2192 no carryover.\n\nPosition4:4+2=6 \u2192 no carryover.\n\nPosition5:7+7=14 \u2192 4, carryover1.\n\nPosition6:2+6=8 +1=9 \u2192 no carryover.\n\nPosition7:9+3=12 \u2192 2, carryover1.\n\nPosition8:3+4=7 +1=8 \u2192 no carryover.\n\nPosition9:6+3=9.\n\nSo putting it all together from right to left:\n\nDigits are:\n\nPosition1:2\n\n2:6\n\n3:6\n\n4:6\n\n5:4\n\n6:9\n\n7:2\n\n8:8\n\n9:9\n\nSo the number is 9 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands?), wait let me construct the number step by step.\n\nWait, the digits from position9 to position1:\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions): 9\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):8\n\nPosition7 (millions):2\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):9\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):4\n\nPosition4 (thousands):6\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):6\n\nPosition2 (tens):6\n\nPosition1 (ones):2\n\nSo the number is 982,946,662.\n\nBut the user thought the answer was 988,946,662. Hmm, discrepancy here. So where did I go wrong?\n\nWait, let me check the positions again. Maybe I messed up the digit positions in the breakdown.\n\nWait the first number is 639,274,549:\n\nBreaking down with commas: 639,274,549 \u2192 639 (millions), 274 (thousands), 549.\n\nSo:\n\nMillions place: 6 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 9 (millions)\n\nThousands place: 2 (hundred thousands),7 (ten thousands),4 (thousands)\n\nHundreds place:5 (hundreds),4 (tens),9 (ones).\n\nWait, so the millions digit breakdown:\n\n- The first three digits (from le