rry1.\n\nDigit3 (millions):3 (first) +8 (second)=11 +1=12 \u21922, carry1.\n\nDigit2 (ten millions):0 (first) +2 (second)=2 +1=3 \u21923, carry0.\n\nDigit1 (hundred millions):1 +8=9.\n\nSo the digits from digit1 to digit9 (hundred millions to ones):\n\nDigit1:9\n\nDigit2:3\n\nDigit3:2\n\nDigit4:3\n\nDigit5:6\n\nDigit6:4\n\nDigit7:3\n\nDigit8:1\n\nDigit9:0\n\nThus the number is 9 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 0 (ones). So that's 932,364,310.\n\nBut the user's answer is 932,334,310. Hmm, so there's a discrepancy in the hundred thousands place. Wait, maybe I made a mistake in digit4?\n\nWait let's check digit4 (hundred thousands place):\n\nFirst number's digit4 is the fourth digit from the left, which is 7 (since first digit is 1, second 0, third 3, fourth 7). The second number's digit4 is the fourth digit from the left, which is 5 (the number is 828,585,557, so digits 1-9: 8,2,8,5,8,5,5,5,7. So the fourth digit is 5. So 7+5 =12, plus carryover 1 from previous? Wait wait, let's go step by step again:\n\nWait in digit5 (ten thousands place):\n\nFirst number's digit5 is the fifth digit:7 (since first is 1,0,3,7,7,...). Second's digit5 is the fifth digit:8 (8,2,8,5,8,...). So 7+8=15 plus carryover 1 (from digit6?) Wait no, let's track carryovers properly:\n\nLet me list each step with the carries:\n\nStarting from digit9 (ones):\n\nSum:3+7=10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\ndigit8 (tens):\n\n5+5=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\ndigit7 (hundreds):\n\n7+5=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\ndigit6 (thousands):\n\n8+5=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\ndigit5 (ten thousands):\n\n7 (first) +8 (second) =15 +1=16 \u21926, carryover1.\n\ndigit4 (hundred thousands):\n\n7 (first) +5 (second)=12 +1 (carry)=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\ndigit3 (millions):\n\n3 (first) +8 (second)=11 +1=12 \u21922, carryover1.\n\ndigit2 (ten millions):\n\n0 +2=2 +1=3 \u21923, carryover0.\n\ndigit1 (hundred millions):\n\n1+8=9.\n\nSo the hundred thousands place (digit4) is indeed 3, with a carryover of 1. So the ten thousands place (digit5) had 15+1=16? Wait digit5 is ten thousands place:\n\nWait digit5 is the fifth from the right? Wait no, sorry, maybe I got the digit numbering reversed. Wait in the way I did it earlier, digit9 is the ones place, so digit1 is the leftmost (hundred millions). So when moving from right to left, digit1 is the highest.\n\nWait but the calculation is correct as per the step-by-step. So the hundred thousands digit (which is digit4 in the left-to-right numbering, but digit6 in the right-to-left numbering) \u2014 perhaps I'm mixing up the digit positions here.\n\nWait, perhaps the confusion is in the place names. Let me try adding the numbers numerically without breaking into places:\n\n103,778,753\n\n+828,585,557\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n3 +7 =10, write 0, carry 1.\n\n5 +5 =10 plus 1 is 11, write 1, carry1.\n\n7 +5 =12 plus 1 is13, write3, carry1.\n\n8 +5 =13 plus1 is14, write4, carry1.\n\n7 (ten thousands place of first number) +8 (ten thousands of second) =15 +1=16 \u21926, carry1.\n\n7 (hundred thousands of first number?) Wait wait, the first number is 103,778,753. Let me write each digit's value:\n\nBreaking down 103,778,753:\n\n- 1 (hundred million)\n\n- 0 (ten million)\n\n- 3 (million)\n\n- 7 (hundred thousand)\n\n- 7 (ten thousand)\n\n- 8 (thousand)\n\n- 7 (hundred)\n\n- 5 (ten)\n\n- 3 (one)\n\nSimilarly, 828,585,557:\n\n- 8 (hundred million)\n\n- 2 (ten million)\n\n- 8 (million)\n\n- 5 (hundred thousand)\n\n- 8 (ten thousand)\n\n- 5 (thousand)\n\n- 5 (hundred)\n\n- 5 (ten)\n\n- 7 (one)\n\nSo adding the hundred thousands place:\n\nFirst number:7 (hundred thousands)\n\nSecond number:5 (hundred thousands)\n\nWait, no. Wait the first number's hundred thousands digit is the fourth digit from the left: 1 (1), 0 (2),3 (3),7 (4) \u2192 yes, so hundred thousands is 7.\n\nSecond number's hundred thousands digit