.\n\nPosition 1 (ones place):.\n\nFirst number: 1.\n\nSecond number:6.\n\nSum:1+6=7. Write 7. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition 2 (tens place):.\n\nFirst number:0.\n\nSecond number:4.\n\nSum:0+4=4. Write 4. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds place):.\n\nFirst number:2.\n\nSecond number:2.\n\nSum:2+2=4. Write 4. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands place):.\n\nFirst number:0.\n\nSecond number:8.\n\nSum:0+8=8. Write 8. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands place):.\n\nFirst number:6.\n\nSecond number:6.\n\nSum:6+6=12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands place):.\n\nFirst number:8.\n\nSecond number:0.\n\nSum:8+0 + 1 (carryover) =9. Write 9. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition 7 (millions place):.\n\nFirst number:7.\n\nSecond number:2.\n\nSum:7+2=9. Write 9. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions place):.\n\nFirst number:1.\n\nSecond number:2.\n\nSum:1+2=3. Write 3. Carryover 0.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions place):.\n\nFirst number:5.\n\nSecond number:7.\n\nSum:5+7=12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nNow, since there are no more digits, we have to account for the carryover 1. This becomes a new digit to the left of the hundred millions place, which is the billions place. Wait, actually, the hundred millions place is the 9th digit from the right. Adding another digit (the carryover 1) would be the ten billions place, which is the 10th digit from the right.\n\nSo putting it all together, the digits from right to left (positions 1 to 10):.\n\nPosition 10 (ten billions place):1.\n\nPosition9 (billions place? Wait, need to clarify numbering. Wait, perhaps I need to structure the digits properly.\n\nWait, let me re-express the result step by step:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones), the digits we have are:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):7.\n\nPosition2 (tens):4.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):4.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):8.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):2.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):9.\n\nPosition7 (millions):9.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):2 (from the sum of 5+7 plus the carryover? Wait no, the hundred millions place was the 9th digit from the right. Wait, in the original numbers, each had 9 digits. When adding, the sum may have 10 digits. So let's see:.\n\nWait, the sum of two 9-digit numbers can be up to 10 digits. In this case, since 517,860,201 + 722,068,246, the first is about 500 million, the second about 700 million, so total around 1.2 billion, which is 10 digits</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
