s place is first's 3 + second's5 + 1 (carryover) = 3+5=8 +1=9. So 9, no carryover.\n\nSo the thousands place is 9.\n\nSo in the final digits, thousands place is 9.\n\nContinuing:\n\nHundred thousands place is 2+4 + the carried 1 from ten thousands (which was 15, so carryover 1). So 2+4=6 +1=7. Correct.\n\nTen thousands place:8+7=15 →5, carryover1.\n\nMillions place:5+1=6 (no carryover).\n\nTen millions:9+3=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:6+3 +1 (carryover) =10 →0, carryover1.\n\nBillions place:1.\n\nSo the final digits from left to right (billions down to ones):\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 6 (millions),7 (hundred thousands),5 (ten thousands),9 (thousands),6 (hundreds),8 (tens),7 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 1,026,759,687? Wait, let's construct it step by step:\n\nBillions: 1,\n\nHundred millions:0,\n\nTen millions:2,\n\nMillions:6,\n\nHundred thousands:7,\n\nTen thousands:5,\n\nThousands:9,\n\nHundreds:6,\n\nTens:8,\n\nOnes:7.\n\nPutting them together:\n\n1,026,759,687?\n\nWait, but the user's answer was 1009459687. Hmm, there's a discrepancy here. Maybe I made an error in the place values.\n\nWait let's check the ten millions place again.\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is 9 (since the first number is 695,283,749: breaking down as 6 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 5 (millions), etc. So yes, ten millions digit is 9.\n\nSecond number's ten millions digit is 3 (314,575,938: 3 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), so ten millions digit is 1? Wait wait, perhaps I messed up the digit positions earlier.\n\nWait let me re-breakdown the numbers properly:\n\nFirst number:695,283,749.\n\nBreaking down each digit from left to right:\n\nDigit 1 (hundred millions place):6\n\nDigit 2 (ten millions):9\n\nDigit3 (millions):5\n\nDigit4 (hundred thousands):2\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):8\n\nDigit6 (thousands):3\n\nDigit7 (hundreds):7\n\nDigit8 (tens):4\n\nDigit9 (ones):9\n\nSecond number:314,575,938.\n\nDigit1 (hundred millions):3\n\nDigit2