ds).\n\n6:3 (hundred thousands).\n\n7:5 (millions).\n\n8:9 (ten millions).\n\n9:4 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number: 602,443,134.\n\nDigits:.\n\n1:4 (ones).\n\n2:3 (tens).\n\n3:1 (hundreds).\n\n4:3 (thousands).\n\n5:4 (ten thousands).\n\n6:4 (hundred thousands).\n\n7:2 (millions).\n\n8:0 (ten millions).\n\n9:6 (hundred millions).\n\nSo when adding, let's go step by step:.\n\nStarting with position 1 (ones):.\n\n4 +4 =8. Write down 8.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):.\n\n0 +3 =3. Write 3.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):.\n\n5 +1=6. Write 6.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):.\n\n0 +3=3. Write 3.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):.\n\n6 +4=10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):.\n\n3 +4 =7, plus the carryover 1 makes 8. Write 8, no carryover.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\n5 +2 =7. Write 7.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\n9 +0=9. Write 9.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\n4 +6=10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nNow, since there's a carryover from the hundred millions, we need to add a new digit at the billions place (position10):.\n\n1. So the total sum is 1 followed by the digits we wrote, replacing the final 0 in the hundred millions.\n\nPutting it all together, the digits from position1 to position10 are:.\n\nPosition10:1.\n\nPosition9:0.\n\nPosition8:9.\n\nPosition7:7.\n\nPosition6:8.\n\nPosition5:0.\n\nPosition4:3.\n\nPosition3:6.\n\nPosition2:3.\n\nPosition1:8.\n\nWait, no. Wait, let me list them properly. The carryover adds a new digit at the front. So the digits are:.\n\nBillions (position10):1.\n\nHundred millions (position9):0 (from the 10 in position9, which was 4+6+0=10, so 0 with carryover).\n\nWait, actually, after position9 (hundred millions), adding 4+6 gives 10, so we write 0 and carryover 1 to the next (billions) place. So the billions place is that 1.\n\nSo the final number is:.\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), then the rest of the digits from the previous steps.\n\nWait let me reconstruct:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (position1) to the left:.\n\nPosition1:8.\n\nPosition2:3.\n\nPosition3:6.\n\nPosition4:3.\n\nPosition5:0 (because we had a carryover here?).\n\nWait, no, let me list each digit step by step again:.\n\nAfter each position's result:.\n\nPosition1: 8.\n\nPosition2:3.\n\nPosition3:6.\n\nPosition4:3.\n\nPosition5:0 (since 10, we wrote 0 and carried 1).\n\nPosition6:8 (from 3+4+1=8).\n\nPosition7:7 (5+2).\n\nPosition8:9 (9+0).\n\nPosition9:0 (from 4+6=10, so 0 with carryover).\n\nAnd then the carryover 1 to position10:1.\n\nTherefore, the total digits are:.\n\nPosition10:1.\n\nPosition9:0.\n\nPosition8:9.\n\nPosition7:7.\n\nPosition6:8.\n\nPosition5:0.\n\nPosition4:3.\n\nPosition3:6.\n\nPosition2:3.\n\nPosition1:8.\n\nPutting them all together from left to right (billions to ones):.\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nWait that can't be right. Wait, let me see:.\n\nWait the digits in each position:.\n\nThe positions are numbered from right (ones) as 1 to left (billions as 10). So when we write the number, the leftmost digit is the billions place. So the digits would be:.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nHundred millions:0.\n\nTen millions:9.\n\nMillions:7.\n\nHundred thousands:8.\n\nTen thousands:0.\n\nThousands:3.\n\nHundreds:6.\n\nTens:3.\n\nOnes:8</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
