the billions place. Wait, perhaps I messed up the order in my previous steps.\n\nWait, let me try adding again, maybe in a different way.\n\nLet me list each digit position step by step, starting from the right (ones place):.\n\nPosition: 1 (ones): 9 + 0 = 9.\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 1 +8=9.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):7 +0=7.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):5 +8=13 → write 3, carryover 1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):8 +2=10 +1=11 → write 1, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):1 +1=2 +1=3 → write 3, no carryover.\n\nPosition7 (millions):8 +8=16 → write6, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):2 +5=7 +1=8 → write8, no carryover.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):8 +5=13 → write3, carryover1.\n\nNow, since both numbers are 9 digits, the carryover1 would go to a new 10th digit (billions place). So the total is 1 followed by the digits we've written: 1 3 8 6 3 1 3 8 0? Wait, wait, let's see.\n\nWait, the digits we have from right to left (after each position):.\n\nThe digits written, starting from the first (ones) to the ninth (hundred millions), plus the carryover:.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to list the digits in order. Let's see:.\n\nAfter each step, the digits from right to left are:.\n\nPosition1:9.\n\nPosition2:9.\n\nPosition3:7.\n\nPosition4:3 (with carryover1).\n\nPosition5:1 (carryover1).\n\nPosition6:3 (no carryover).\n\nPosition7:6 (carryover1).\n\nPosition8:8 (no carryover).\n\nPosition9:3 (carryover1).\n\nThen the final carryover is 1.\n\nSo arranging all the digits from right to left, the digits are:.\n\nPosition1:9.\n\nPosition2:9.\n\nPosition3:7.\n\nPosition4:3.\n\nPosition5:1.\n\nPosition6:3.\n\nPosition7:6.\n\nPosition8:8.\n\nPosition9:3.\n\nPlus the final carryover 1 as the 10th digit (billions place).\n\nSo putting them all together from left to right (billions, hundred millions, etc.):.\n\nThe digits would be:.\n\n1 (billions), then 3 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 9 (ones). Wait, that doesn't seem right. Let me think again.\n\nWait, perhaps I should list the digits as:.\n\nStarting from the highest place (billions), which is the 1 from the carryover, then the hundred millions place is the 3 (from 8+5 + carryover?), no, let me see:.\n\nWait, the digits we computed:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\n1. Ones:9.\n\n2. Tens:9.\n\n3. Hundreds:7.\n\n4. Thousands:3 (with carryover1).\n\n5. Ten thousands:1 (with carryover1).\n\n6. Hundred thousands:3 (no carryover).\n\n7. Millions:6 (with carryover1).\n\n8. Ten millions:8 (no carryover).\n\n9. Hundred millions:3 (with carryover1).\n\nPlus the carryover1 to billions place.\n\nSo the digits from right to left (as written) are:.\n\n9 (ones),9 (tens),7 (hundreds),3 (thousands),1 (ten thousands),3 (hundred thousands),6 (millions),8 (ten millions),3 (hundred millions), then 1 (billions).\n\nTherefore, when written from left to right (billions first), it's 1,3 (hundred millions),8 (ten millions),6 (millions),3 (hundred thousands),1 (ten thousands),3 (thousands),7 (hundreds),9 (tens),9 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
