final_output:  ['1,386,313,799? Wait, but the user thought the correct answer was 1,316,313,799? Hmm, maybe I made a mistake here. Let me check again.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the millions place. Let me go through the steps again carefully.\n\nLet me write both numbers vertically aligned:\n\n         828,185,719\n       + 558,128,080\n       ----------------\n\nBreaking down each digit:\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):\n\n1. Ones: 9 + 0 = 9 → 9\n2. Tens: 1 + 8 = 9 → 9\n3. Hundreds:7 +0 =7 →7\n4. Thousands:5 +8 =13 → write 3, carryover 1\n5. Ten thousands:8 +2 =10 +1 (carryover) =11 → write 1, carryover 1\n6. Hundred thousands:1 +1 =2 +1 (carryover) =3 → write 3, no carryover\n7. Millions place:8 +8 =16 → write6, carryover1\n8. Ten millions place:2 +5 =7 +1 (carryover) =8 → write8, no carryover\n9. Hundred millions place:8 +5 =13 → write3, carryover1\n10. The final carryover1 becomes the billions place.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\n9 (ones), 9 (tens),7 (hundreds),3 (thousands),1 (ten thousands),3 (hundred thousands),6 (millions),8 (ten millions),3 (hundred millions),1 (billions).\n\nTherefore, when written from left to right, it\'s 1 (billions), 3 (hundred millions),8 (ten millions),6 (millions),3 (hundred thousands),1 (ten thousands),3 (thousands),7 (hundreds),9 (tens),9 (ones).\n\nSo that\'s 1,386,313,799. But the user said their answer is 1,316,313,799. Hmm, there\'s a discrepancy here. Let me check the millions place again.\n\nWait, in step 7: Millions place. The first number has 8 (millions place?), let me confirm the place values again.\n\nWait, the first number is 828,185,719. Let\'s break it down:\n\n- 8 (hundred millions)\n- 2 (ten millions)\n- 8 (millions)\n- 1 (hundred thousands)\n- 8 (ten thousands)\n- 5 (thousands)\n- 7 (hundreds)\n- 1 (tens)\n- 9 (ones)\n\nSecond number:558,128,080:\n\n-5 (hundred millions)\n-5 (ten millions)\n-8 (millions)\n-1 (hundred thousands)\n-2 (ten thousands)\n-8 (thousands)\n-0 (hundreds)\n-8 (tens)\n-0 (ones)\n\nSo when adding the millions place (third digit from the left in each number):\n\nFirst number\'s millions digit is 8 (from 828,185,719).\n\nSecond number\'s millions digit is 8 (from 558,128,080).\n\nSo 8 +8 =16. So that\'s correct, which gives 6 in millions place with carryover 1. Then the ten millions place:\n\nFirst number\'s ten millions digit is 2, second is 5. 2+5=7 plus carryover1 makes 8. So ten millions place is 8, which is correct.\n\nHundred millions place: first number 8, second 5 →13, so 3 with carryover1. That\'s right.\n\nSo the result is 1,386,313,799. But the user\'s answer is 1,316,313,799. That\'s different in the ten millions place. Wait, maybe I messed up which digit is which.\n\nWait, perhaps the problem is in the millions and ten millions places. Let me recount:\n\nWait, let\'s list all the digits with their place values properly:\n\nFirst number: 828,185,719\n\nDigits from right (ones) to left (hundred millions):\n\nPosition 1:9 (ones)\n\n2:1 (tens)\n\n3:7 (hundreds)\n\n4:5 (thousands)\n\n5:8 (ten thousands)\n\n6:1 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:8 (millions)\n\n8:2 (ten millions)\n\n9:8 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number:558,128,080\n\nDigits from right to left:\n\n1:0 (ones)\n\n2:8 (tens)\n\n3:0 (hundreds)\n\n4:8 (thousands)\n\n5:2 (ten thousands)\n\n6:1 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:8 (millions)\n\n8:5 (ten millions)\n\n9:5 (hundred millions)\n\nWait, that might have been my mistake earlier. Wait, the second number is 558,128,080. Let\'s parse it properly:\n\nBreaking down 558,128,080:\n\n- 5 (hundred millions place)\n\n- 5 (ten millions)\n\n- 8 (millions)\n\n- 1 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 2 (ten thousands)\n\n- 8 (thousands)\n\n- 0 (hundreds)\n\n- 8 (tens)\n\n- 0 (ones)\n\nSo the digits from left to right are:\n\nHundred millions:5, ten millions:5, millions:8, hundred thousands:1, ten thousands:2, thousands:8, hundreds:0, tens:8, ones:0.\n\nTherefore, when adding the millions place (position7 from the right):\n\nFirst number\'s millions digit (position7 from the right is millions place): 8.\n\nSecond number\'s millions digit:8 (since millions is the 7th digit from the right). So 8+8=16, correct. So that\'s 6 with carryover1 to the ten millions.\n\nThen ten millions place (position8 from the right):\n\nFirst number\'s ten millions digit is 2 (position8 from the right).\n\nSecond number\'s ten millions digit is5 (position8 from the right).\n\n2+5=7 plus the carryover1 gives 8. So ten millions digit is8, correct.\n\nHundred millions place (position9 from the right):\n\nFirst number\'s hundred millions digit is8, second is5 →13. So 3 with carryover1.\n\nTherefore, the digits are as before. So the sum is 1,386,313,799. But the user\'s answer is 1,316,313,799. Hmm, where is the difference?\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the hundred thousands place?\n\nLet me check again:\n\nHundred thousands place (position6 from the right):\n\nFirst number:1 (since the breakdown was first number\'s hundred thousands is1? Wait let\'s see:\n\nFirst number breakdown:\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands): 1? Let me confirm:\n\nFirst number 828,185,719:\n\nBreaking down each digit from right (position1):\n\nPosition1:9 (ones)\n\n2:1 (tens)\n\n3:7 (hundreds)\n\n4:5 (thousands)\n\n5:8 (ten thousands)\n\n6:1 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:8 (millions)\n\n8:2 (ten millions)\n\n9:8 (hundred millions)\n\nSo yes, position6 (hundred thousands) is 1 for first number.\n\nSecond number\'s hundred thousands digit (position6 from the right):1 (since 558,128,080\'s hundred thousands digit is1).\n\nSo adding 1 +1 =2 plus the carryover from previous step (position5 was ten thousands place where we had a carryover1? Wait, let\'s see step5.\n\nStep5 was ten thousands place (position5):\n\nFirst number\'s ten thousands digit is8 (position5), second\'s is2 (position5).\n\n8+2=10 plus carryover from thousands place (position4\'s carryover1). So total 11. So the ten thousands place becomes1 with carryover1 to the next (hundred thousands place).\n\nWait,