r 1 (from tens place) =14. So the carryover to thousands place is 1. So thousands place: 6 (first) +2 (second) +1 (carryover) =9. So thousands digit is 9, correct. So that part is right.\n\nSo where does the user get 6 in the thousands place? Wait in their answer, 1,150,919,461. Let's parse that number:\n\nBreaking down 1,150,919,461:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:1\n\nTens:6\n\nHundreds:4\n\nThousands:9\n\nTen thousands:1\n\nHundred thousands:1\n\nMillions:9\n\nTen millions:5\n\nHundred millions:0\n\nWait no, commas are different. Let me separate each digit properly.\n\nThe number is 1,150,919,461. Let's count the digits:\n\n1 (billions), 1 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 1 (ones). Wait that would make it 10 digits? Wait no, commas are placed every three digits starting from the right. So 1,150,919,461 is actually:\n\n1 (billions place?), but let me see:\n\n1,150,919,461:\n\nBreakdown:\n\n- The first comma after three digits from the right: 461 is the last three (hundreds, tens, ones).\n\nThen next three: 919 (thousands, ten thousands, hundred thousands? Wait commas are in the standard way: \n\nActually, in the US numbering system, commas separate every three digits starting from the right. So 1,150,919,461 would be:\n\n1,150,919,461 \u2192 1 (billions), 150 (millions), 919 (thousands), 461 (units). Wait, that's not standard. Wait, actually, commas are placed as follows for numbers over a billion:\n\n1,150,919,461 is 1 billion, 150 million, 919 thousand, 461. So breaking down:\n\nDigits:\n\n1 (billions place),\n\n1 (hundred millions),\n\n5 (ten millions),\n\n0 (millions),\n\n9 (hundred thousands),\n\n1 (ten thousands),\n\n9 (thousands),\n\n4 (hundreds),\n\n6 (tens),\n\n1 (ones).\n\nSo the thousands place is 9 (from the 9 in the thousands place). Wait, the thousands digit is the fourth from the right, which is 9 in the user's answer. That matches our calculatio