at 1 becomes an additional digit at the beginning.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 0.\n\nTens: 5.\n\nHundreds: 8.\n\nThousands: 2.\n\nTen-thousands: 4.\n\nHundred-thousands:7.\n\nMillions:8.\n\nTen-millions:8.\n\nHundred-millions:3 with a carryover 1 to the next (billions) place.\n\nSo the total would be 1 followed by 3, then the rest. Let me check the digits again step by step to ensure I didn't misalign anything.\n\nWait, maybe I should write the entire addition step by step to confirm:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nFirst digit (ones):.\n\n5 +5 =10. 0, carryover 1.\n\nTens: 8+6=14 +1=15 →5, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:2+5=7+1=8 →8, no carryover.\n\nThousands:6+6=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nTen-thousands:6+7=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:8+8=16 +1=17 →7, carryover1.\n\nMillions:3+4=7 +1=8 →8, no carryover.\n\nTen millions:2+6=8 →8.\n\nHundred millions:8+5=13 →3, carryover1.\n\nBillions place: The carryover 1. Since both numbers are 9-digit, adding gives a 10-digit number</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
