ryover 1 from position6 gives 15 \u2192 so that's 5 in millions place and carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\nNumber1's ten millions digit is 7, number2's is5. 7+5=12, plus carryover1 is 13 \u2192 3 in ten millions, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\nNumber1's hundred millions is7, number2's is5 \u2192 12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nSo the digits so far up to hundred millions are 3 (hundred millions), then the carryover1 to billions. So the number is:\n\nBillions:1, hundred millions:3, ten millions:3, millions:5, etc. So 1,335,210,582.\n\nBut user has 1,338,210,582. So the difference is in the ten millions place? Wait in the user's answer, the ten millions digit is 3 (since 1,338,210,582 \u2192 breakdown:\n\n1,338,210,582 \u2192 the digits are:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:3,\n\nTen millions:3,\n\nMillions:8,\n\nWait, no. Wait let's parse 1,338,210,582:\n\nBreaking into groups:\n\n1,338,210,582 \u2192 the commas are after every three digits starting from the right. So:\n\n1 (billions), 338 (millions), 210 (thousands), 582 (units).\n\nSo the millions part is 338, meaning hundred millions digit is 3, ten millions is 3, millions is 8.\n\nBut according to my calculation, the millions digit was 5. So that's where the problem is. So where did I make the mistake?\n\nWait let's re-express the digits again:\n\nLet me write both numbers digit by digit:\n\nFirst number: 777,475,107\n\nDigits from left to right (hundred millions to units):\n\nPosition9:7 (hundred millions),\n\nPosition8:7 (ten millions),\n\nPosition7:7 (millions),\n\nPosition6:4 (hundred thousands),\n\nPosition5:7 (ten thousands),\n\nPosition4:5 (thousands),\n\nPosition3:1 (hundreds),\n\nPosition2:0 (tens),\n\nPosition1:7 (units).\n\nSecond number:557,735,475\n\nDigits:\n\nPosition9:5 (hundred millions),\n\nPosition8:5 (ten millions),\n\nPosition7:7 (millions),\n\nPosition6:7 (hundred thousands),\n\nPosition5:3 (ten thousands),\n\nPosition4:5 (thousands),\n\nPosition3:4 (hundreds),\