\n\n9 (hundred millions):6.\n\nSecond number: 642,544,369.\n\nPositions:.\n\n1 (ones):9.\n\n2 (tens):6.\n\n3 (hundreds):3.\n\n4 (thousands):4.\n\n5 (ten thousands):4.\n\n6 (hundred thousands):5.\n\n7 (millions):2.\n\n8 (ten millions):4.\n\n9 (hundred millions):6.\n\nSo when adding:.\n\nLet me write each digit's place step by step:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones digit):.\n\nDigit 1 (ones): 5 + 9 = 14 → write 4, carryover 1.\n\nDigit 2 (tens): 2 + 6 = 8 + 1 (carryover) = 9 → write 9, no carryover.\n\nDigit 3 (hundreds): 9 + 3 = 12 → write 2, carryover 1.\n\nDigit 4 (thousands):5 +4 =9 +1 (carryover) =10 → write 0, carryover 1.\n\nDigit 5 (ten thousands):8 +4 =12 +1 =13 → write 3, carryover 1.\n\nDigit 6 (hundred thousands):1 +5 =6 +1 =7 → write 7, no carryover.\n\nDigit 7 (millions):6 +2 =8 → write 8, no carryover.\n\nDigit 8 (ten millions):3 +4 =7 → write7, no carryover.\n\nDigit 9 (hundred millions):6 +6 =12 → write 2 and carryover 1, but since there are no more digits, that carryover becomes a new digit at the beginning.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, when adding the hundred millions place (digit 9), 6 +6=12, so we write 2 and carryover 1. But since there are no more digits to the left, that carried over 1 has to be added as a new digit in the billions place. So the total sum will have a 1 in the billions place followed by the 2 from the hundred millions, etc.\n\nSo putting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nDigit 1:4.\n\nDigit2:9.\n\nDigit3:2.\n\nDigit4:0 (with carryover 1 to next).\n\nDigit5:3.\n\nDigit6:7.\n\nDigit7:8.\n\nDigit8:7.\n\nDigit9:2 (but with a carryover 1 to the next, which is the billions place).\n\nSo the digits from right to left (units to hundred millions) are 4,9,2,0,3,7,8,7,2, plus the carried over 1 in the billions place. .\n\nWait, let me reconstruct the number step by step:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units), each digit we calculated is:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):4.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):9.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):2.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):0.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):7.\n\nPosition7 (millions):8.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):7.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):2.\n\nPlus the carryover 1 in the billions place (position 10).\n\nSo the final number is:.\n\n1 (billions) 2 (hundred millions) 7 (ten millions) 8 (millions) 7 (hundred thousands) 3 (ten thousands) 0 (thousands) 2 (hundreds) 9 (tens) 4 (ones).\n\nWait, let me write that out:.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nHundred millions:2.\n\nTen millions:7.\n\nMillions:8.\n\nHundred thousands:7.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nThousands:0.\n\nHundreds:2.\n\nTens:9.\n\nOnes:4.\n\nWait, but when arranging the digits from highest to lowest place, it's:.\n\n1 (billions), 2 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 4 (ones).\n\nSo combining these digits:</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
