ons), 1 (millions). So when adding the hundred millions:5+7=12 plus carryover1 makes 13, so 3 with carryover1. That's correct.\n\nWait, the breakdown method must have an error. Let me see:\n\nWait, 591,931,376 is equal to 591 million plus 931,376. But 591 million is 591,000,000.\n\nBut 591,000,000 +744,000,000 =1,335,000,000. But the first number is actually 591,931,376 which is 591 million plus 931,376. So the total should be 591,931,376 +744,089,601 = (591,000,000 +744,000,000) + (931,376 +89,601). But that is 1,335,000,000 +1,020,977 =1,336,020,977.\n\nBut digit-wise addition gives 1,333,602,097. So there's a contradiction. Therefore I must have messed up in one of the calculations.\n\nWait let me do the addition step-by-step with the actual numbers:\n\n591,931,376\n\n+744,089,601\n\nLet me add them digit by digit:\n\nStart from the right:\n\n6 (units) +1 (units) =7 \u2192 units:7.\n\n7 (tens) +0 (tens)=7 \u2192 tens:7.\n\n3 (hundreds) +6 (hundreds)=9 \u2192 hundreds:9.\n\n1 (thousands) +9 (thousands)=10 \u2192 thousands:0, carryover1.\n\n3 (ten thousands) +8 (ten thousands)=11 +1=12 \u2192 ten thousands:2, carryover1.\n\n9 (hundred thousands) +0 (hundred thousands)=9 +1=10 \u2192 hundred thousands:0, carryover1.\n\n1 (millions) +4 (millions)=5 +1=6 \u2192 millions:6, carryover0.\n\n9 (ten millions) +4 (ten millions)=13 \u2192 ten millions:3, carryover1.\n\n5 (hundred millions) +7 (hundred millions)=12 +1=13 \u2192 hundred millions:3, carryover1.\n\nFinally, carryover1 \u2192 billions:1.\n\nSo the result is 1,333,602,097.\n\nAh, so the breakdown method must have an error. Let me check the breakdown again.\n\nWait in the breakdown method:\n\nFirst number:591,931,376 is split as 591,000,000 +931,376.\n\nSecond number:744,089,601 is 744,000,000 +89,601.\n\nAdding the millions parts:591,000,000 +744,000,000 =1,335,000,000.\n\nAdding the smaller parts:931,376 +89,601.\n\nWait, 931,376 +89,601:\n\nLet's compute that again:\n\n931,376\n\n+ 89,601\n\n=?\n\n931,376 +80,00