igit (millions place) should be6. But according to the digit-wise breakdown above, the digits are:\n\nPosition7 (millions):6,\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3,\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):3,\n\nPosition10 (billions):1.\n\nWait, so the number should be written as:\n\nPosition10 (billions):1,\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):3,\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3,\n\nPosition7 (millions):6,\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0,\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):2,\n\nPosition4 (thousands):0,\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9,\n\nPosition2 (tens):7,\n\nPosition1 (units):7.\n\nThus the number is 1 (billions),\n\n3 (hundred millions),\n\n3 (ten millions),\n\n6 (millions),\n\n0 (hundred thousands),\n\n2 (ten thousands),\n\n0 (thousands),\n\n9 (hundreds),\n\n7 (tens),\n\n7 (units).\n\nSo writing it out:1,333,602,097.\n\nBut according to the breakdown, it\'s1,336,020,977.\n\nThe difference is in the ten millions and millions places:\n\nDigit-wise:\n\nTen millions:3,\n\nMillions:6,\n\nBreakdown\'s result:\n\nTen millions:3,\n\nMillions:6,\n\nBut the breakdown\'s hundred millions is3, ten millions3, millions6.\n\nWait then why the difference in the overall number?\n\nWait let\'s write both numbers:\n\nDigit-wise:1,333,602,097 → 1 billion, 333 million, 602 thousand, 97.\n\nBreakdown\'s result:1,336,020,977 → 1 billion, 336 million, 20 thousand, 977.\n\nAh! The breakdown\'s result has an extra 3 million (336 vs 333). So the mistake is in the breakdown method.\n\nWait let me re-express the breakdown:\n\nFirst number:591,931,376.\n\nSecond number:744,089,601.\n\nAdding them:\n\n591,931,376 +744,089,601.\n\nLet me add the hundred millions:\n\n500,000,000 +700,000,000 =1,200,000,000.\n\nThen 90,000,000 +40,000,000 =130,000,000 → total 1,330,000,000.\n\nThen 1,000,000 +4,000,000 =5,000,000 → total 1,335,000,000.\n\nThen 900,000 +80,000 =980,000 → total 1,335,980,000.\n\n30,000 +9,000 =39,000 → total 1,336,019,000.\n\n1,000 +600 =1,600 → total 1,336,020,600.\n\n300 +0 =300 → 1,336,020,900.\n\n70 +1 =71 →