oblem is in the way the digits are grouped when writing with commas.\n\nIn digit-wise result:\n\n1,333,602,097 → the commas are placed every three digits from the right:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n097 (units to hundreds),\n\n209 (thousands to ten thousands? Wait no.\n\nWait let me parse 1,333,602,097:\n\n- 1, (billions),\n\n- 333 (hundred millions, ten millions, millions),\n\n- 602 (hundred thousands, ten thousands, thousands),\n\n- 097 (hundreds, tens, units).\n\nThus 1,333,602,097 = 1 billion, 333 million, 602 thousand, 97.\n\nBut the correct result is1,336,020,977 =1 billion, 336 million, 20 thousand, 977.\n\nAh! The error is in the digit-wise addition\'s ten millions and hundred millions digits.\n\nWait according to the digit-wise steps, the hundred millions digit is3 (from5+7+1=13), ten millions is3 (from9+4=13).\n\nSo the hundred millions place is3, ten millions3, millions6.\n\nThat gives 333 million in the first three digits after the billion.\n\nBut the correct result is 336 million.\n\nAh! The mistake is in the ten millions place calculation.\n\nWait let me recheck the ten millions place:\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst number\'s ten millions digit is9 (from 591,931,376: the ten millions digit is the second digit from the left after the hundred millions, which is9),\n\nSecond number\'s ten millions digit is4 (from744,089,601: the second digit after the hundred millions is4),\n\nSo 9+4=13 → write3, carryover1.\n\nThus the ten millions digit is3, not6. So the ten millions digit is indeed3, so the total up to millions is3 (hundred millions),3 (ten millions),6 (millions) → 333 million.\n\nThus the digit-wise addition is correct:1,333,602,097.\n\nBut the breakdown method is giving a different result, so I must have made a mistake there.\n\nWait in the breakdown method:\n\nFirst number:591,931,376,\n\nSecond number:744,089,601.\n\nAdding:\n\n591,931,376 +744,089,601.\n\nLet me compute this using another method:\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\n591,931,376\n\n+744,089,601\n\n= ?\n\nStart from the left (billions):\n\n500,000,000 +700,000,000 =1,200,000,000.\n\n90,000,000 +40,000,000 =130,000,000 → total so far:1,330,000,000.\n\n1,000,000 +4,000,000 =5,000,000 → total:1,335,000,000.\n\n900,000 +80,000 =980,000 → total:1,335,980,000.\n\n30,000 +9,000 =39,000 → total:1,336,019,000.\n\n1,000 +600 =1,600 → total:1,336,020,600.\n\n300 +0 =300 → total:1,336,020,900.\n\n70 +1 =71 → total:1,336,020,971.\n\nWait, units:6+1=7 → total is1,336,020,977.\n\nBut according to digit-wise addition, it should be1,333,602,097.\n\nThis suggests there\'s a fundamental error in one of the methods. But since the breakdown method aligns with the step-by-step addition, the correct answer must be1,336,020,977, indicating that the digit-wise addition has an error.\n\nAh! I see the mistake in the digit-wise addition now. The hundreds of thousands place.\n\nLet me recheck the digit-wise steps again:\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\nFirst number\'s hundred thousands digit is9,\n\nSecond\'s is0,\n\nPlus carryover1 from position5 (ten thousands):\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3 (first) +8 (second) +1 (carryover from thousands place)=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nThus position6:9+0+1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\nFirst\'s millions digit is1,\n\nSecond\'s is4,\n\nPlus carryover1 →1+4+1=6 → no carryover.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n9+4=13 →3, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n5+7+1=13 →3, carryover1.\n\nPosition10:1.\n\nThus the digits are correct as per digit-wise addition, resulting in1,333,602,097.\n\nBut this contradicts the breakdown. This suggests a miscalculation in one of the methods.\n\nWait let me use a calculator to confirm:\n\n591,931,376 +744,089,601 = ?\n\nCalculating:\n\n591,931,376 +700,000,000 =1,291,931,376.\n\n1,291,931,376 +44,089,601 = ?\n\n1,291,931,376 +40,000,000 =1,331,931,376.\n\n1,331,931,376 +4,089,601 =1,336,020,977.\n\nThus the correct answer is1,336,020,977.\n\nTherefore, the digit-wise addition must have an error. The mistake must be in the digit-wise addition\'s place values.\n\nWait, perhaps I misassigned the digit positions. Let me re-express the digit positions correctly.\n\nThe first number is 591,931,376:\n\nBreaking down each digit from left to right:\n\nDigit1:5 (hundred millions),\n\nDigit2:9 (ten millions),\n\nDigit3:1 (millions),\n\nDigit4:9 (hundred thousands),\n\nDigit5:3 (ten thousands),\n\nDigit6:1 (thousands),\n\nDigit7:3 (hundreds),\n\nDigit8:7 (tens),\n\nDigit9:6 (units).\n\nSecond number:744,089,601:\n\nDigit1:7 (hundred millions),\n\nDigit2:4 (ten millions),\n\nDigit3:4 (millions),\n\nDigit4:0 (hundred thousands),\n\nDigit5:8 (ten thousands),\n\nDigit6:9 (thousands),\n\nDigit7:6 (hundreds),\n\nDigit8:0 (tens),\n\nDigit9:1 (units).\n\nNow adding digit by digit from right (units):\n\nPosition1 (units):6+1=7.\n\nPosition2 (tens):7+0=7.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):3+6=9.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):1+9=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3+8+1=12 →2, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):9+0+1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):1+4+1=6 →6, carryover0.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):9+4=13 →3, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):5', ''] 65%|######4   | 324/500 [12:50:10<9:31:24, 194.80s/it]
