\n\nHmm. Now there\'s a conflict between the two methods. Which is correct?\n\nLet me do the addition step by step with actual numbers:\n\nFirst number:874,473,157\n\nSecond number:775,664,967\n\nAdding them:\n\nStart with 874,473,157 + 700,000,000 = 1,574,473,157\n\nThen add the remaining 75,664,967:\n\n1,574,473,157 +75,664,967.\n\nBreakdown:\n\n1,574,473,157 +70,000,000 = 1,644,473,157\n\nAdd 5,664,967:\n\n1,644,473,157 +5,664,967 = 1,650,138,124.\n\nWait so this gives 1,650,138,124. But the digit-by-digit method gave 1,650,318,124. So discrepancy in the hundred thousands and ten thousands places?\n\nWait let\'s check the digit-by-digit again:\n\nLooking at the ten thousands place: first number\'s ten thousands digit is 7 (from 874,473,157: the fifth digit from the left is 7?), let\'s see:\n\nFirst number: digits are 8 (1),7 (2),4 (3),4 (4),7 (5),3 (6),1 (7),5 (8),7 (9).\n\nYes, the fifth digit from the left (ten thousands place) is 7.\n\nSecond number\'s ten thousands digit: the fifth digit in 775,664,967 is 6 (since digits are 7,7,5,6,6,4,9,6,7). So fifth digit is 6.\n\nThus 7 +6 =13 →3 with carryover1. So that part is correct.\n\nHundred thousands place: first number\'s hundred thousands digit is the fourth digit from left: 4 (from 874,473,157), second\'s hundred thousands is the fourth digit:6 (since the second number\'s digits are 7,7,5,6,... so fourth digit is6). So 4+6=10 +1 (carryover)=11 →1, carryover1. Correct.\n\nMillions place: first\'s millions digit (third digit from left):4, second\'s third digit:5. So 4+5 +1 (carryover)=10 →0, carryover1. So millions digit is 0. So that\'s correct.\n\nTen millions place: first\'s second digit:7, second\'s second digit:7. So 7+7 +1 (carryover)=15 →5, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions place: first\'s first digit:8, second\'s first digit:7. 8+7 +1=16 →6, carryover1. So the digits so far:\n\nBillions place:1, hundred millions:6, ten millions:5, millions:0, hundred thousands:1, ten thousands:3, thousands:8, hundreds:1, t