error caused the discrepancy. Let me retrace the entire addition correctly now.\n\nStarting over with correct digits:\n\n1. Ones place: 7+1=8. Write 8, no carryover.\n\n2. Tens place:1 (from first number) +9 (second)=10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\n3. Hundreds place:1 (first) +4 (second)=5 + carryover 1 =6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\n4. Thousands place:6 (first) +2 (second)=8. Write 8, no carryover.\n\n5. Ten thousands place:6 (first) +6 (second)=12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\n6. Hundred thousands place:3 (first) +2 (second)=5 +1 (carryover)=6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\n7. Millions place:8 (first) +7 (second)=15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n\n8. Ten millions place:0 (first) +3 (second)=3 +1 (carryover)=4. Write 4, no carryover.\n\n9. Hundred millions place:8 (first) +8 (second)=16. Write 6, carryover 1.\n\n10. Billions place:0+0 +1 (carryover)=1. Write 1.\n\nSo putting all digits together from right to left:\n\nOnes:8\n\nTens:0\n\nHundreds:6\n\nThousands:8\n\nTen thousands:2\n\nHundred thousands:6\n\nMillions:5\n\nTen millions:4\n\nHundred millions:6\n\nBillions:1\n\nSo the number is 1,645,628,608. \n\nAh, so the correct sum is 1,645,628,608. Which matches the second method's result. The mistake in the first calculation was incorrectly taking the hundreds digit of the first number as 6 instead of 1. That error propagated through the rest. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be 1,645,628,608. The user thought it was 1,645,628,668? Wait, the user's answer is written as 1645628668. Let me check the digits of their answer:\n\n1,645,628,668. Wait, the user's answer is 1645628668 which would be 1,645,628,668. But according to the correct calculation, it's 1,645,628,608. So there's a discrepancy in the last two digits? Wait, let's see:\n\nWait in the thousands place, when I re-did the calculation, it was 8 (thousands digit). Let's check that again.\n\nThousands place: first number's thousands digit is 6 (from 808,366,117: the thousands digit is the fourth from the right, which i