o clarify each digit's position properly.\n\nWait, let me write the numbers digit by digit:.\n\nFirst number: 7 3 0 0 6 4 0 5 3.\n\nSecond number:5 5 2 0 5 9 9 0 1.\n\nWait, actually, let's count the digits. Both numbers are 9 digits long.\n\nFirst number: 7 (hundred millions?), let me see:.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to write each digit's place step by step.\n\nLet me list each digit from right to left with their positions:.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 3 +1 =4.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):5 +0=5.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):0 +9=9.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):4 +9=13 → 3 with carryover 1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6 +5 +1 (carryover) =12 → 2 with carryover 1.\n\nWait, let me check again:.\n\nWait, the first number is 730,064,053. Breaking down each digit from right:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nDigit 1 (ones): 3.\n\nDigit 2 (tens):5.\n\nDigit3 (hundreds):0.\n\nDigit4 (thousands):4.\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):6.\n\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):0.\n\nDigit7 (millions):3.\n\nDigit8 (ten millions):0.\n\nDigit9 (hundred millions):7.\n\nWait, maybe I need to write them out digit by digit:.\n\nFirst number: 7 3 0 0 6 4 0 5 3.\n\nSecond number:5 5 2 0 5 9 9 0 1.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the digit breakdown. Let me write both numbers digit by digit, starting from the right (units place):.\n\nFirst number: 730,064,053.\n\nBreaking down each digit from right (position 1 to 9):.\n\nPosition 1: 3 (units).\n\nPosition 2: 5 (tens).\n\nPosition3:0 (hundreds).\n\nPosition4:4 (thousands).\n\nPosition5:6 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition6:0 (hundred thousands).\n\nPosition7:0 (millions? Wait, no, let's see:.\n\nWait, the number is 730,064,053. Let's parse it properly:.\n\nThe number is 730,064,053. So commas are placed every three digits. So:.\n\n730,064,053 is 7 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 3 (units). Wait, no, perhaps:.\n\nWait, 730,064,053:.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\n- The first comma from