rs with their digits clearly:\n\nFirst number: 314,229,068.\n\nBreaking down each digit from left (hundred millions) to right (ones):\n\n1. Hundred millions: 3\n\n2. Ten millions:1\n\n3. Millions:4\n\n4. Hundred thousands:2\n\n5. Ten thousands:2\n\n6. Thousands:9\n\n7. Hundreds:0\n\n8. Tens:6\n\n9. Ones:8\n\nSecond number:679,409,818.\n\nDigits:\n\n1. Hundred millions:6\n\n2. Ten millions:7\n\n3. Millions:9\n\n4. Hundred thousands:4\n\n5. Ten thousands:0\n\n6. Thousands:9\n\n7. Hundreds:8\n\n8. Tens:1\n\n9. Ones:8\n\nNow adding each column starting from the right (ones):\n\nOnes:8+8=16 →6, carryover1.\n\nTens:6 (first) +1 (second) +1 (carry)=8, no carryover.\n\nHundreds:0 +8=8, no carryover.\n\nThousands:9 (first) +9 (second)=18 →8, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:2 (first) +0 (second) +1 (carry)=3, no carryover.\n\nHundred thousands:2 +4=6, no carryover.\n\nMillions:4 (first) +9 (second)=13 →3, carryover1.\n\nTen millions:1 (first) +7 (second) +1 (carry)=9, no carryover.\n\nHundred millions:3 +6=9.\n\nSo putting all together, the digits from hundred millions to ones are:\n\n9 (hundred millions),9 (ten millions),3 (millions),6 (hundred thousands),3 (ten thousands),8 (thousands),8 (hundreds),8 (tens),6 (ones).\n\nSo the total is 993,638,886.\n\nTherefore the user\'s answer of 992,638,886 is incorrect because they probably made an error in the millions place. The correct answer should be 993,638,886.\n\nWait, but maybe I made a mistake in the digit positions. Let me try adding them another way, perhaps by separating the numbers into parts.\n\n314,229,068 + 679,409,818.\n\nLet me add the millions first:\n\n300,000,000 + 600,000,000 = 900,000,000.\n\nThen 14,000,000 +79,000,000 = 93,000,000. Wait, no. Wait, breaking down each number:\n\nFirst number: 300,000,000 + 14,000,000 + 229,068.\n\nSecond number: 600,000,000 + 79,000,000 + 409,818.\n\nAdding the millions parts:\n\n300M +600M=900M.\n\n14M +79M=93M.\n\nThen the rest: 229,068 +409,818.\n\n229,068 +409,818:\n\n229,068 +