o carryover.\n\n2. Position1 (tens):7+1=8. No carryover.\n\n3. Position2 (hundreds):8+2=10 \u2192 digit0, carry1.\n\n4. Position3 (thousands):0 (from first) +9 (from second) +1 (carry) =10 \u2192 digit0, carry1.\n\n5. Position4 (ten thousands):8 (first) +1 (second) +1 (carry) =10 \u2192 digit0, carry1.\n\n6. Position5 (hundred thousands):6 (first) +1 (second) +1 (carry) =8 \u2192 digit8, carry0.\n\n7. Position6 (millions):1 (first) +7 (second)=8 \u2192 digit8, carry0.\n\n8. Position7 (ten millions):2 (first) +6 (second)=8 \u2192 digit8, carry0.\n\n9. Position8 (hundred millions):7 (first) +9 (second)=16 \u2192 digit6, carry1.\n\n10. Position9 (billions): carry1 \u2192 digit1.\n\nSo the digits from position9 down to 0 are:\n\n1 (billions),\n\n6 (hundred millions),\n\n8 (ten millions),\n\n8 (millions),\n\n8 (hundred thousands),\n\n0 (ten thousands),\n\n0 (thousands),\n\n0 (hundreds),\n\n8 (tens),\n\n7 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 1,688,000,087.\n\nWait but the user's answer was 1,678,800,087. Hmm, so where did I go wrong?\n\nWait let me check the hundred thousands place again. Position5 is hundred thousands:\n\nFirst number's hundred thousands digit is 6 (from 721,680,873 \u2192 breakdown: 721,680,873 \u2192 the digits are 7 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 3 (ones).\n\nSecond number's hundred thousands digit is 1 (from 967,119,214 \u2192 breakdown: 9 (hundred millions),6 (ten millions),7 (millions),1 (hundred thousands),1 (ten thousands),9 (thousands),2 (hundreds),1 (tens),4 (ones).\n\nSo adding position5 (hundred thousands):\n\n6 (first) +1 (second) +1 (carry from ten thousands place) = 8. So that's correct. So the hundred thousands digit is 8, no carryover.\n\nPosition6 (millions): first number has 1 (millions digit), second has7 \u2192 1+7=8. Correct.\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):2+6=8. Correct.\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):7+9=16 \u21926 with carryover1. Correct.\n\nSo the total is 1,688,000,087. But the user's answer is 1,678,800,087. Hmm, maybe I made a mistake in the ten thousands place?\n\nWait position4 (ten thousands):\n\nFirst number's ten thousands digit is 8 (from 721,680,873 \u2192 the '8' in 680,873 is the ten thousands place?), let's see:\n\nWait the number is 721,680,873. Breaking down:\n\n- 721,680,873: the digits before the comma are millions. So 721 (million), then 680 (thousand), 873. Wait perhaps I messed up the place values.\n\nWait perhaps the confusion comes from the way the numbers are written. Let's parse each number correctly:\n\nFirst number: 721,680,873.\n\nBreaking into parts:\n\n- 721 million \u2192 721,000,000.\n\n- 680 thousand \u2192 680,000.\n\n- 873 \u2192 873.\n\nSo total is 721,680,873.\n\nBreaking down digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the left:\n\nDigit1 (billions place): 0 (since it's a 9-digit number),\n\nDigit2 (hundred millions):7,\n\nDigit3 (ten millions):2,\n\nDigit4 (millions):1,\n\nDigit5 (hundred thousands):6,\n\nDigit6 (ten thousands):8,\n\nDigit7 (thousands):0,\n\nDigit8 (hundreds):8,\n\nDigit9 (tens):7,\n\nDigit10 (ones):3.\n\nWait maybe I misassigned the digit positions earlier. Let's use another notation where each digit's place is clear.\n\nLet me write both numbers with their place values:\n\nFirst number: 721,680,873.\n\n- 7: hundred millions place (position8)\n\n- 2: ten millions (position7)\n\n- 1: millions (position6)\n\n- 6: hundred thousands (position5)\n\n- 8: ten thousands (position4)\n\n- 0: thousands (position3)\n\n- 8: hundreds (position2)\n\n- 7: tens (position1)\n\n- 3: ones (position0)\n\nSecond number:967,119,214.\n\n-9: hundred millions (position8)\n\n-6: ten millions (position7)\n\n-7: millions (position6)\n\n-1: hundred thousands (position5)\n\n-1: ten thousands (position4)\n\n-9: thousands (position3)\n\n-2: hundreds (position2)\n\n-1: tens (position1)\n\n-4: ones (position0)\n\nNow let's add each position from 0 to8, then handle the carryover to position