9+5=14 → 4, carryover1.\n\nPosition3:5 +3 =8 +1=9? Wait no: 5 (from first) +3 (second) =8, plus carryover 1 → 9. Wait, but wait, position3 is hundreds place. Wait, the first number's hundreds digit is 5, second's is 3. So 5+3=8 plus the carryover 1 makes 9? Wait, but in my previous calculation I thought it was 5+2. Maybe I made a mistake earlier.\n\nWait this is confusing. Let's do each step carefully:.\n\nStarting from the right (position1 to position9):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones):.\n\nFirst number:0, second:6 → total 6. Write 6, carryover 0.\n\nPosition2 (tens):.\n\nFirst:9, second:5 → 9+5=14. Write 4, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):.\n\nFirst:5, second:3 →5+3=8 +1 (carry) =9. So write 9, carryover0.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):.\n\nFirst:9, second:2 →9+2=11. Write 1, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):.\n\nFirst:0, second:1 →0+1=1 +1=2. Write 2, carryover0.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):.\n\nFirst:7, second:3 →7+3=10. Write 0, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\nFirst:3, second:6 →3+6=9 +1 (carry)=10. Write 0, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\nFirst:9, second:9 →9+9=18 +1 (carry)=19. Write 9, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\nFirst:8, second:3 →8+3=11 +1 (carry)=12. Write 2, carryover1.\n\nNow, since we've reached the highest digit (position9), the carryover1 becomes a new digit at the beginning. So the total sum is 1 followed by the digits we've written: 1 2 9 0 0 2 9 1 4 6?.\n\nWait, let me list all the written digits with their positions:.\n\nPositions 1-9:.\n\nPosition1:6.\n\nPosition2:4.\n\nPosition3:9.\n\nPosition4:1.\n\nPosition5:2.\n\nPosition6:0.\n\nPosition7:0.\n\nPosition8:9.\n\nPosition9:2.\n\nPlus the final carryover1.\n\nSo the digits from highest to lowest (position9 to position1):.\n\nCarryover1, then position9 is 2, position8 is9, position7 is0, position6 is0, position5 is2, position4 is1, position3 is9, position2 is4, position1 is6.\n\nSo putting them together: 1 (from carryover) then 2 (hundred millions place), then 9 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands?), wait let me see:.\n\nWait the places are:.\n\nThe final number will have 10 digits because of the carryover. Let's see:.\n\nThe positions after adding:.\n\nThe first digit (leftmost) is the 1 from the carryover, which is the billions place.\n\nThen:.\n\nBillions place:1.\n\nHundred millions:2 (from position9).\n\nTen millions:9 (position8).\n\nMillions:0 (position7).\n\nHundred thousands:0 (position6).\n\nTen thousands:2 (position5).\n\nThousands:1 (position4).\n\nHundreds:9 (position3).\n\nTens:4 (position2).\n\nOnes:6 (position1).\n\nSo putting all together:.\n\n1,2 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nWait, but let's write that as a number:.\n\n1,290,021,946?.\n\nWait let's see:.\n\nBreaking it down:.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nHundred millions:2 → so 1,2 (billion and hundred million).\n\nThen ten millions:9 → 1,290 (millions).\n\nMillions place:0 → 1,290,0 (hundred thousands).\n\nHundred thousands:0 → 1,290,00 (ten thousands).\n\nTen thousands:2 → 1,290,020 (thousands).\n\nThousands:1 → 1,290,021,000?.\n\nWait no, maybe I'm getting confused. Let me write each part:.\n\nThe digits are:.\n\n1 (billions place),.\n\n2 (hundred millions),.\n\n9 (ten millions),.\n\n0 (millions),.\n\n0 (hundred thousands),.\n\n2 (ten thousands),.\n\n1 (thousands),.\n\n9 (hundreds),.\n\n4 (tens),.\n\n6 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 1,290,021,946.\n\nWait, let me check with another method. Maybe adding the numbers using another approach.\n\nAlternatively, let's compute 839,709,590 + 396,133,256.\n\nLet me add 839,709,590 + 396,133,256.\n\nBreaking them into parts:.\n\n800,000,000 + 30,000,000 + 9,000,000 + 700,000 + 9,000 + 500 + 90 +0.\n\nplus.\n\n300,000,000 +90,000,000 +6,000,000 +10,000 +3,000 +300 +20 +5 +6?.\n\nWait maybe this is getting too complicated. Alternatively, let me use approximate values to check.\n\n839 millio