irst. Wait, actually, when adding, we start from the rightmost digit, so let's go step by step again, carefully:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (position 1):\n\nPosition 1 (ones):\n\n6 +1 =7 → write 7, carryover 0.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):\n\n0 +7 =7 → write 7, carryover 0.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):\n\n0 +1 =1 → write 1, carryover 0.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):\n\n8 +7 =15 → write 5, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):\n\n7 (from first number) +2 (second) =9, plus carryover 1 → 10 → write 0, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\n8 +2 =10, plus carryover1 →11 → write 1, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\n5 (first) +1 (second) =6, plus carryover1 →7 → write7, carryover0.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n9 (first) +7 (second) =16 → write6, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n4 (first) +2 (second) =6, plus carryover1 →7 → write7, carryover0.\n\nSo the final digits from right to left are:\n\nPosition1:7\n\nPosition2:7\n\nPosition3:1\n\nPosition4:5\n\nPosition5:0\n\nPosition6:1\n\nPosition7:7\n\nPosition8:6\n\nPosition9:7\n\nSo when written as a number, starting from the highest (position9) to the lowest (position1):\n\n7 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 1 (hundreds),7 (tens),7 (ones).\n\nWait, but let's see:\n\nWait, Position9 is hundred millions:7\n\nPosition8 is ten millions:6\n\nPosition7 is millions:7\n\nPosition6 is hundred thousands:1\n\nPosition5 is ten thousands:0\n\nPosition4 is thousands:5\n\nPosition3 is hundreds:1\n\nPosition2 is tens:7\n\nPosition1 is ones:7\n\nSo putting them together:\n\n7 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions),7 (millions),1 (hundred thousands),0 (ten thousands),5 (thousands),1 (hundreds),7 (tens),7 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 767,105,177.\n\nBut the user thought the answer was 737,105,177. Hmm, so there's a discrepancy here. Did I make a mistake?\n\nWait, let me check the millions place again. Let's see:\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\nFirst number's millions digit is 5 (since the first number is 495,878,006 → the millions place is the 5 in 495 million). The second number's millions digit is 1 (since 271,227,171 → 271 million, so the millions digit is 1). Adding those: 5+1=6, plus carryover from hundred thousands place (which was 1) → total 7. That seems right.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is 9 (from 495,878,006 → 495, so the 9 is ten millions). Second number's ten millions digit is 7 (from 271, so the 7 is ten millions). So 9 +7=16, so 6 in ten millions, carryover1. That's correct.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n4 +2 +1 (carryover) =7. Correct.\n\nHmm, so then the correct answer should be 767,105,177. But the user's answer is 737 million. Wait, maybe the user made a mistake, or maybe I messed up the digit positions?\n\nWait, let me check the original numbers again. The first number is 495,878,006. Let's parse this:\n\n495,878,006. Breaking down:\n\n- 4 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 5 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nSecond number is 271,227,171:\n\n- 2 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 1 (ones).\n\nWait, let me add them again column by column:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:6+1=7.\n\nTens:0+7=7.\n\nHundreds:0+1=1.\n\nThousands:8+7=15 →5, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:7+2=9 +1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:8+2=10 +1=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nMillions:5+1=6 +1=7 →7, no carryover.\n\nTen millions:9+7=16 →6, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:4+2=6 +1=7 →7.\n\nYes, so the sum is 767,105,177. Therefore the user's answer of 737,105,177 is incorrect. The correct answer is 767,105,177. So maybe there was a mistake in the user's thought.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made a mistake in the ten millions place. Let me see:\n\nTen millions place: first number has 9, second has7. 9+7