nPosition2:9 (tens).\n\nPosition3:8 (hundreds).\n\nPosition4:4 (thousands).\n\nPosition5:1 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition6:9 (hundred thousands).\n\nPosition7:1 (millions).\n\nPosition8:1 (ten millions).\n\nPosition9:3 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the digit positions. Let me confirm:.\n\nThe second number is 311,914,894. Let's split into digits:.\n\nDigit 1 (rightmost):4.\n\nDigit2:9.\n\nDigit3:8.\n\nDigit4:4.\n\nDigit5:1.\n\nDigit6:9.\n\nDigit7:1 (millions place).\n\nDigit8:1 (ten millions).\n\nDigit9:3 (hundred millions).\n\nSo when adding the millions place (position7):.\n\nFirst number's millions digit is 0 (since 670,109,857 has 6 (hundred million), 7 (ten million), 0 (million), then 1 (hundred thousand), etc.).\n\nSecond number's millions digit is 1 (from the 311,914,894, the millions place is the 7th digit from the right, which is 1).\n\nSo adding position7: 0 (first) +1 (second) + carryover1 from position6 (which was 10, so carryover1). So 0+1+1=2? Wait, position6 was hundred thousands place. Let me check again.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):.\n\nFirst number's hundred thousands digit is 1.\n\nSecond's hundred thousands digit is9.\n\nSo 1+9=10. So write 0, carryover1. That's correct. Then moving to position7 (millions):.\n\nFirst number's millions digit is0, second's is1. So 0+1 +1(carry) =2. So write 2, no carryover. Wait, but in my previous step I thought it was 9, but maybe I made a mistake here.\n\nWait, so perhaps I messed up the positions earlier. Let me redo all steps carefully.\n\nLet me write both numbers with their digits in order:.\n\nFirst number (670,109,857):.\n\nPositions (from right, 1 to 9):.\n\n1:7.\n\n2:5.\n\n3:8.\n\n4:9.\n\n5:0.\n\n6:1.\n\n7:0.\n\n8:7.\n\n9:6.\n\nSecond number (311,914,894):.\n\nPositions (from right,1 to9):.\n\n1:4.\n\n2:9.\n\n3:8.\n\n4:4.\n\n5:1.\n\n6:9.\n\n7:1.\n\n8:1.\n\n9:3.\n\nNow adding each position:.\n\nStarting at position1 (ones):.\n\n7 +4 =11 → 1, carryover1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):.\