igit by digit again.\n\nAlternatively, let me compute 878,962,317 + 143,379,661 numerically:\n\n878,962,317\n\n+143,379,661\n\n= ?\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\nStart from the right:\n\nOnes:7+1=8\n\nTens:1+6=7\n\nHundreds:3+6=9\n\nThousands:2+9=11, write 1, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands:6+7=13 +1=14, write4, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:9+3=12 +1=13, write3, carryover1.\n\nMillions:8+3=11 +1=12, write2, carryover1.\n\nTen millions:7+4=11 +1=12, write2, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:8+1=9 +1=10, write0, carryover1.\n\nThen the final carryover 1 becomes the billions place.\n\nSo the total is 1,022,341,978.\n\nBut the user\'s answer is 1,002,341,978. Hmm, discrepancy here. Let me check if I miscounted the digits.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the millions place addition.\n\nWait let\'s go through each digit step by step with their place values:\n\nLet me write both numbers with each digit\'s place:\n\nFirst number: 878,962,317\n\nDigits from right (position 1 to 9):\n\nPosition 1 (ones):7\n\n2:1 (tens)\n\n3:3 (hundreds)\n\n4:2 (thousands)\n\n5:6 (ten thousands)\n\n6:9 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:8 (millions)\n\n8:7 (ten millions)\n\n9:8 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number:143,379,661\n\nDigits:\n\nPosition1:1 (ones)\n\n2:6 (tens)\n\n3:6 (hundreds)\n\n4:9 (thousands)\n\n5:7 (ten thousands)\n\n6:3 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:3 (millions)\n\n8:4 (ten millions)\n\n9:1 (hundred millions)\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nPosition1:7+1=8\n\nPosition2:1+6=7\n\nPosition3:3+6=9\n\nPosition4:2+9=11 → 1, carryover1\n\nPosition5:6+7=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1\n\nPosition6:9+3=12 +1=13 →3, carryover1\n\nPosition7:8+3=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1\n\nPosition8:7+4=11 +1=12 →2, carryover1\n\nPosition9:8+1=9 +1=10 →0, carryover1\n\nThen the carryover1 adds a new digit at position10 (billions):1\n\nSo the result is:\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 9 (hundreds),7 (tens),8 (ones)\n\nWait, no, let\'s structure it properly. The digits from position1 to 10 are:\n\nPosition10:1\n\nPosition9:0\n\nPosition8:2\n\nPosition7:2\n\nPosition6:3\n\nPosition5:4\n\nPosition4:1 (wait no, let me see):\n\nWait, the result digits after each step are:\n\nPositions:\n\nAfter Position9, we had the 0, and carryover1 to the next (10th). So the digits from right to left are:\n\nPosition1:8\n\n2:7\n\n3:9\n\n4:1 (since we wrote 1 in thousands place)\n\n5:4 (ten thousands)\n\n6:3 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:2 (millions)\n\n8:2 (ten millions)\n\n9:0 (hundred millions)\n\n10:1 (billions)\n\nSo putting it all together from left to right (billions down to ones):\n\n1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 9 (hundreds),7 (tens),8 (ones). So that\'s 1,022,341,978.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be 1,022,341,978, but the user thought it was 1,002,341,978. Hmm, there\'s a difference in the ten millions place. Let me check again:\n\nWait in the ten millions place (position8):\n\nFirst number\'s ten millions digit is7, second\'s is4. 7+4=11, plus the carryover1 from millions place addition (which was 12, so carryover1). So 11+1=12. So that gives 2 in ten millions place and carryover1. So the ten millions digit is 2. So the ten millions place is indeed 2, so in the total, the digits are 1 (billions),0 (hundred millions),2 (ten millions), etc. So the ten millions is 2, so the number is 1,022... So the user\'s answer has 0 in the ten millions place, which would be wrong.\n\nWait maybe I miscalculated. Let me do the addition using another method. Let\'s add the two numbers digit by digit:\n\nFirst number: 878,962,317\n\nSecond number:143,379,661\n\nAdding them:\n\nStart from the right:\n\nOnes: 7 +1 =8\n\nTens:1 +6=7\n\nHundreds:3+6=9\n\nThousands:2+9=11. Write down 1, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:6+7=13 +1=14. Write 4, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:9+3=12 +1=13. Write 3, carryover1.\n\nMillions:8 (from first