+3=6. Wait, so that contradicts my initial thought where I thought second number had 8 in the ones. Wait that was a mistake.\n\nWait hold on, perhaps I made a mistake in the initial breakdown. Let me re-express both numbers digit by digit:.\n\nFirst number: 673,694,013.\n\nDigits from right (ones to hundred millions):.\n\nPosition: 1 (ones):3.\n\n2 (tens):1.\n\n3 (hundreds):0.\n\n4 (thousands):4.\n\n5 (ten thousands):9.\n\n6 (hundred thousands):6.\n\n7 (millions):3.\n\n8 (ten millions):7.\n\n9 (hundred millions):6.\n\nSecond number:222,745,983.\n\nDigits from right:.\n\nPosition1 (ones):3.\n\nPosition2 (tens):8.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):9.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):5.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):4.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):7.\n\nPosition7 (millions):2.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):2.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):2.\n\nAh, okay, so the second number's tens digit is 8, hundreds is 9, thousands is5, etc. So my initial calculation was correct except for the ones place.\n\nWait, so let me start over with correct digit breakdown.\n\nStarting from the ones place:.\n\nOnes place: 3 (first) + 3 (second) = 6. So write 6, no carryover.\n\nTens place: 1 (first) +8 (second) =9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\nHundreds place:0 (first) +9 (second)=9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\nThousands place:4 (first) +5 (second)=9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands place:9 (first) +4 (second)=13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands place:6 (first) +7 (second) +1 (carryover) =14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nMillions place:3 (first) +2 (second) +1 (carryover)=6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\nTen millions place:7 (first) +2 (second)=9. Write 9.\n\nHundred millions place:6 (first) +2 (second)=8. Write 8.\n\nPutting it all together, the sum would be:.\n\nStarting from the ones digit:.\n\n6 (ones), 9 (tens), 9 (hundreds), 9 (thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 4 (hundred thousands), 6 (millions), 9 (ten millions), 8 (hundred millions)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
