he original numbers again.\n\nWait the first number is 300,588,772.\n\nSecond is 440,091,769.\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet me try adding them in another way, maybe breaking down the numbers:\n\n300,588,772 + 440,091,769.\n\nLet me add 300,588,772 + 400,000,000 = 700,588,772.\n\nThen add the remaining 40,091,769.\n\n700,588,772 + 40,091,769 = ?\n\nAdding 700,588,772 + 40,000,000 = 740,588,772.\n\nThen add 91,769: 740,588,772 + 91,769 = ?\n\n740,588,772 + 90,000 = 740,678,772,\n\nthen +1,769 → 740,678,772 +1,769 = 740,680,541.\n\nAh! So that gives 740,680,541, which matches my previous calculation. But the user\'s answer was 740,380,541. So perhaps the user made a mistake? Or maybe I made an error in the digit positions.\n\nWait let me check the initial digit-by-digit addition again.\n\nWait let me do the addition in the standard vertical way:\n\nFirst number: 300,588,772\n\nSecond number:440,091,769\n\nAdding:\n\nLet\'s write them aligned:\n\n      300,588,772\n\n    +440,091,769\n\n    ---------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 2 +9 =11 → 1, carryover 1\n\nTens:7 +6 =13 +1=14 →4, carryover1\n\nHundreds:7 +7 =14 +1=15 →5, carryover1\n\nThousands:8 +1 =9 +1=10 →0, carryover1\n\nTen thousands:8 +9 =17 +1=18 →8, carryover1\n\nHundred thousands:5 +0=5 +1=6 →6, no carryover\n\nMillions:0 +4=4 →4 (Wait, original first number has 0 in millions place, second has 4?)\n\nWait wait, let me check the positions again. The first number is 300,588,772:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n3 (hundred millions),\n\n0 (ten millions),\n\n0 (millions),\n\n5 (hundred thousands),\n\n8 (ten thousands),\n\n8 (thousands),\n\n7 (hundreds),\n\n7 (tens),\n\n2 (ones).\n\nSecond number:440,091,769:\n\n4 (hundred millions),\n\n4 (ten millions),\n\n0 (millions),\n\n0 (hundred thousands),\n\n9 (ten thousands),\n\n1 (thousands),\n\n7 (hundreds),\n\n6 (tens),\n\n9 (ones).\n\nSo when adding the millions place (third digit from the left):\n\nFirst number\'s millions digit is 0,\n\nSecond number\'s millions digit is