ecomes the new digit at the billions place.\n\nSo the digits from billions down are:\n\n1 (billions), 3 (hundred millions),5 (ten millions),8 (millions),1 (hundred thousands),2 (ten thousands),5 (thousands),4 (hundreds),2 (tens),3 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 1,358,125,423. But the user\'s answer is 1,358,125,473. Hmm, so there\'s a difference in the thousands place. Wait in my calculation, the thousands place was 5 (from 7+8+carryover?), but let me check again.\n\nWait, position6 (thousands place):\n\nFirst number:7 (thousands digit)  \nSecond number:8 (thousands digit)  \n7+8=15. So digit is5, carryover1 to ten thousands place. That\'s correct. Then ten thousands place is 8 (first) +3 (second) +1=12, so 2 with carryover1. That\'s right. Hundred thousands place:1+9+1=11 →1 with carryover1. Millions place:8+9+1=18 →8 and carryover1. Ten millions:7+7+1=15 →5 and carryover1. Hundred millions:6+6+1=13 →3 and carryover1. So the thousands digit is indeed 5. So the total is 1,358,125,423. But user says 1,358,125,473. Where is the 7 coming from?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the hundreds place? Let me check again:\n\nPosition7 (hundreds): first number has 2, second has2 → 4. Correct.\n\nPosition6 (thousands):5, as above.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):2.\n\nPosition4 (hundred thousands):1.\n\nPosition3 (millions):8.\n\nPosition2 (ten millions):5.\n\nPosition1 (hundred millions):3.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nSo putting all together:\n\nBillions:1  \nHundred millions:3  \nTen millions:5  \nMillions:8  \nHundred thousands:1  \nTen thousands:2  \nThousands:5  \nHundreds:4  \nTens:2  \nOnes:3  \n\nSo written as 1,358,125,423.\n\nWait, so the user\'s answer has 1,358,125,473 which differs in the thousands and hundreds place? Wait, let me see:\n\nWait the user\'s answer is 1,358,125,473. Let\'s parse that:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n1 (billion), 3 (hundred million),5 (ten million),8 (million),1 (hundred thousand),2 (ten thousand),5 (thousand),7 (hundred),4 (ten?), wait no. Wait 473 at the end