en thousands),5 (thousands), 1 (hundreds),0 (tens),3 (ones).\n\nWait that's not correct. Wait 545,103:\n\nDigits from right:\n\nOnes:3,\n\nTens:0,\n\nHundreds:1,\n\nThousands:5,\n\nTen thousands:4,\n\nHundred thousands:5.\n\nSo hundreds place is 1, but in the digit-by-digit method, the hundreds place was 7. That's a conflict.\n\nAh! Here's the problem. The digit-by-digit method must have an error in the hundreds place.\n\nWait let me recalculate the digit-by-digit addition step-by-step again carefully:\n\nStarting over with positions from right (1 to 9):\n\nPosition1 (ones):6+7=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):9 (first number's tens digit) +4 (second's tens digit?) Wait, the second number's tens digit is position2 from the right, which is 4 (since second number's breakdown from right is position2:4). So 9+4=13 +1 (carryover)=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds): first number's hundreds digit is 6 (position3 from right), second's hundreds digit is 0 (position3 from right in second number is 0). So 6 +0 +1 (carryover) =7 \u21927, no carryover.\n\nPosition4 (thousands): first's thousands digit is4, second's thousands digit is0 (position4 from right for second number is 0). So 4+0=4 \u21924, no carryover.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands): first's ten thousands is4, second's ten thousands is0 \u21924+0=4 \u21924.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands): first's is1, second's is4 \u21921+4=5 \u21925.\n\nPosition7 (millions): first's is6, second's is4 \u21926+4=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions): first's is9, second's is9 \u21929+9+1 (carryover)=19 \u21929, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions): first's is5, second's is9 \u21925+9+1=15 \u21925, carryover1.\n\nThen the carryover1 becomes the next digit (billions).\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\nPosition1:3,\n\n2:4,\n\n3:7,\n\n4:4,\n\n5:4,\n\n6:5,\n\n7:0,\n\n8:9,\n\n9:5,\n\nthen 1.\n\nSo arranging from left to right (billions first):\n\n1 (billions),\n\n5 (hundred millions),\n\n9 (ten millions),\n\n0 (millions),\n\n5 (hundred thousands),\n\n4 (ten thousands),\n\n4 (thousands),\n\n7 (hundreds),\n\n4 (tens),\n\n3 (ones).\n\nThus the number is 1,590,544,743.\n\nBut the breakdown method gave 1,590,545,103. Where is this discrepancy?\n\nAh, I see the problem now. In the breakdown method, I incorrectly added 144,696 + 400,407 as 545,103, but actually:\n\nLet me recalculate that addition:\n\n144,696 +400,407.\n\nStart from the right:\n\nOnes:6+7=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nTens:9+0+1=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:6+4=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nThousands:4 (from first number) +0 (second) +1=5 \u21925.\n\nTen thousands:4 (from first) +0 (second) =4.\n\nHundred thousands:1 (first) +4 (second) =5.\n\nSo the sum is 545,103. That's correct. So total is 1,590,000,000 +545,103 =1,590,545,103.\n\nBut digit-by-digit got 1,590,544,743. The difference is in the hundreds and thousands place. Wait, let's see:\n\nIn digit-by-digit, the thousands place was 4 (from first number's 4 + second's 0). But in the breakdown method, the thousands place in the small parts addition is 5 (from 400,407's thousands digit is 0, so 4 (first number's thousands digit) +0 (second) plus carryover? Wait, in the small parts addition (144,696 +400,407):\n\nWait the thousands digit in 144,696 is 4 (since it's 144 thousand... so thousands digit is the fourth digit from the right). Let me write them out:\n\n144,696 is:\n\n1 (hundred thousands),4 (ten thousands),4 (thousands),6 (hundreds),9 (tens),6 (ones).\n\n400,407 is:\n\n4 (hundred thousands),0 (ten thousands),0 (thousands),4 (hundreds),0 (tens),7 (ones).\n\nAdding the thousands place:4 (from first) +0 (second)=4, but in the breakdown addition, the thousands place became 5 because there was a carryover from hundreds place.\n\nWait in the breakdown addition:\n\nWhen adding the hundreds place (6+4=10, so 10 + carryover from tens place? Wait let me re-express the small parts addition properly:\n\nAdding 144,696 and 400,407 step by step:\n\n          144,696\n        + 400,407\n        -----------\nOnes:6+7=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nTens:9 +0 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:6 +4 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nThousands:4 +0 +1=5 \u21925, carryover0.\n\nTen thousands:4 +0=4 \u21924.\n\nHundred thousands:1 +4=5 \u21925.\n\nSo the result is 545,103. The thousands digit here is 5 (from 4+0 + carryover1). However, in the digit-by-digit overall addition, the thousands place (position4) was 4 (from first number's 4 and second's 0, plus no carryover from hundreds place? Wait in the digit-by-digit addition for the entire problem, the thousands place (position4) was 4 (first number's 4 + second's 0) with no carryover, so that would be 4, but in the breakdown method, the thousands place in the smaller addition is 5 because of a carryover from hundreds.\n\nAh, here's the key difference. The digit-by-digit addition for the entire problem must have a different carryover because the thousands place in the full addition includes all parts, not just the smaller components.\n\nWait, actually, in the full digit-by-digit addition, the thousands place (position4) is first's 4 + second's 0 =4, and there was no carryover from the hundreds place (position3 had 7 with no carryover). So in the full addition, the thousands digit is 4, but in the breakdown method, the thousands digit in the small part was 5. This inconsistency shows that the breakdown method might have an error.\n\nWait, but in the breakdown method, we split the numbers into the large parts (millions and above) and the smaller parts (below millions). The smaller parts were 144,696 and 400,407, which add to 545,103. That 545,103 is entirely in the lower part, so when added to 1,590,000,000, it should be correct.\n\nHowever, the digit-by-digit method gives a different result. Therefore, there must be a miscalculation in the digit-by-digit steps.\n\nLet me check position3 again (hundreds place):\n\nIn digit-by-digit:\n\nPosition3 (hundreds): first number has 6, second has 0. So 6 +0 +1 (carryover from tens place which was 14 \u2192 carryover1). So 6+0+1=7. That's correct. So hundreds digit is7.\n\nPosition4 (thousands): first's 4, second's0. So 4+0=4. No carryover from hundreds place (since 7 didn't cause a carryover). So thousands digit is4.\n\nBut in the breakdown method, the thousands digit in the small parts was 5. That's because the small parts addition includes the lower digits which are separate from the large parts. The large parts (millions and above) don't affect the thousands place in the small parts addition. So that's okay.\n\nWait, but when you add the large parts (596,000,000 + 994,000,000) gives 1,590,000,000. The small parts (144,696 +400,407)=545,103. So total is 1,590,000,000 +545,103=1,590,545,103.\n\nHowever, the digit-by-digit method gives 1,590,544,743. The difference is exactly 360 (545,103 vs 544,743). The difference of 360 is because in the digit-by-digit, the thousands digit is4 (in 544,743) versus5 in the breakdown method (545,103). So why is there a discrepancy?\n\nWait, perhaps I made an error in the digit-by-digit calculation in the thousands place. Let me retrace:\n\nPosition3 (hundreds): 6 +0 +1 (from previous carryover) =7 \u2192 correct.\n\nPosition4 (thousands): first's thousands digit is4, second's thousands digit is0. So 4+0=4. But wait, the thousands digit in the second number's breakdown was 0? Let me confirm:\n\nSecond number is 994,400,407. Breaking down:\n\nFrom right to left (positions1-9):\n\nPosition1:7 (ones),\n\n2:4 (tens),\n\n3:0 (hundreds),\n\n4:0 (thousands),\n\n5:0 (ten thousands),\n\n6:4 (hundred thousands),\n\n7:4 (millions),\n\n8:9 (ten millions),\n\n9:9 (hundred millions).\n\nYes, so the thousands digit (position4) is0. Therefore, adding 4+0=4. So the thousands digit is4, which is correct. But in the breakdown method, the thousands digit in the small parts addition is5. How is this possible?\n\nWait, because the small parts addition includes the thousands digit from the original numbers. The small parts are the last six digits (since the large parts were up to millions). Wait, maybe my breakdown was incorrect.\n\nWait the first number is 596,144,696. The breakdown was into 596,000,000 (millions and above) plus144,696 (the last six digits). Similarly, the second number is994,400,407 \u2192994,000,000 +400,407.\n\nWait, but 596,144,696: the part below millions is 144,696. The millions digit in the first number is6 (the first number is 596 million... so the millions part is up to 596, so the rest is 144,696). So the thousands digit in the small part (144,696) is4 (from the 144 thousand...).\n\nSimilarly, the second number's small part is400,407, which has thousands digit0 (since it's 400 thousand..., so thousands digit is0). So adding those:\n\n144,696 has thousands digit4, and400,407 has thousands digit0. So when adding those small parts, the thousands digit is4+0 plus any carryover from hundreds place.\n\nWait in the small parts addition:\n\nThe hundreds place:6 (from144,696) +4 (from400,407) =10 \u21921 with carryover1.\n\nThen thousands place:4 (from first) +0 (second) +1 (carryover)=5. So that's correct. Thus the thousands digit in the small part is5, which carries over to the total.\n\nBut in the digit-by-digit of the entire number, the thousands digit is4. This discrepancy arises because in the digit-by-digit method, the thousands place is part of the entire number, not just the small parts. Wait no, the thousands digit in the entire number should include both the large and small parts. Wait, but the large parts (millions and above) don't affect the thousands digit. The thousands digit is only from the small parts and any carryover from lower digits.\n\nWait the thousands digit in the full number is the same as the thousands digit in the small parts addition, because the large parts (millions and above) don't contribute to thousands place. Therefore, there must be an error in the digit-by-digit calculation.\