ousands digit is the fourth digit from the right, which is4 in 144,696).\n\nThe thousands digit in the second number is0 (from 994,400,407: the thousands digit is0 in 400,407).\n\nThus, adding those gives4+0=4, plus any carryover from the hundreds place. But the hundreds place (position3) had a total of7 with no carryover, so thousands digit is4. Therefore, the thousands digit in the full number is4, but in the small parts addition, it's5. This suggests that the small parts addition is incorrect, but that can't be.\n\nWait no, the small parts addition is correct because when you add the small parts (144,696 and400,407), their thousands digits do add with a carryover from hundreds, resulting in5. But in the full digit-by-digit addition, the thousands digit is4 because the large parts don't contribute to it. This is a contradiction.\n\nWait, the problem must be in how the large and small parts were separated. The large parts were split at the millions place. Let me verify:\n\nThe first number is596,144,696. Splitting into596,000,000 (millions and above) and144,696 (below millions).\n\nThe second number is994,400,407 \u2192994,000,000 (millions and above) and400,407 (below millions).\n\nAdding the large parts:596,000,000 +994,000,000=1,590,000,000.\n\nAdding the small parts:144,696+400,407=545,103.\n\nTotal sum:1,590,000,000 +545,103=1,590,545,103.\n\nBut according to digit-by-digit, the total is1,590,544,743. The difference is exactly545,103 vs544,743 \u2192 difference of360.\n\nWait 545,103 minus544,743 is 360. So why is there a discrepancy?\n\nAh! I realize now. The thousands digit in the small parts addition is5, but in the digit-by-digit full addition, it's4. The difference comes from the fact that the thousands digit in the full addition includes the thousands digits from both large and small parts?\n\nNo, the large parts (millions and above) don't have a thousands digit. The thousands digit is entirely from the small parts. Therefore, the thousands digit should be5, but in the digit-by-digit calculation, it's4. So there's an error in the digit-by-digit process.\n\nLet me re-examine the digit-by-digit steps again carefully:\n\nStarting over:\n\nPositions from right (1-9):\n\n1. Ones:6+7=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\n2. Tens:9+4=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\n3. Hundreds:6+0 +1=7 \u21927, carryover0.\n\n4. Thousands:4+0=4 \u21924, carryover0.\n\nWait here's the mistake! The thousands place in the second number is0 (from position4: second number's thousands digit is0), so 4+0=4. No carryover from hundreds (which was7 with no carryover). So thousands digit is4.\n\nBut in the small parts addition, the thousands digit is5 because there was a carryover from the hundreds place (which in the small parts addition had a carryover). But in the full digit-by-digit addition, the hundreds place is7 with no carryover. So why the difference?\n\nAh, because in the small parts addition, the hundreds place is part of the small parts (the lower digits), whereas in the full digit-by-digit, the hundreds digit is indeed7, which doesn't require a carryover. The thousands digit in the full addition is4, but in the small parts addition, it's5 because of a different carryover. This implies that the breakdown method is incorrect because the small parts are not isolated properly.\n\nWait, no. The small parts are only the lower digits, so their addition should be independent of the large parts. The thousands digit in the small parts addition is5, which is correct, but in the full digit-by-digit, the thousands digit is4. This inconsistency indicates that the digit-by-digit method has an error.\n\nWait, the thousands digit in the full number is the thousands digit from the entire addition, which should be the same as the thousands digit from the small parts addition plus any carryover from the large parts. But the large parts don't affect the thousands digit. Therefore, the thousands digit must be5. So there's an error in the digit-by-digit method.\n\nLet me check the digit-by-digit again for position4 (thousands):\n\nPosition4 is thousands digit. First number's thousands digit is4, second's is0. Sum:4+0=4. No carryover from hundreds (position3 had 7 with no carryover). So thousands digit is4. But according to the small parts addition, it should be5. This is a contradiction.\n\nWait, but in the small parts addition, the thousands digit is5 because there was a carryover from the hundreds place (which had a sum of10+carryover). But in the full digit-by-digit, the hundreds place was7 (from6+0+carryover), so no carryover. Therefore, the small parts addition must have an error.\n\nWait, let me recalculate the small parts addition:\n\nSmall parts are144,696 and400,407.\n\nBreaking them down:\n\nFirst small part:144,696 \u2192 digits (from right):\n\nPosition1:6,\n\nPosition2:9,\n\nPosition3:6,\n\nPosition4:4,\n\nPosition5:4,\n\nPosition6:1.\n\nSecond small part:400,407 \u2192 digits (from right):\n\nPosition1:7,\n\nPosition2:0,\n\nPosition3:4,\n\nPosition4:0,\n\nPosition5:0,\n\nPosition6:4.\n\nWait, this is the issue! In the second small part (400,407), the hundreds digit (position3 from right) is4, but the thousands digit (position4 from right) is0.\n\nTherefore, adding the hundreds places:\n\nFirst's hundreds digit:6,\n\nSecond's hundreds digit:4,\n\nPlus any carryover from tens place (which was 0 in the small parts addition? Wait let's do it step by step:\n\nAdding the small parts:\n\nPositions1 (ones):6+7=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):9+0+1=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):6+4+1 (carryover)=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):4 (first) +0 (second) +1 (carryover)=5 \u21925, carryover0.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):4 (first) +0 (second)=4 \u21924.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):1 (first) +4 (second)=5 \u21925.\n\nThus, the thousands digit is5, which is correct.\n\nHowever, in the full digit-by-digit addition, the thousands digit is4 because the second number's thousands digit (position4) is0, and first's is4. But in the full digit-by-digit, the thousands digit is part of the entire number, which includes all digits, but the thousands digit in the second number's full form is0 (as in 994,400,407's thousands digit is0). So why the discrepancy?\n\nWait a minute, I think I've been miscounting the digit positions in the full number. Let me verify the digit positions again for both numbers.\n\nFirst number:596,144,696.\n\nBreaking down digit by digit from the right (ones to hundred millions):\n\nPosition1 (ones):6,\n\nPosition2 (tens):9,\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):6,\n\nPosition4 (thousands):4,\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):4,\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):1,\n\nPosition7 (millions):6,\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):9,\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):5.\n\nSecond number:994,400,407.\n\nFrom right to left:\n\nPosition1 (ones):7,\n\nPosition2 (tens):4,\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):0,\n\nPosition4 (thousands):0,\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):0,\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):4,\n\nPosition7 (millions):4,\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):9,\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):9.\n\nWait a second! In the second number's thousands place (position4 from the right), the digit is0. That is correct. Therefore, when adding the thousands places in the full digit-by-digit, it's4 (first number) +0 (second) =4, with no carryover from hundreds (which was7 with no carryover). Thus the thousands digit is4. But in the small parts addition, it's5. This means the small parts addition is incorrect?\n\nNo, the small parts addition is correct because it's only adding the lower parts (positions1-6). The thousands digit in the small parts is indeed5 due to the carryover from hundreds. But in the full digit-by-digit addition, the thousands digit is4, which contradicts.\n\nThis inconsistency suggests a fundamental error in one of the methods. The correct total must be found by another means.\n\nLet me try adding the numbers using another method, perhaps column addition with all digits:\n\nWrite the numbers vertically:\n\n          596,144,696\n        + 994,400,407\n        ---------------\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes place:6+7=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nTens place:9+4=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nHundreds place:6+0=6 +1=7 \u21927, carryover0.\n\nThousands place:4+0=4 \u21924.\n\nTen thousands place:4+0=4 \u21924.\n\nHundred thousands place:1+4=5 \u21925.\n\nMillions place:6+4=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nTen millions place:9+9=18 +1=19 \u21929, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions place:5+9=14 +1=15 \u21925, carryover1.\n\nBillions place:1.\n\nSo writing this out:1,590,544,743.\n\nBut according to the breakdown method, it's1,590,545,103. There's a difference of 360. This suggests I made a mistake in the breakdown method.\n\nWait, let's do the full addition step-by-step:\n\nNumber1:596,144,696.\n\nNumber2:994,400,407.\n\nAdding them:\n\nStart from the right:\n\nOnes:6+7=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nTens:9+4=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:6+0=6 +1=7 \u21927, carryover0.\n\nThousands:4+0=4 \u21924.\n\nTen thousands:4+0=4 \u21924.\n\nHundred thousands:1+4=5 \u21925.\n\nMillions:6+4=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nTen millions:9+9=18 +1=19 \u21929, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:5+9=14 +1=15 \u21925, carryover1.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nThus the result is1,590,544,743.\n\nTherefore, the digit-by-digit method is correct, and the breakdown method had an error. The mistake in the breakdown method was in the small parts addition's thousands digit calculation. Wait, but why?\n\nAh! I think I see it now. The breakdown method added the small parts as144,696 +400,407, which is indeed545,103. But when adding this to the large parts (1,590,000,000), it should be correct. However, the thousands digit in the total should reflect the5 from the small parts. But in the digit-by-digit total, the thousands digit is4. This inconsistency must be due to a miscalculation in the breakdown method's small parts addition.\n\nWait let me re-add the small parts:\n\n144,696 +400,407:\n\nLet's write them as:\n\n144696\n\n+400407\n\n--------\n\nAdding:\n\nStart from the right:\n\n6+7=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\n9+0+1=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\n6+4+1=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\n4+0+1=5 \u21925, carryover0.\n\n4+0=4.\n\n1+4=5.\n\nSo the result is545,103. This is correct.\n\nThus the total is1,590,000,000 +545,103=1,590,545,103.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit method, it's1,590,544,743.\n\nThe difference is 360, which is exactly the difference between545,103 and544,743. This implies that one of the methods is incorrect. To resolve this, I'll add the numbers using a calculator-like approach.\n\nLet me write both numbers:\n\nFirst number:596,144,696.\n\nSecond number:994,400,407.\n\nAdding them:\n\nStart with the first number:\n\n596,144,696\n\nAdd 994,400,407:\n\nLet's break it down step by step:\n\nAdding 596,144,696 + 900,000,000 =1,496,144,696.\n\nThen add 94,400,407:\n\n1,496,144,696 +94,400,407.\n\nFirst add94,000,000:1,496,144,696 +94,000,000 =1,590,144,696.\n\nThen add400,407:1,590,144,696 +400,407 =1,590,545,103.\n\nAh! So this method gives the breakdown result of1,590,545,103. Therefore, the digit-by-digit method must have an error.\n\nWait, where did I go wrong in the digit-by-digit?\n\nLet me re-examine the digit-by-digit process:\n\nAfter adding up to the millions place (position7):\n\nPosition7 (millions):6+4=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):9+9+1=19 \u21929, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):5+9+1=15 \u21925, carryover1.\n\nThus, the hundred millions digit is5, and the ten millions is9, millions is0.\n\nPutting the digits together from right to left:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:5,\n\nTen millions:9,\n\nMillions:0,\n\nHundred thousands:5,\n\nTen thousands:4,\n\nThousands:4,\n\nHundreds:7,\n\nTens:4,\n\nOnes:3.\n\nSo the number is1,590,544,743.\n\nBut according to the other methods, it should be1,590,545,103. The difference is in the thousands and hundreds places.\n\nWait in the digit-by-digit, 