igit in the full addition should be4 (4+0) with no carryover. But according to other methods, it should be5. This inconsistency is unresolved.\n\nWait now I'm really confused. Let's add the numbers manually:\n\n596,144,696\n\n+994,400,407\n\n-------------\n\nLet's write them aligned:\n\n          596,144,696\n\n        + 994,400,407\n\n        -----------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:6+7=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nTens:9+4=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:6+0=6 +1=7 \u21927, carryover0.\n\nThousands:4+0=4 \u21924.\n\nTen thousands:4+0=4 \u21924.\n\nHundred thousands:1+4=5 \u21925.\n\nMillions:6+4=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nTen millions:9+9=18 +1=19 \u21929, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:5+9=14 +1=15 \u21925, carryover1.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nThus the total is1,590,544,743.\n\nBut when adding using the large/small parts:\n\nLarge parts (millions and above):\n\n596,000,000 +994,000,000 =1,590,000,000.\n\nSmall parts:\n\n144,696 +400,407 =545,103.\n\nTotal:1,590,000,000 +545,103 =1,590,545,103.\n\nThese two results are different. This means one of the methods is wrong. But which one?\n\nLet me verify with another approach: using calculator approximation.\n\nEstimate the numbers:\n\n596,144,696 is approximately 600,000,000.\n\n994,400,407 is approximately 1,000,000,000.\n\nTheir sum is approximately1,600,000,000. The two results are1,590,544,743 and1,590,545,103, both around1.59 billion, so that's consistent.\n\nThe difference between the two is360. To find the exact sum, let's do a precise addition:\n\nLet me write both numbers:\n\nFirst number:596,144,696.\n\nBreakdown:\n\n500,000,000 +\n\n90,000,000 +\n\n6,000,000 +\n\n100,000 +\n\n40,000 +\n\n4,000 +\n\n600 +\n\n90 +\n\n6.\n\nSecond number:994,400,407.\n\nBreakdown:\n\n900,000,000 +\n\n90,000,000 +\n\n4,000,000 +\n\n400,000 +\n\n0 +\n\n0 +\n\n4,000 +\n\n0 +\n\n7.\n\nAdding each component:\n\nBillions:\n\n500M +900M =1,400,000,000.\n\nHundred millions:\n\n90M +90M =180,000,000.\n\nTen millions:\n\n6M +4M =10M.\n\nMillions:\n\n0 +0 (since first has no millions beyond that, and second has4M? Wait first number's breakdown:\n\nWait let's re-express both numbers properly:\n\nFirst number:5 (hundred millions),9 (ten millions),6 (millions),1 (hundred thousands),4 (ten thousands),4 (thousands),6 (hundreds),9 (tens),6 (ones).\n\nSecond number:9 (hundred millions),9 (ten millions),4 (millions),4 (hundred thousands),0 (ten thousands),0 (thousands),0 (hundreds),4 (tens),7 (ones).\n\nAdding each place:\n\nHundred millions:5+9=14 \u21924 with carryover1.\n\nTen millions:9+9=18 +1 (carryover)=19 \u21929 with carryover1.\n\nMillions:6+4=10 +1=11 \u21921 with carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:1+4=5 +1=6.\n\nTen thousands:4+0=4.\n\nThousands:4+0=4.\n\nHundreds:6+0=6.\n\nTens:9+4=13 \u21923 with carryover1.\n\nOnes:6+7=13 \u21923 with carryover1.\n\nWait this approach is different and may help:\n\nStarting from the highest place (hundred millions):\n\nHundred millions place:5 +9 =14 \u2192 write4, carryover1.\n\nTen millions:9+9=18 +1=19 \u21929, carryover1.\n\nMillions:6+4=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:1+4=5 +1=6 \u21926, carryover0.\n\nTen thousands:4+0=4.\n\nThousands:4+0=4.\n\nHundreds:6+0=6.\n\nTens:9+4=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nOnes:6+7=13 +1 (carryover)=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nWait but this is a different way of adding from left to right, which might be error-prone. The final result would be:\n\nBillions:1 (from carryover),\n\nHundred millions:4,\n\nTen millions:9,\n\nMillions:1,\n\nHundred thousands:6,\n\nTen thousands:4,\n\nThousands:4,\n\nHundreds:6,\n\nTens:3,\n\nOnes:4.\n\nWait that gives1,491,644,634, which is incorrect. Clearly this approach is wrong because adding from left to right without considering all carries is messy.\n\nBack to the initial problem. The two methods give different results, which means one is wrong. Since the digit-by-digit addition step-by-step carefully led to1,590,544,743, but the large/small parts method gives1,59