t cause a carryover). So thousands digit is4.\n\nBut in the breakdown method, the thousands digit in the small parts was 5. That's because the small parts addition includes the lower digits which are separate from the large parts. The large parts (millions and above) don't affect the thousands place in the small parts addition. So that's okay.\n\nWait, but when you add the large parts (596,000,000 + 994,000,000) gives 1,590,000,000. The small parts (144,696 +400,407)=545,103. So total is 1,590,000,000 +545,103=1,590,545,103.\n\nHowever, the digit-by-digit method gives 1,590,544,743. The difference is exactly 360 (545,103 vs 544,743). The difference of 360 is because in the digit-by-digit, the thousands digit is4 (in 544,743) versus5 in the breakdown method (545,103). So why is there a discrepancy?\n\nWait, perhaps I made an error in the digit-by-digit calculation in the thousands place. Let me retrace:\n\nPosition3 (hundreds): 6 +0 +1 (from previous carryover) =7 → correct.\n\nPosition4 (thousands): first's thousands digit is4, second's thousands digit is0. So 4+0=4. But wait, the thousands digit in the second number's breakdown was 0? Let me confirm:\n\nSecond number is 994,400,407. Breaking down:\n\nFrom right to left (positions1-9):\n\nPosition1:7 (ones),\n\n2:4 (tens),\n\n3:0 (hundreds),\n\n4:0 (thousands),\n\n5:0 (ten thousands),\n\n6:4 (hundred thousands),\n\n7:4 (millions),\n\n8:9 (ten millions),\n\n9:9 (hundred millions).\n\nYes, so the thousands digit (position4) is0. Therefore, adding 4+0=4. So the thousands digit is4, which is correct. But in the breakdown method, the thousands digit in the small parts addition is5. How is this possible?\n\nWait, because the small parts addition includes the thousands digit from the original numbers. The small parts are the last six digits (since the large parts were up to millions). Wait, maybe my breakdown was incorrect.\n\nWait the first number is 596,144,696. The breakdown was into 596,000,000 (millions and above) plu