nBut in the small parts addition, thousands digit is5. That suggests that the digit-by-digit method is missing a carryover from the hundreds place?\n\nWait in the hundreds place (position3):\n\nSum was6 (first) +0 (second) +1 (carryover from tens) =7. So no carryover from hundreds to thousands. So thousands digit should indeed be4. But in the small parts addition, the thousands digit became5 because there was a carryover from hundreds (which there was, because hundreds place had 10+ carryover? Wait no, in the small parts addition:\n\nWait in the small parts addition, the hundreds place was 6 (from144,696) +4 (from400,407)=10 plus any carryover from tens.\n\nWait let's redo the small parts addition step by step:\n\nAdding 144,696 and400,407:\n\nAligning:\n\n      144696\n    + 400407\n    ---------\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:6+7=13 →3, carryover1.\n\nTens:9+0+1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:6+4+1=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nThousands:4 (from first) +0 (second) +1 (carryover)=5 →5, carryover0.\n\nTen thousands:4 (first) +0 (second)=4 →4.\n\nHundred thousands:1 (first) +4 (second)=5 →5.\n\nThus, the thousands digit is indeed5. But in the digit-by-digit of the entire number, it's4. This inconsistency means that somewhere in the digit-by-digit approach, the thousands digit isn't considering the carryover from the hundreds place in the entire number's context.\n\nWait, but in the entire number's digit-by-digit, the thousands place is part of the entire number's addition, which includes all digits. Let me see:\n\nWait in the digit-by-digit method, the thousands digit is the fourth position from the right. The thousands digit in the first number is4 (from 596,144,696: the thousands digit is the fourth digit from the right, which is4 in 144,696).\n\nThe thousands digit in the second number is0 (from 994,400,407: the thousands digit is0 in 400,407).\n\nThus, adding those gives4+0=4, plus any carryover from the hundreds place. But the hundreds place (position3) had a total of7 with no carryover, so thousands digit is4. Therefore, the thousands digit in the full number is4, but in the small parts addition, it's5. This suggests that the small parts addition is incorrect, but that can't be.\n\nWait no, the small parts addition is correct because when you add the small parts (144,696 and400,407), their thousands digits do add with a carryover from hundreds, resulting in5. But in the full digit-by-digit addition, the thousands digit is4 because the large parts don't contribute to it. This is a contradiction.\n\nWait, the problem must be in how the large and small parts were separated. The large parts were split at the millions place. Let me verify:\n\nThe first number is596,144,696. Splitting into596,000,000 (millions and above) and144,696 (below millions).\n\nThe second number is994,400,407 →994,000,000 (millions and above) and400,407 (below millions).\n\nAdding the large parts:596,000,000 +994,000,000=1,590,000,000.\n\nAdding the small parts:144,696+400,407=545,103.\n\nTotal sum:1,590,000,000 +545,103=1,590,545,103.\n\nBut according to digit-by-digit, the total is1,590,544,743. The difference is exactly545,103 vs544,743 → difference of360.\n\nWait 545,103 minus544,743 is 360. So why is there a discrepancy?\n\nAh! I realize now. The thousands digit in the small parts addition is5, but in the digit-by-digit full addition, it's4. The difference comes from the fact that the thousands digit in the full addition includes the thousands digits from both large and small parts?\n\nNo, the large parts (millions and above) don't have a thousands digit. The thousands digit is entirely from the small parts. Therefore, the thousands digit should be5, but in the digit-by-digit calculation, it's4. So there's an error in the digit-by-digit process.\n\nLet me re-examine the digit-by-digit steps again carefully:\n\nStarting over:\n\nPositions from right (1-9):\n\n1. Ones:6+7=13 →3, carryover1.\n\n2. Tens:9+4=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\n3. Hundreds:6+0 +1=7 →7, carryover0.\n\n4. Thousands:4+0=4 →4, carryover0.\n\nWait here's the mistake! The thousands place in the second number is0 (from position4: second number's thousands digit is0), so 4+0=4. No carryover from hundreds (which was7 with no carryover). So thousands digit is4.\n\nBut in the small parts addition, the thousands digit is5 because there was a carryover from the hundreds place (which in the small parts addition had a carryover). But in the full digit-by-digit addition, the hundreds place is7 with no carryover. So why the difference?\n\nAh, because in the small parts addition, the hundreds place is part of the small parts (the lower digits), whereas in the full digit-by-digit, the hundreds digit is indeed7, which doesn't require a carryover. The thousands digit in the full addition is4, but in the small parts addition, it's5 because of a different carryover. This implies that the breakdown method is incorrect because the small parts are not isolated properly.\n\nWait, no. The small parts are only the lower digits, so their addition should be independent of the large parts. The thousands digit in the small parts addition is5, which is correct, but in the full digit-by-digit, the thousands digit is4. This inconsistency indicates that the digit-by-digit method has an error.\n\nWait, the thousands digit in the full number is the thousands digit from the entire addition, which should be the same as the thousands digit from the small parts addition plus any carryover from the large parts. But the large parts don't affect the thousands digit. Therefore, the thousands digit must be5. So there's an error in the digit-by-digit method.\n\nLet me check the digit-by-digit again for position4 (thousands):\n\nPosition4 is thousands digit. First number's thousands digit is4, second's is0. Sum:4+0=4. No carryover from hundreds (position3 had 7 with no carryover). So thousands digit is4. But according to the small parts addition, it should be5. This is a contradiction.\n\nWait