adds up to 13. Hmm, so I need to write down 3 and carry over 1 to the next column.\n\nWait, let me confirm the place values again. Let me make sure I'm not mixing up the positions. The first number is 563,062,438. Breaking it down:.\n\nPositions (from right):.\n\n1. Ones: 8.\n\n2. Tens: 3.\n\n3. Hundreds: 4.\n\n4. Thousands: 2.\n\n5. Ten thousands: 6.\n\n6. Hundred thousands: 0.\n\n7. Millions: 3.\n\n8. Ten millions: 6.\n\n9. Hundred millions: 5.\n\nSecond number 744,974,250:.\n\nPositions:.\n\n1. Ones: 0.\n\n2. Tens:5.\n\n3. Hundreds:2.\n\n4. Thousands:4.\n\n5. Ten thousands:7.\n\n6. Hundred thousands:9.\n\n7. Millions:4.\n\n8. Ten millions:4.\n\n9. Hundred millions:7.\n\nSo, the fifth digit from the right (ten thousands place) for the first number is 6, and the second number has 7 there. 6+7=13. So we write 3 in the ten thousands place and carry over 1 to the next (hundred thousands) column.\n\nMoving to the hundred thousands place: first number has 0, second has 9. Adding those gives 0 +9 =9, plus the carryover 1 makes 10. So we write down 0 here and carry over 1 again.\n\nNext, the millions place: first number has 3, second has 4. 3+4=7 plus the carryover 1 is 8. So 8 here, no carryover.\n\nTen millions place: first number 6, second number 4. 6+4=10. So write 0 and carry over 1.\n\nHundred millions place: first number 5, second number 7. 5+7=12 plus the carryover 1 makes 13. Hmm, so that would be 3 in the hundred millions place and carry over 1 to the next (billions) place.\n\nWait, but since both numbers are 9 digits, adding them could result in a 10-digit number. So the carryover 1 would create a new digit at the beginning.\n\nLet me go through each step again carefully, perhaps writing down each column's sum with carryover:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones):.\n\n1. Ones: 8 + 0 = 8. Write 8.\n\n2. Tens: 3 +5 =8. Write 8.\n\n3. Hundreds:4 +2 =6. Write 6.\n\n4. Thousands:2 +4 =6. Write6.\n\n5. Ten thousands:6 +7 =13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\n6. Hundred thousands:0 +9 =9 +1 (carryover)=10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\n7. Millions:3 +4 =7 +1=8. Write8, no carryover.\n\n8. Ten millions:6 +4 =10. Write 0, carryover1.\n\n9. Hundred millions:5 +7 =12 +1 (carryover)=13. Write 3, carryover1.\n\nNow, since there's a carryover of 1 after the hundred millions place, we need to add a new digit at the beginning (billions place): 1.\n\nPutting all together, the digits from right to left would be:.\n\nStarting from the ones: 8,8,6,6,3,0,8,0,3 and the carryover 1. Wait, let me structure it properly:.\n\nThe digits from right to left after each step are:.\n\nOnes:8.\n\nTens:8.\n\nHundreds:6.\n\nThousands:6.\n\nTen thousands:3 (with a carryover).\n\nHundred thousands:0 (with carryover).\n\nMillions:8.\n\nTen millions:0 (with carryover).\n\nHundred millions:3 (with carryover).\n\nThen the carryover 1 as the next digit.\n\nSo arranging them from highest to lowest place:.\n\nThe final digits would be:.\n\nBillions place:1.\n\nHundred millions:3.\n\nTen millions:0.\n\nMillions:8.\n\nHundred thousands:0.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nThousands:6.\n\nHundreds:6.\n\nTens:8.\n\nOnes:8.\n\nWait, maybe I need to reconstruct the number step by step. Let's list the digits in order from the highest place (billions) down:.\n\nThe result has a billions digit of 1, then hundred millions 3, ten millions 0, millions 8, hundred thousands 0, ten thousands 3, thousands 6, hundreds 6, tens 8, ones 8.\n\nWait, but let me write it out properly:.\n\nStarting from the left (billions place):.\n\n1 (billions).\n\n3 (hundred millions).\n\n0 (ten millions).\n\n8 (millions).\n\n0 (hundred thousands).\n\n3 (ten thousands).\n\n6 (thousands).\n\n6 (hundreds).\n\n8 (tens).\n\n8 (ones).\n\nWait, but the ten millions place was the 8th digit from the right. Wait, perhaps I need to re-express this in terms of the digit positions.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's better to write the sum step by step:.\n\nLet me try adding the numbers vertically:.\n\n          5 6 3 0 6 2 4 3 8.\n        + 7 4 