to the billions place.\n\nSince there are no more digits except the carried over 1, that becomes the next digit. So putting it all together, starting from the billions place down:.\n\nThe carried over 1, then 2 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 8 (ones). Wait, let me check again step by step to make sure I didn't mix up any places.\n\nWait, maybe I should write each digit step by step properly. Let me list the numbers digit by digit:.\n\nFirst number: 7 3 4 1 9 0 8 2 1.\n\nSecond number:5 5 3 7 6 0 9 4 7.\n\nWait, actually, the numbers are 734,190,821 which is 9 digits: 7 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 1 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 553,760,947. Let's see: 5 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 7 (ones). Yes, that's correct.\n\nSo aligning them:.\n\n          7 3 4 1 9 0 8 2 1.\n        + 5 5 3 7 6 0 9 4 7.\n        --------------------.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\n1 +7 =8. Ones digit:8.\n\nTens:2+4=6. Tens digit:6.\n\nHundreds:8+9=17. Write 7, carryover 1.\n\nThousands:0+0 +1 (carry) =1. Thousands digit:1.\n\nTen thousands:9+6=15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands:1+7=8 +1 (carry) =9. Hundred thousands digit:9.\n\nMillions place:4 +3=7. Millions digit:7.\n\nTen millions:3 +5=8. Ten millions digit:8.\n\nHundred millions:7 +5=12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nSince there's a carryover 1 after the hundred millions, we add a new digit at the billions place:1</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
