dition?\n\nWait, in my first method, I must have made an error in the digit positions. Let me go back to the first approach.\n\nFirst approach:\n\nThe numbers are:\n\nFirst: 3 6 1 7 3 4 5 5 5 (digits from hundred millions to ones).\n\nSecond:5 3 1 4 0 1 1 7 7.\n\nWait, but when I broke down the digits earlier, perhaps I assigned the positions incorrectly.\n\nLet me re-express each number digit by digit with their place values:\n\nFirst number: 361,734,555.\n\nBreaking down each digit from left to right:\n\nDigit1 (hundred millions place):3.\n\nDigit2 (ten millions):6.\n\nDigit3 (millions):1.\n\nDigit4 (hundred thousands):7.\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):3.\n\nDigit6 (thousands):4.\n\nDigit7 (hundreds):5.\n\nDigit8 (tens):5.\n\nDigit9 (ones):5.\n\nSecond number:531,401,177.\n\nDigit1 (hundred millions):5.\n\nDigit2 (ten millions):3.\n\nDigit3 (millions):1.\n\nDigit4 (hundred thousands):4.\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):0.\n\nDigit6 (thousands):1.\n\nDigit7 (hundreds):1.\n\nDigit8 (tens):7.\n\nDigit9 (ones):7.\n\nNow, when adding digit by digit from right (ones) to left (hundred millions):\n\nPosition1 (ones):5 +7=12 → 2, carryover1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):5 +7=12 +1=13 →3, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):5 +1=6 +1=7 →7, no carryover.\n\nWait, hold on! Wait the hundreds place (position3) in the first number is 5 (since the first number\'s hundreds digit is 5). The second number\'s hundreds digit is 1 (from the breakdown above). So 5+1=6, plus carryover1 gives 7. So that\'s correct. Wait, but in my first calculation I had 4+1=5 here, which was wrong.\n\nOh! Here\'s the mistake! In my initial digit breakdown earlier, I incorrectly assigned the hundreds digit of the first number as 4 instead of 5. That was the error.\n\nLooking back, in the first approach, I had written:\n\nFirst number breakdown (incorrectly):\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):4.\n\nWait, that\'s the error. The first number\'s hundreds digit is actually 5, not 4. Because the first number is 361,734,555. Breaking it down: