 thousands is4.\n\nPosition4: thousands is2.\n\nPosition3: hundreds is0.\n\nPosition2: tens is4.\n\nPosition1: ones is8.\n\nSecond number:624,837,300.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):8 (since breakdown:\n\nPosition9:6 (hundred millions).\n\nPosition8:2 (ten millions).\n\nPosition7:4 (millions).\n\nPosition6:8 (hundred thousands).\n\nPosition5:3 (ten thousands).\n\nPosition4:7 (thousands).\n\nPosition3:3 (hundreds).\n\nPosition2:0 (tens).\n\nPosition1:0 (ones).\n\nSo digit6 (hundred thousands):8+8=16. So that\'s correct.\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands place): first number has 4, second has3 → 4+3=7. So that\'s correct.\n\nDigit7 (millions place): first\'s millions digit is7, second\'s is4 → 7+4=11, plus carryover1 makes 12 → digit2, carryover1. So digit7 is 2, carryover to digit8.\n\nDigit8 (ten millions): first has6, second has2 →6+2=8 plus carryover1 is 9. So digit8 is9.\n\nDigit9 (hundred millions):8+6=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nThus the digits:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:4\n\nTen millions:9\n\nMillions:2\n\nHundred thousands:6 (from 16)\n\nTen thousands:7\n\nThousands:9\n\nHundreds:3\n\nTens:4\n\nOnes:8.\n\nSo putting that together: 1,492,679,348.\n\nBut the user\'s answer is 1,492,671,348. So where did I go wrong?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the breakdown of the numbers. Let me check the original numbers again.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\nFirst number:867842048. Let\'s parse this as 867,842,048. So breaking down each digit:\n\nStarting from the left: 8 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions),7 (millions),8 (hundred thousands),4 (ten thousands),2 (thousands),0 (hundreds),4 (tens),8 (ones).\n\nSecond number:624837300. Which is 624,837,300. Breaking down:\n\n6 (hundred millions),2 (ten millions),4 (millions),8 (hundred thousands),3 (ten thousands),7 (thousands),3 (hundreds),0 (tens),0 (ones).\n\nWait a minute! In the thousands place of the second number, it\'s 7 (thousands place). So when adding the thousands place (digit4):\n\nFirst number\'s thousands digit is2 (fro