en millions place: first number is 9, second is 6. 9 +6 =15. So write 5 and carry over 1.\n\nHundred millions place: first number is 4, second is 4. 4+4=8 plus the carried 1 makes 9.\n\nWait, but let me check again step by step to make sure I didn't mix up any digits. Let me go through each digit position again carefully.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes place:.\n495252955 has 5 (ones).\n463839118 has 8 (ones).\n5 +8 =13 → 3, carry 1.\n\nTens place:.\n5 (from first number's tens) +1 (from second's tens) +1 carried =7. So 7, no carry.\n\nHundreds:.\n9 (first) +1 (second) =10. So 0, carry 1.\n\nThousands:.\n2 (first) +9 (second) =11, plus 1 carried is 12 → 2, carry 1.\n\nTen-thousands:.\n5 (first's ten-thousands) +3 (second's ten-thousands) =8, plus 1 →9. No carry.\n\nHundred thousands:.\n2 (first's hundred thousands) +8 (second's hundred thousands) =10 →0, carry 1.\n\nMillions:.\n5 (first's millions) +3 (second's millions) =8 +1 carried =9.\n\nTen millions:.\n9 (first's ten millions) +6 (second's ten millions) =15 →5, carry 1.\n\nHundred millions:.\n4 (first's hundred millions) +4 (second's hundred millions) =8 +1 carried =9</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
