4,881. Breaking it down: 4 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 1 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 420,745,962. So 4 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 2 (ones).\n\nWait a second, when I was adding the millions place, I thought the first number had 4 and the second 0. That's correct. So 4+0+1 (carryover) is 5. Then the ten-millions place: 3 +2 =5. Hundred-millions: 4+4=8. .\n\nPutting all the digits together from the ones up: 3 (ones), 4 (tens), 8 (hundreds), 0 (thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 5 (hundred thousands), 5 (millions), 5 (ten millions), 8 (hundred millions). Wait, but when I added the hundred thousands place, I had 7+7+1=15, so that's 5 in that place and carryover 1. Then the millions place was 4 (from first number) +0 (second) +1 (carryover) =5. Ten millions: 3+2=5. Hundred millions:4+4=8</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
