llions).\n\nNow, adding each position:.\n\nStarting from position 1 (ones):.\n\nFirst number: 0, second number:4 → 0+4=4. Write down 4. No carryover.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):.\n\n0 +9=9. Write 9. No carryover.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):.\n\n0 +3=3. Write 3. Still no carryover.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):.\n\n6 (from first number) +4 (second number) =10. Wait, 6 +4 is 10. So here we get 0 and carryover 1 to the next higher place.\n\nWait, but the thousands place is position 4. So the sum here is 6 +4 =10. So we write 0 and carryover 1 to the ten thousands place (position 5).\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):.\n\nFirst number has 4, second has 9. So 4+9=13, plus the carryover 1 makes 14. So here, we write 4 and carryover 1 again.\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):.\n\nFirst number has 8, second has6. 8+6=14, plus carryover 1 is 15. So write 5 and carryover 1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):.\n\nFirst number:9, second:8. 9+8=17, plus carryover 1=18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):.\n\nFirst number:1, second:9. 1+9=10, plus carryover 1=11. So write 1 and carryover 1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):.\n\nFirst number:4, second:8. 4+8=12, plus carryover 1=13. So here we have 13. Since this is the highest place, we just write 13.\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nStarting from position1 (ones) to position9 (hundred millions):.\n\nPositions:.\n\n1:4.\n\n2:9.\n\n3:3.\n\n4:0 (with carryover 1).\n\n5:4 (with carryover 1).\n\n6:5 (with carryover1).\n\n7:8 (carryover1).\n\n8:1 (carryover1).\n\n9:3 (since 13, but the 1 is carried over? Wait, hold on. Wait, the hundred millions place was 4+8+1=13, so actually, the digit in the hundred millions place is 3, and we have a carryover to a new billion place. Wait, but since both numbers are 9 digits, adding them could result in a 10-digit number. Hmm, I didn't consider that. Let me check again.\n\nWait, when adding the hundred millions place (position9):.\n\n4 (from first) +8 (second) +1 (carryover from position8) =13. So the digit he