ference between the two results:\n\nMy result: 1,457,738,830.\n\nUser\'s answer:1,557,737,830.\n\nLooking at the numbers:\n\nThe difference is in the hundred millions place and the millions? Let me see:\n\nMy answer:\n\n1 (billions), 4 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions),7 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 3 (tens),0 (ones).\n\nWait, maybe I miscounted the places. Let me write the number as:\n\nStarting from the billions digit:\n\n1 (billions),\n\n4 (hundred millions),\n\n5 (ten millions),\n\n7 (millions),\n\n7 (hundred thousands),\n\n3 (ten thousands),\n\n8 (thousands),\n\n8 (hundreds),\n\n3 (tens),\n\n0 (ones).\n\nWait, but that would be 1,457,738,830. \n\nThe user\'s answer is 1,557,737,830. So let\'s compare digit by digit:\n\nBillions: 1 vs 1 → same.\n\nHundred millions:4 vs5 → different.\n\nTen millions:5 vs5 → same.\n\nMillions:7 vs7 → same.\n\nHundred thousands:7 vs3? Wait, no, let me see the user\'s answer breakdown:\n\nWait the user\'s answer is 1,557,737,830.\n\nBreaking that down:\n\n1 (billions),\n\n5 (hundred millions),\n\n5 (ten millions),\n\n7 (millions),\n\n7 (hundred thousands),\n\n3 (ten thousands),\n\n7 (thousands),\n\n8 (hundreds),\n\n3 (tens),\n\n0 (ones).\n\nWait, so the hundred millions digit is 5, not 4, which is different from my calculation. So where did that come from?\n\nAh, perhaps I made an error in the hundred millions place addition. Let me check again:\n\nHundred millions place (the 9th digit from the right):\n\nFirst number\'s hundred millions digit is 7,\n\nSecond number\'s is also 7,\n\nso 7 +7 =14. So the digit here is 4, carryover1 to the next (billions place). Therefore, the hundred millions digit should be 4, not 5. So the user\'s answer has 5 there, which is wrong.\n\nTherefore, my calculation seems correct. The user might have made a mistake. Wait, but maybe I messed up another digit.\n\nWait, let\'s add the two numbers using another method: adding the numbers step by step i