→ digits from right:\n\n1 (1),5 (2),6 (3),1 (4),1 (5),8 (6),0 (7),2 (8),7 (9).\n\nAdding each column:\n\n1. Ones (1st):9+1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\n2. Tens (2nd):7+5=12 +1=13 →3, carryover1.\n\n3. Hundreds (3rd):1+6=7 +1=8 →8, no carryover.\n\n4. Thousands (4th):6+1=7 →7.\n\n5. Ten thousands (5th):2+1=3 →3.\n\n6. Hundred thousands (6th):9+8=17 →7, carryover1.\n\n7. Millions (7th):6+0=6 +1 (carryover) →7.\n\n8. Ten millions (8th):3+2=5 →5.\n\n9. Hundred millions (9th):7+7=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nThen the carryover1 →1 in the billions place.\n\nSo writing the digits from right to left (positions 1-9 plus the carryover):\n\nDigits:\n\nPosition1:0,\n\n2:3,\n\n3:8,\n\n4:7,\n\n5:3,\n\n6:7,\n\n7:7,\n\n8:5,\n\n9:4,\n\nplus the carryover1 at position10.\n\nTherefore, the number is:\n\n1 (position10),\n\n4 (position9),\n\n5 (position8),\n\n7 (position7),\n\n7 (position6),\n\n3 (position5),\n\n7 (position4),\n\n8 (position3),\n\n3 (position2),\n\n0 (position1).\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, position7 is the millions place. Let me map this properly:\n\nThe digits from right to left (after adding):\n\nPosition1 (ones):0,\n\nPosition2 (tens):3,\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):8,\n\nPosition4 (thousands):7,\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):3,\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):7,\n\nPosition7 (millions):7,\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):5,\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):4,\n\nPosition10 (billions):1.\n\nSo when written as a number, starting from position10 down to 1:\n\nPosition10:1,\n\nPosition9:4,\n\nPosition8:5,\n\nPosition7:7,\n\nPosition6:7,\n\nPosition5:3,\n\nPosition4:7,\n\nPosition3:8,\n\nPosition2:3,\n\nPosition1:0.\n\nSo the number is 1,457,737,830.\n\nWait, that\'s different from my first calculation where I thought I had 1,457,738,830. I must have made an error in the thousands place. Let me check the thousands place again.\n\nThousands place (position4): first number has 6, second has 1. 6+1=7 → correct. So that\'s 7 in thousands place.\n\nSo when written out, the thousands digit is 7, which is