→6, no carryover.\n\nPosition7 (millions):3 +0=3 →3.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):9 +9=18 →8, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):8 +9=17 +1(carry)=18 →8, carryover1. Wait, but there are no more digits. So we have to add that carryover as a new digit to the left. So the total becomes 1 in the next (billions) place.\n\nSo putting all together, the sum would be:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nPositions 1-9 digits: 2,0,3,2,1,6,3,8,8 plus the carryover 1 at the beginning (billions place). So the digits are 1 (billions), 8 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), etc. Wait, maybe I need to list them properly.\n\nWait, let me write the digits step by step:.\n\nAfter adding each position with carryover:.\n\nPositions:.\n\n1 (ones):2.\n\n2 (tens):0.\n\n3 (hundreds):3.\n\n4 (thousands):2.\n\n5 (ten thousands):1.\n\n6 (hundred thousands):6.\n\n7 (millions):3.\n\n8 (ten millions):8.\n\n9 (hundred millions):8 (from 8+9+1?), wait no. Wait, let's see:.\n\nWait the hundred millions place (position9):.\n\nFirst number's hundred millions digit is 8, second is9. So adding those:8+9=17, plus the carryover from the ten millions place addition. Wait, the ten millions place was position8, which was 9 (from first) +9 (second)=18, so we wrote 8 and carried over 1 to the hundred millions place. So when adding the hundred millions place:.\n\n8 (first) +9 (second) +1 (carryover from ten millions) =18. So that gives 8 in the hundred millions place, and carryover 1 to the next (billions) place. Since there are no more digits, that 1 becomes the new billions digit.\n\nSo the final digits from right to left (positions 1 to 9 plus the new billion):.\n\nSo the digits are:.\n\nBillions place:1.\n\nHundred millions:8.\n\nTen millions:8.\n\nMillions:3.\n\nHundred thousands:6.\n\nTen thousands:1.\n\nThousands:2.\n\nHundreds:3.\n\nTens:0.\n\nOnes:2.\n\nWait, maybe arranging them properly as a number:.\n\nStarting from the billions digit:.\n\n1 (billions), 8 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 3 