ten millions), 0 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nWait, actually, the number 240,006,408 is structured as:.\n\nDigits from left to right: 2 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nThe second number is 109,105,608:.\n\nBreaking it down: 1 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 8 (ones).\n\nSo when adding vertically:.\n\n          2 4 0 0 0 6 4 0 8.\n        + 1 0 9 1 0 5 6 0 8.\n        ---------------------.\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 8 +8 =16. Write 6, carryover 1.\n\nTens: 0 +0 +1 =1. Write 1.\n\nHundreds:4 +6 =10. Write 0, carryover1.\n\nThousands:6 +5 +1 (carry)=12. Write 2, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:0 +0 +1=1. Write1.\n\nHundred thousands:0 +1=1. Write1.\n\nMillions:0 +9=9. Write9.\n\nTen millions:4 +0=4. Write4.\n\nHundred millions:2 +1=3. Write3.\n\nWait, let me check each place again step by step.\n\nLet me list each digit position:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 8 +8 =16 → 6, carryover 1.\n\nPosition 2 (tens): 0 +0 +1 =1 → 1, no carry.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):4 +6 =10 →0, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):6 (from first number) +5 (second) =11, plus carryover1 makes 12 →2, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):0 +0 +1 (carry) →1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0 (from first) +1 (second) =1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):0 (first) +9 (second) =9.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):4 (first) +0 (second) =4.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):2 +1 =3.\n\nSo putting all the digits together from right to left:.\n\nThe digits we wrote down starting from the right are:.\n\nPosition1:6, pos2:1, pos3:0, pos4:2, pos5:1, pos6:1, pos7:9, pos8:4, pos9:3.\n\nSo the number is 3 (hundred millions),4 (ten millions),9 (millions),1 (hundred thousands),1 (ten thousands),2 (thousands),0 (hundreds),1 (tens),6 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
