s:0,\n\nTen millions:7,\n\nMillions:6,\n\nHundred thousands:3,\n\nTen thousands:8,\n\nThousands:9,\n\nHundreds:9,\n\nTens:2,\n\nOnes:8 \u2192 which is 1,076,389,928.\n\nAh! So why the discrepancy?\n\nBecause in the first method, when adding the original numbers directly, the hundred millions digits were 2 and8, giving 10+1=11, but in the second method, the first number after adding 800,000,000 became 1,014,759,092, which has 0 in the hundred millions place (since it's 1 billion, 14 million...). Therefore, the breakdown method is correct because when you add 214,759,092 + 800,000,000, the hundred millions digit (originally 2) becomes part of the billions place. Wait:\n\nWait original first number is 214,759,092. Breaking down:\n\n2 (hundred millions),1 (ten millions),4 (millions), etc.\n\nAdding 800,000,000 to it:\n\n214,759,092 +800,000,000 = 1,014,759,092.\n\nThis moves the hundred millions digit (2) into the billions place as 1 (since 2+8=10, but actually 214 million +800 million =1,014 million, which is 1,014,000,000 +759,092.\n\nTherefore, the hundred millions digit (originally 2 in the first number) becomes part of the billion increment, hence in the new number 1,014,759,092, the hundred millions digit is 1 (from the 14 in 1,014 million). Wait, no:\n\nWait 1,014,759,092 is composed of:\n\n1,000,000,000 (1 billion),\n\nplus 14,759,092.\n\nSo the hundred millions digit (position9 from the right) is '0' because the number is 1,014,759,092.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n1 (billion),\n\n0 (hundred millions),\n\n1 (ten millions),\n\n4 (millions),\n\n7 (hundred thousands),\n\n5 (ten thousands),\n\n9 (thousands),\n\n0 (hundreds),\n\n9 (tens),\n\n2 (ones).\n\nWait that's not correct. Let me parse 1,014,759,092:\n\nThe number is 1,014,759,092.\n\nCommas separate as: 1,014,759,092 \u2192 which is 1 billion, 14 million, 759 thousand, and 92.\n\nSo breaking down:\n\n- Billions: 1\n\n- Hundred millions: 0\n\n- Ten millions:1\n\n- Millions:4\n\n- Hundred thousands:7\n\n- Ten thousands