ons), 3 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 3 (ones). .\n\nThe second number is 711,514,593. Let's see: 7 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 3 (ones). So both are 9 digits. .\n\nSo when adding the hundred-millions place: first number has 3, second has 7. 3+7=10. So we write 0 here and carry over 1. But since there are no more digits beyond that, the carried-over 1 becomes the next digit (billions place). .\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 6.\n\nTens:7 (with carryover).\n\nHundreds:7.\n\nThousands:1 (carryover 1).\n\nTen-thousands:8.\n\nHundred-thousands:8.\n\nMillions:4.\n\nTen-millions:0 (carryover 1).\n\nHundred-millions:0 (carryover 1).\n\nThen the billions place is the carried-over 1</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
