y1)\n\n8:4 (1+2+1=4, no carry)\n\n9:8 (9+9=18 →8, carry1)\n\nThen the carryover 1 from the 9th position becomes the 10th digit (billions place).\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nPosition 10:1\n\nPosition9:8\n\nPosition8:4\n\nPosition7:2\n\nPosition6:2\n\nPosition5:2\n\nPosition4:1\n\nPosition3:2\n\nPosition2:3\n\nPosition1:9\n\nSo writing the number from billions down:\n\n1 (billions), 8 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 9 (units).\n\nWait, perhaps better to list the digits in order:\n\nStarting from the highest digit (billions):\n\n1 (billion)\n\nThen hundred millions:8\n\nten millions:4\n\nmillions:2\n\nhundred thousands:2\n\nten thousands:2\n\nthousands:1\n\nhundreds:2\n\ntens:3\n\nunits:9\n\nSo putting that together:\n\n1,842,221,239.\n\nBut the user thought it was 1,802,221,239. Hmm, so where did I go wrong?\n\nWait let's check the million's place. Let me re-examine each step again.\n\nPosition 7 (millions place):\n\nFirst number's millions digit is 8 (since the first number is 918,492,001: the millions digit is the 7th digit from the right, which is 8).\n\nSecond number's millions digit is 3 (since 923,729,238 has 3 in the millions place (the 7th digit from right is 3).\n\nAdding 8 +3 =11, plus the carryover 1 from previous (position 6's carryover?), wait let me retrace.\n\nWait position 6 is hundred thousands:\n\nFirst number:4, second:7 →4+7=11 plus carryover from position5.\n\nWait let's go step by step again carefully:\n\nStarting from position1 (units):\n\n1+8=9, no carry.\n\nPosition2 (tens):\n\n0+3=3, no carry.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\n0+2=2, no carry.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\n2 (first) +9 (second)=11 → digit1, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\n9 (first) +2 (second) =11 → plus carryover1 →12 → digit2, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\n4 (first) +7 (second) =11 → plus carryover1 →12 → digit2, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\n8 (first) +3 (second) =11 → plus carryover1 →12 → digit2, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n1 (first) +2 (second) =3 → plus carryover1 →4, no carry.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n9 +9 =18 → digit8, carryover1.\n\nThen the final carryover1 → billions place.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:8\n\nTen millions:4\n\nMillions:2\n\nHundred thousands:2\n\nTen thousands:2\n\nThousands:1\n\nHundreds:2\n\nTens:3\n\nUnits:9\n\nThus the number is 1,842,221,239. So the user's answer of 1,802,221,239 is incorrect. The correct answer should be 1,842,221,239. Wait but maybe I made a mistake in the millions place.\n\nWait let's check the millions place again. Position7:\n\nFirst number's millions digit: 8 (from 918,492,001: the millions digit is the third digit from the left? Wait perhaps I need to confirm the digit positions again.\n\nWait let's write the first number as 918,492,001:\n\nBreaking down each digit from left to right:\n\nDigit 1: 9 (hundred millions)\n\nDigit2:1 (ten millions)\n\nDigit3:8 (millions)\n\nDigit4:4 (hundred thousands)\n\nDigit5:9 (ten thousands)\n\nDigit6:2 (thousands)\n\nDigit7:0 (hundreds)\n\nDigit8:0 (tens)\n\nDigit9:1 (units)\n\nWait hold on! Wait a second. Maybe I messed up the digit positions.\n\nWait, the number 918,492,001 is structured as follows:\n\nBillions place: 0 (since it's a 9-digit number)\n\nHundred millions:9\n\nTen millions:1\n\nMillions:8\n\nHundred thousands:4\n\nTen thousands:9\n\nThousands:2\n\nHundreds:0\n\nTens:0\n\nUnits:1\n\nSimilarly, the second number 923,729,238 is:\n\nHundred millions:9\n\nTen millions:2\n\nMillions:3\n\nHundred thousands:7\n\nTen thousands:2\n\nThousands:9\n\nHundreds:2\n\nTens:3\n\nUnits:8\n\nSo when adding, the millions place for the first number is digit3 (8) and for the second number digit3 is 3 (since second number's millions digit is 3). So 8 +3 =11 plus carryover1 gives 12 → so digit2 with carryover1. So that part is correct.\n\nBut the Ten millions place:\n\nFir