5=12, plus the carryover 1 gives 13. So 3 here and carry over 1 again.\n\nFinally, the hundred millions place. The first number has a 3, and the second has a 7. 3 +7 =10, plus the carried 1 makes 11. So we write 1 in the hundred millions place and have to add another digit for the billions place with the carried 1. So the billions digit is 1.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right, the digits are 7 (ones), 5 (tens), 1 (hundreds), 3 (thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 0 (hundred thousands), 4 (millions), 3 (ten millions), 1 (hundred millions), and 1 (billions). So the total should be 1,133,073,157. Wait, let me check again to make sure I didn't mix up any digits. Maybe I should write each step more carefully.\n\nWait, let me recount each place step by step to ensure accuracy:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n1. Ones: 3 + 4 = 7 → 7.\n2. Tens: 5 + 0 = 5 →5.\n3. Hundreds:4 +7=11 → write 1, carryover 1.\n4. Thousands:9 +3 +1(carry)=13 → 3, carryover1.\n5. Ten thousands:5 +1 +1=7 →7, no carry.\n6. Hundred thousands:6 +4=10 →0, carryover1.\n7. Millions:8 +5 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n8. Ten millions:7 +5 +1=13 →3, carryover1.\n9. Hundred millions:3 +7 +1=11 →1, carryover1.\n10. Billions: The carryover 1, so 1.\n\nPutting all together from highest to lowest place: 1 (billions), 1 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands),7 (ten thousands),3 (thousands),1 (hundreds),5 (tens),7 (ones). Wait, that would make 1,133,073,157. But when I first thought of the digits, I might have miscounted the places. Let me verify with another method. Maybe adding the numbers in parts.\n\nAlternatively, adding 378,659,453 + 755,413,704.\n\nLet me break it down:.\n\nFirst, add the millions:.\n\n378,659,453 is 378,659 thousands + 453.\n\nWait, perhaps better to add digit by digit again.\n\nAlternatively, use another approach:.\n\n378,659,453.\n+755,413,704.\n= ?.\n\nAdding digit by digit:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nPosition 1 (units): 3 +4 =7.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):5 +0 =5.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):4 +7 =11 → write 1, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):9 +3 +1 =13 → 3, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):5 +1 +1 =7 →7, no carry.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):6 +4 =10 →0, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):8 +5 +1 =14 →4, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):7 +5 +1 =13 →3, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):3 +7 +1 =11 →1, carryover1.\n\nPosition10 (billions): carryover1 →1.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:.\n\n7 (units),5 (tens),1 (hundreds),3 (thousands),7 (ten thousands),0 (hundred thousands),4 (millions),3 (ten millions),1 (hundred millions), and the leading 1 in billions. .\n\nSo written as 1,133,073,157. Wait, but when constructing the number, the places are:.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nHundred millions:1.\n\nTen millions:3.\n\nMillions:3.\n\nHundred thousands:0.\n\nTen thousands:7.\n\nThousands:3.\n\nHundreds:1.\n\nTens:5.\n\nOnes:7.\n\nSo yes, 1,133,073,157. Let me check with a calculator approach. Let me compute 378,659,453 +755,413,704.\n\n378,659,453 + 700,000,000 = 1,078,659,453. Then add 55,413,704. 1,078,659,453 +55,413,704 = ?.\n\n1,078,659,453 +50,000,000 =1,128,659,453</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
