 than a billion? Wait, 510 million is less than a billion, 961 million is also less than a billion. Wait, but 510,548,739 is 510 million, so the billions digit is 0. The second number, 961,904,936 is 961 million, so also 0 in billions place. So adding the billions place: 0 +0 + carried 1 =1. So the total sum will have a 1 in the billions place. That's important.\n\nLet me go through each digit step by step again, carefully:.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nOnes: 9 +6 =15. Write 5, carryover 1.\n\nTens: 3 +3 +1=7. Write 7, no carryover.\n\nHundreds:7 +9=16. Write 6, carryover 1.\n\nThousands:8 +4 +1=13. Write 3, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:4 +0=4 +1=5. Write5, no carryover.\n\nHundred thousands:5 +9=14. Write4, carryover1.\n\nMillions place:0 (from first number's millions place) +1 (second number's millions) +1 (carryover)=2. Wait, first number's millions place is 0? Let's check again:.\n\nWait first number breakdown:.\n\n510,548,739:.\n\nBreaking down the places:.\n\nBillions:0.\n\nHundred millions:5.\n\nTen millions:1.\n\nMillions:0.\n\nHundred thousands:5.\n\nTen thousands:4.\n\nThousands:8.\n\nHundreds:7.\n\nTens:3.\n\nOnes:9.\n\nYes, so millions place is 0.\n\nSecond number's millions place is 1 (from 961, so the millions digit is 1).\n\nSo millions place addition: 0 +1 +1 (carryover from hundred thousands) = 2. Write 2, no carryover.\n\nTen millions place: first number has 1, second has 6. 1+6=7. Write7.\n\nHundred millions place:5 +9=14. Write4, carryover1.\n\nBillions place:0 +0 +1 (carryover)=1. So the final digit is 1.\n\nPutting all together:.\n\nStarting from the ones: 5,7,6,3,5,4,2,7,4,1 (with the billions digit first). Wait, let me reconstruct the digits step by step.\n\nWait, let me list each digit position:.\n\nBillions: 1.\n\nHundred millions:4.\n\nTen millions:7.\n\nMillions:2.\n\nHundred thousands:4.\n\nTen thousands:5.\n\nThousands:3.\n\nHundreds:6.\n\nTens:7.\n\nOnes:5.\n\nWait, let me see:.\n\nWait the order from highest to lowest 