 No carry-over needed.\n\nTen millions place: first number has a 6, second has a 3. 6 + 3 = 9. Still no carry-over.\n\nFinally, the hundred millions place: first number has a 6, second has a 4. 6 + 4 = 10. Wait, that's 10, so I need to write down 0 and carry over 1 to the billions place. But since there are no more digits beyond the hundred millions place, that carried-over 1 becomes a new digit at the beginning.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right, the digits we've calculated are 7 (ones), 6 (tens), 5 (hundreds), 0 (thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 6 (hundred thousands), 9 (millions), 9 (ten millions), 0 (hundred millions), and then the carried-over 1 in the billions place. So the total should be 1,050,834,267. Wait, let me check that again because sometimes when carrying over, it's easy to miscount the places. Let me verify each step once more to be sure.\n\nStarting over:.\n\nNumbers:.\n\n612,185,691.\n\n+437,648,576.\n\nLet me align them:.\n\n        612,185,691.\n\n      +437,648,576.\n\n      -----------------.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 1 + 6 =7.\n\nTens: 9 +7 =16 (write 6, carry 1).\n\nHundreds:6 +8=14 +1=15 (write5, carry1).\n\nThousands:5 +4=9 +1=10 (write0, carry1).\n\nTen thousands:8 +6=14 +1=15 (write5, carry1).\n\nHundred thousands:1 +4=5 +1=6 (write6, no carry).\n\nMillions:2 +7=9 (write9).\n\nTen millions:6 +3=9 (write9).\n\nHundred millions:1 (from first number) +7 (wait, hold on, wait the first number is 612,185,691, so breaking it down:.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the place values. Let me write each number's digits clearly.\n\nFirst number: 612,185,691.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\nBillions place: 6 (since it's a 9-digit number, the first digit is hundred millions place actually. Wait, wait, maybe I need to clarify the place values properly.\n\nWait, 612,185,691 is 6 hundred millions, 1 ten millions, 2 millions, 1 hundred thousand, 8 ten thousands, 5 thousands, 6 hundreds, 9 tens, 1 one.\n\nSimilarly, the second number: 437,648,5