e 80,000. So that matches.\n\nWait then why is there a discrepancy?\n\nWait the second method\'s total is 1,257,586,769. Let\'s break that down:\n\n1,257,586,769 → the digits are:\n\n1 (billion),\n\n2 (hundred millions),\n\n5 (ten millions),\n\n7 (millions),\n\n5 (hundred thousands),\n\n8 (ten thousands),\n\n6 (thousands),\n\n7 (hundreds),\n\n6 (tens),\n\n9 (ones).\n\nWait so the hundred thousands digit is5 instead of8. Wait that\'s the problem. In the second method, when adding the hundred thousands:\n\nFirst number\'s hundred thousands is900,000 (9*100,000) and second is600,000 (6*100,000). So total 1,500,000, which is 15*100,000. So in the total, that\'s the hundred thousands place being5 (since 15 has a 5 in hundred thousands and carryover1 to millions). But in the second method\'s total, the hundred thousands digit is5, which is correct. Then why in the second method\'s final total, the ten thousands digit is8 (from 80,000), which is also correct.\n\nWait so the total in the second method is 1,257,586,769. Let\'s see:\n\nBreaking down 1,257,586,769:\n\n- 1,257,586,769 → the millions part is 257 (million), then 586 (thousands), etc.\n\nWait, 257,586,769? No, commas are placed every three digits from the right.\n\nBreaking 1,257,586,769:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n- 769 (ones, tens, hundreds),\n\n- 586 (thousands, ten thousands, hundred thousands),\n\n- 257 (millions, ten millions, hundred millions),\n\n- 1 (billion).\n\nSo the hundred thousands place is the third digit from the comma in the thousands group: the \'5\' in 586, so yes, that\'s 5 hundred thousands (500,000), which comes from the 1.5 million (1,500,000), which is 1 million and 500,000. The 1 million would carry over to the millions place, hence the millions digit increases by1. Wait, but in the second method, the total after adding the hundred thousands was 1,257,500,000. That 1,500,000 added to 1,256,000,000 gives 1,257,500,000. So that\'s correct.\n\nWait then where is the discrepancy?\n\nIn th