final_output:  ["1,190,323,000 plus the remaining 114+61=175.\n\nSo total is 1,190,323,000 + 175 = 1,190,323,175?.\n\nWait, this contradicts my previous result. Hmm, where did I go wrong here?\n\nWait, let me recalculate this way:\n\nBreaking down both numbers:\n\nFirst number: 831,100,114.\n\nBreakdown:\n\n800,000,000 (8*100,000,000)\n\n30,000,000 (3*10,000,000)\n\n1,000,000 (1*1,000,000)\n\n0 (hundred thousands place is 1, but wait, let me see:\n\nWait 831,100,114:\n\nThe digits are 8 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions),1 (millions),1 (hundred thousands),0 (ten thousands),0 (thousands),1 (hundreds),1 (tens),4 (ones).\n\nSo the breakdown is:\n\n800,000,000 +\n\n30,000,000 +\n\n1,000,000 +\n\n100,000 +\n\n0 (ten thousands) +\n\n0 (thousands) +\n\n100 +\n\n10 +\n\n4.\n\nSecond number:359,223,061.\n\nBreakdown:\n\n300,000,000 +\n\n50,000,000 +\n\n9,000,000 +\n\n200,000 +\n\n20,000 +\n\n3,000 +\n\n0 (hundreds) +\n\n60 +\n\n1.\n\nNow adding term by term:\n\nHundred millions:\n\n800,000,000 +300,000,000 =1,100,000,000.\n\nTen millions:\n\n30,000,000 +50,000,000=80,000,000.\n\nMillions:\n\n1,000,000 +9,000,000=10,000,000.\n\nHundred thousands:\n\n100,000 +200,000=300,000.\n\nTen thousands:\n\n0 +20,000=20,000.\n\nThousands:\n\n0 +3,000=3,000.\n\nHundreds:\n\n100 +0=100.\n\nTens:\n\n10 +60=70.\n\nOnes:\n\n4 +1=5.\n\nNow sum all these components:\n\nStart with the largest:\n\n1,100,000,000\n\n+80,000,000 → 1,180,000,000\n\n+10,000,000 →1,190,000,000\n\n+300,000 →1,190,300,000\n\n+20,000 →1,190,320,000\n\n+3,000 →1,190,323,000\n\n+100 →1,190,323,100\n\n+70 →1,190,323,170\n\n+5 →1,190,323,175.\n\nWait a minute, so this method gives 1,190,323,175, but the previous digit-by-digit addition gave me 1,190,322,415. There's a discrepancy here. I must have messed up in one of the methods.\n\nLet me check again the digit-by-digit addition step by step.\n\nFirst number: 831,100,114.\n\nSecond number: 359,223,061.\n\nAdding digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the right (ones):\n\nOnes:4+1=5.\n\nTens:1+0=1.\n\nHundreds:1+3=4.\n\nThousands:0+2=2.\n\nTen thousands:0+2=2.\n\nHundred thousands:1+2=3.\n\nMillions:1+9=10 → write 0, carryover 1.\n\nTen millions:3+5 +1 (carryover) =9.\n\nHundred millions:8+3=11 → write 1, carryover 1.\n\nBillions:1 (carryover).\n\nSo the digits from right to left (ones to billions):\n\nOnes:5\n\nTens:1\n\nHundreds:4\n\nThousands:2\n\nTen thousands:2\n\nHundred thousands:3\n\nMillions:0 (with carryover)\n\nTen millions:9\n\nHundred millions:1 (from 11, so 1 is written here)\n\nBillions:1 (carryover).\n\nSo arranging them from billions down:\n\n1 (billions),\n\n1 (hundred millions),\n\n9 (ten millions),\n\n0 (millions),\n\n3 (hundred thousands),\n\n2 (ten thousands),\n\n2 (thousands),\n\n4 (hundreds),\n\n1 (tens),\n\n5 (ones).\n\nSo that's:\n\n1,190,322,415.\n\nBut in the component breakdown method, I got 1,190,323,175. There's a difference of 700,000. Hmm, that's a significant error. Where did I go wrong?\n\nWait, let me re-examine the component breakdown approach:\n\nBreaking down the first number:\n\n831,100,114:\n\n800,000,000 (hundred millions)\n\n30,000,000 (ten millions)\n\n1,000,000 (millions)\n\n100,000 (hundred thousands)\n\n0 (ten thousands)\n\n0 (thousands)\n\n100 (hundreds)\n\n10 (tens)\n\n4 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 359,223,061:\n\n300,000,000 (hundred millions)\n\n50,000,000 (ten millions)\n\n9,000,000 (millions)\n\n200,000 (hundred thousands)\n\n20,000 (ten thousands)\n\n3,000 (thousands)\n\n0 (hundreds)\n\n60 (tens)\n\n1 (ones).\n\nNow when adding the hundred thousands place:\n\nFirst number has 100,000.\n\nSecond number has 200,000.\n\nTotal: 300,000 → correct.\n\nTen thousands place:\n\nFirst has 0, second has 20,000 → total 20,000.\n\nThousands place:\n\nFirst has 0, second has 3,000 → total 3,000.\n\nHundreds place: first has 100, second has 0 → total 100.\n\nTens: first has 10, second has 60 → total 70.\n\nOnes: 4+1=5.\n\nSo adding all parts:\n\n1,100,000,000 (hundred millions)\n\n+80,000,000 (ten millions: 30M +50M)\n\n+10,000,000 (millions:1M +9M)\n\n+300,000 (hundred thousands)\n\n+20,000 (ten thousands)\n\n+3,000 (thousands)\n\n+100 (hundreds)\n\n+70 (tens)\n\n+5 (ones).\n\nAdding step by step:\n\nStart with 1,100,000,000.\n\nAdd 80,000,000 → 1,180,000,000.\n\nAdd 10,000,000 → 1,190,000,000.\n\nAdd 300,000 → 1,190,300,000.\n\nAdd 20,000 → 1,190,320,000.\n\nAdd 3,000 → 1,190,323,000.\n\nAdd 100 → 1,190,323,100.\n\nAdd 70 → 1,190,323,170.\n\nAdd 5 → 1,190,323,175.\n\nHmm, so that's correct.\n\nBut in the digit-by-digit addition, I got 1,190,322,415. There's a discrepancy of 700,000. Where is the mistake?\n\nWait, let me check the digit-by-digit breakdown again. Maybe I messed up the places.\n\nLet me write both numbers aligned vertically:\n\n        831,100,114  \n      +359,223,061  \n      =============  \nLet's write them out digit by digit with place values:\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):\n\nPosition 1 (ones):\n\n4 (from first) +1 (from second) =5.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):\n\n1 +0 =1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\n1 +3=4.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\n0 +2=2.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\n0 +2=2.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\n1 +2=3.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\n1 +9=10 → write 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n3 +5 +1 (carryover) =9.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n8 +3=11 → write 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition10 (billions):\n\n1 (carryover).\n\nSo the digits from position1 to position10 are:\n\n5 (ones),\n\n1 (tens),\n\n4 (hundreds),\n\n2 (thousands),\n\n2 (ten thousands),\n\n3 (hundred thousands),\n\n0 (millions),\n\n9 (ten millions),\n\n1 (hundred millions),\n\n1 (billions).\n\nWait, hold on! Here's the mistake. When I listed the digits earlier, I might have miscounted the positions.\n\nLet me list the digits again properly:\n\nThe places from right to left (units to billions):\n\nPosition 1 (ones): 4 +1=5.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):1 +0=1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):1 +3=4.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):0 +2=2.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):