y-digit addition for the thousands place.\n\nLet me go through the thousands place again.\n\nFirst number: 831,100,114.\n\nBreaking down the thousands place:\n\nThe thousands digit is the fourth digit from the right.\n\nLet me write the first number as:\n\n8 (100M), 3 (10M),1 (M),1 (100k),0 (10k),0 (thousands),1 (hundreds),1 (tens),4 (ones).\n\nWait, the thousands place is the sixth digit from the right? Let me count:\n\nNumber: 8 3 1 1 0 0 1 1 4.\n\nPositions from right (ones is 1):\n\nPosition1:4\n\nPosition2:1 (tens)\n\nPosition3:1 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:0 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:0 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:1 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:1 (millions)\n\nPosition8:3 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:8 (hundred millions)\n\nWait, that can't be. Wait, perhaps I'm numbering incorrectly.\n\nWait, let's write the number as:\n\nDigit positions from right (units to left):\n\nNumber: 831,100,114.\n\nLet's separate each digit:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nDigit 1 (ones):4\n\nDigit2 (tens):1\n\nDigit3 (hundreds):1\n\nDigit4 (thousands):0\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):0\n\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):1\n\nDigit7 (millions):1\n\nDigit8 (ten millions):3\n\nDigit9 (hundred millions):8\n\nSo the thousands place (digit4) is 0 in the first number.\n\nSecond number:359,223,061.\n\nDigits from right:\n\nDigit1:1 (ones)\n\nDigit2:6 (tens) → wait no:\n\nWait 359,223,061:\n\nBreaking down each digit:\n\nDigit1 (ones):1\n\nDigit2 (tens):6\n\nDigit3 (hundreds):0\n\nDigit4 (thousands):3\n\nDigit5 (ten thousands):2\n\nDigit6 (hundred thousands):2\n\nDigit7 (millions):9\n\nDigit8 (ten millions):5\n\nDigit9 (hundred millions):3\n\nWait, so thousands place (digit4) in the second number is 3 (since 359,223,061's thousands digit is the 4th from the right: the number is 359,223,061 → the thousands digit is the fourth digit from the end: 0 (thousands place?) Let me write the second number's digits properly:\n\n359,223,061:\n\nDigits from left to right:\n\n3 (hundred millions),\n\n5 (ten millions)