-9):\n\n1:4, 2:1, 3:1, 4:0, 5:0, 6:1, 7:1, 8:3, 9:8.\n\nSecond:359,223,061 → digits from right (positions1-9):\n\n1:1, 2:6, 3:0, 4:3, 5:2, 6:2, 7:9, 8:5, 9:3.\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nPosition1 (ones):4+1=5.\n\nPosition2 (tens):1+6=7.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):1+0=1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):0+3=3.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):0+2=2.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):1+2=3.\n\nPosition7 (millions):1+9=10 → write 0, carryover 1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3+5 +1 (carryover) =9.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):8+3=11 → write 1, carryover 1.\n\nPosition10 (billions): carryover 1.\n\nNow compiling the digits from position1 to position10:\n\nPosition1:5 (ones)\n\nPosition2:7 (tens)\n\nPosition3:1 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:3 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:2 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:3 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:0 (millions)\n\nPosition8:9 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:1 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition10:1 (billions).\n\nSo the number is:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:1,\n\nTen millions:9,\n\nMillions:0,\n\nHundred thousands:3,\n\nTen thousands:2,\n\nThousands:3,\n\nHundreds:1,\n\nTens:7,\n\nOnes:5.\n\nPutting this together with commas:\n\n1 (billion),\n\n1 (hundred million),\n\n9 (ten million),\n\n0 (million),\n\n3 (hundred thousand),\n\n2 (ten thousand),\n\n3 (thousand),\n\n1 (hundred),\n\n7 (ten),\n\n5 (one).\n\nSo that's 1,190,323,175, which matches the component breakdown method. \n\nWow, so my initial mistake was in the thousands place digit of the second number. I had thought it was 2 instead of 3. That's why the first calculation was off. \n\nTherefore, the correct sum is 1,190,323,175. The user's answer was 1,990,323,175 which is incorrect because it's 1.19 billion versus 1.99 billion. The correct answer is 1,190,323,175.\n\nSo to summarize the correct digit-by-digit steps:\n\n- Ones:4+1=5\n\n- Tens:1+6=7\n\n- Hundreds:1+0=1\n\n- Thousands:0+3=3\n\n- Ten thousands:0+2=2\n\n- Hundred thousands:1+2=3\n\n- Millions:1+9=10 (write 0, carryover 1)\n\n- Ten milli