he first number has 7 (ten millions digit), second has 6 (ten millions digit). So 7+6=13, plus any carryover? The previous carryover was from the hundred thousands place (which was 1). Wait, the hundred thousands place sum was 7+9+1 (carryover from ten thousands?), no, let me retrace:\n\nWait, when adding the hundred thousands place (the 6th digit from the right):\n\nThe first number's hundred thousands digit is 7 (since 874,747,711 is 8 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions),4 (millions),7 (hundred thousands), etc.), so the digits are:\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):8\n\n8 (ten millions):7\n\n7 (millions):4\n\n6 (hundred thousands):7\n\n5 (ten thousands):4\n\n4 (thousands):7\n\n3 (hundreds):7\n\n2 (tens):1\n\n1 (ones):1\n\nSimilarly for the second number 960,954,509:\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):9\n\n8 (ten millions):6\n\n7 (millions):0\n\n6 (hundred thousands):9\n\n5 (ten thousands):5\n\n4 (thousands):4\n\n3 (hundreds):5\n\n2 (tens):0\n\n1 (ones):9\n\nSo when adding the hundred thousands place (position6):\n\nFirst number:7 (from 7 in hundred thousands place)\n\nSecond number:9 (from 9 in hundred thousands place)\n\nplus any carryover from the previous (ten thousands place which was 10, so carried over 1).\n\nSo 7+9+1=17 → write 7, carryover1 to the millions place.\n\nThen millions place (position7):\n\nFirst number:4 (millions digit)\n\nSecond number:0 (millions digit)\n\nplus carryover 1 →4+0+1=5. So no carryover.\n\nThen ten millions place (position8):\n\nFirst number:7 (ten millions)\n\nSecond number:6 (ten millions)\n\nplus any carryover? No, the carryover from millions was none. So 7+6=13 → write 3, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions place (position9):\n\n8 +9 +1 (carryover) =18 → 8, carryover1.\n\nThus the billions place is the 1 carried over. So that's correct. So the user's answer must have an error, likely adding 8+9 in the hundred millions and got 17 but then added something else incorrectly. Alternatively, maybe they thought 8+9 is 17, so wrote 7 and carried 1, then 7 (from ten millions) +6 +1 carried over? Wait, if they did that, the ten millions would be 7+6 +1 =14, so 4 with carryover1, but that would lead to different numbers. Maybe they messed up the order of the digits.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they added the numbers as 874,747,711 + 960,954,509 and thought the first digit is 8+9=17, so 7 with carryover1, then 7+6+1=14, so 4 with carryover1, then 4+0+1=5, etc. But that would give 17 in the first digits, leading to 1,745... but no, the correct answer is 1,835... Hmm, maybe the user's answer is a typo? Or miscalculation.\n\nIn any case, following the step-by-step addition, the correct sum is 1,835,702,220. Therefore, the user's provided answer of 6,835,702,220 is incorrect. I should point that out and provide the correct reasoning.\n</think>", ''] 96%|#########6| 482/500 [19:15:45<40:21, 134.53s/it]
