swer, it's 4. That's conflicting.\n\nWait, user's answer has 1,167,812,497.\n\nLooking at the digits:\n\nBreaking down 1,167,812,497:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:7,\n\nTens:9,\n\nHundreds:4,\n\nThousands:2,\n\nTen thousands:1,\n\nHundred thousands:8,\n\nMillions:7,\n\nTen millions:6,\n\nHundred millions:1,\n\nBillions:1.\n\nWait a second, so in the hundreds place, they have 4, but according to our calculation it's 6. So where did that 4 come from?\n\nHmm, maybe I made a mistake in the hundreds place.\n\nWait, let's check again:\n\nPosition7 (hundreds):\n\nFirst number: 0 (since in the first number breakdown, position7 is hundreds, which was 0).\n\nSecond number:6 (position7 is hundreds in the second number, which is 6).\n\n0+6=6. So that should be 6 in the hundreds place. But the user's answer has 4 there. So that's an inconsistency.\n\nWait, perhaps I made an error in the breakdown of the original numbers.\n\nLet me confirm the original numbers again:\n\nFirst number is 187,369,043.\n\nLet me parse this: 187,369,043.\n\nBreaking into digits:\n\n1 (hundred millions),\n\n8 (ten millions),\n\n7 (millions),\n\n3 (hundred thousands),\n\n6 (ten thousands),\n\n9 (thousands),\n\n0 (hundreds),\n\n4 (tens),\n\n3 (ones).\n\nYes, that's correct. So hundreds place is 0.\n\nSecond number:980,443,654.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n9 (hundred millions),\n\n8 (ten millions),\n\n0 (millions),\n\n4 (hundred thousands),\n\n4 (ten thousands),\n\n3 (thousands),\n\n6 (hundreds),\n\n5 (tens),\n\n4 (ones).\n\nAh! Wait a second, in the second number, the hundreds digit is 6 (since the breakdown is ...3 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 4 (ones). So that's correct. So adding 0 +6 gives 6. So the hundreds digit should be 6, but the user's answer has 4 there. That suggests the user might have made an error, or perhaps I made a mistake in another column.\n\nWait, let's check all digits again step by step carefully:\n\nStarting over:\n\nFirst number: 187,369,043\n\nSecond number:980,443,654\n\