final_output:  ["600,000,000 +56,341,301 =656,341,301.\n\nWait, but earlier when adding digit by digit I got 656,323,301. Hmm, there's a discrepancy here. So I must have made a mistake in one of the steps.\n\nWait let me check the digit-by-digit again.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\nFirst number: 211,964,427.\n\nLet me write each digit:\n\nHundred millions: 2.\n\nTen millions:1.\n\nMillions:1.\n\nHundred thousands:9.\n\nTen thousands:6.\n\nThousands:4.\n\nHundreds:4.\n\nTens:2.\n\nOnes:7.\n\nSecond number:444,376,874.\n\nHundred millions:4.\n\nTen millions:4.\n\nMillions:4.\n\nHundred thousands:3.\n\nTen thousands:7.\n\nThousands:6.\n\nHundreds:8.\n\nTens:7.\n\nOnes:4.\n\nWait, let me list each digit position by position:\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):\n\nPosition 1 (Ones):\n\nFirst number:7\n\nSecond number:4 → 7+4=11 → 1, carryover1.\n\nPosition 2 (Tens):\n\nFirst:2\n\nSecond:7 →2+7=9 +1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (Hundreds):\n\nFirst:4\n\nSecond:8 →4+8=12 +1=13 →3, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (Thousands):\n\nFirst:4\n\nSecond:6 →4+6=10 +1=11 →1? Wait wait, wait, no:\n\nWait first number's thousands digit: let me check again.\n\nWait the first number is 211,964,427.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n2 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 2 (tens),7 (ones).\n\nWait, so thousands place is 4.\n\nSecond number's thousands digit: the number is 444,376,874.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n4 (hundred millions),4 (ten millions),4 (millions),3 (hundred thousands),7 (ten thousands),6 (thousands),8 (hundreds),7 (tens),4 (ones).\n\nSo thousands place:6.\n\nSo 4 +6 =10, plus carryover 1 makes 11. Wait! Here's the mistake earlier. In step 4 (Thousands place), I previously thought first number's thousands digit was 6, but it's actually 4. So 4+6=10, plus carryover1 gives 11, so digit is 1, carryover1.\n\nWait that changes things. So my previous reasoning had an error here.\n\nSo let me redo the digit-by-digit step correctly:\n\n1. Ones:7+4=11 →1, carryover1.\n\n2. Tens:2+7=9 +1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\n3. Hundreds:4+8=12 +1=13 →3, carryover1.\n\n4. Thousands:4+6=10 +1=11 →1, carryover1.\n\n5. Ten-thousands:6 (from first number) +7 (from second) =13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nWait, ten thousands place:\n\nFirst number's ten thousands digit:6.\n\nSecond number's ten thousands digit:7 (since second number's breakdown has 7 in ten thousands place).\n\nSo 6+7=13, plus carryover1 gives 14 →4, carryover1.\n\n6. Hundred-thousands:9 (first number) +3 (second) =12 +1=13 →3, carryover1.\n\n7. Millions:1 (first number's millions digit is the third digit from left, which is 1) +4 (second number's millions digit is 4) →1+4=5 +1 (carryover) =6 →6, no carryover.\n\n8. Ten millions: first number's ten millions digit is 1, second's is4 →1+4=5.\n\n9. Hundred millions:2+4=6.\n\nSo now putting the digits from right to left (the digits we got):\n\nOnes:1.\n\nTens:0.\n\nHundreds:3.\n\nThousands:1.\n\nTen-thousands:4.\n\nHundred-thousands:3.\n\nMillions:6.\n\nTen-millions:5.\n\nHundred-millions:6.\n\nSo arranging from highest to lowest place:.\n\nHundred-millions (6), ten-millions (5), millions (6), hundred-thousands (3), ten-thousands (4), thousands (1), hundreds (3), tens (0), ones (1).\n\nWait that gives the number as 656,341,301.\n\nWhich matches the second method's result of 656,341,301.\n\nAh! So my initial mistake was in step 4 (thousands place), I had incorrectly thought the first number's thousands digit was 6 instead of 4. That's why I had discrepancies.\n\nTherefore, the correct sum is 656,341,301. But the user thinks the answer is 656,349,301. Hmm, so there must be another error here.\n\nWait let's recalculate step by step again carefully.\n\nFirst number: 211,964,427.\n\nSecond number:444,376,874.\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n211,964,427.\n\n+444,376,874.\n\nAdding each column:\n\nStarting from the right (ones):\n\nOnes:7+4=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nTens:2+7=9 +1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:4 (from first number) +8 (from second) =12 +1=13 →3, carryover1.\n\nThousands place: first number's thousands digit: looking at 211,964,427, the digits are:\n\nBreaking down 211,964,427:\n\n2 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 2 (tens),7 (ones). So thousands digit is 4.\n\nSecond number's thousands digit is 6 (from 444,376,874: 4 (hundred millions),4 (ten millions),4 (millions),3 (hundred thousands),7 (ten thousands),6 (thousands),8 (hundreds),7 (tens),4 (ones). So thousands digit is 6.\n\nSo 4+6 =10 +1 (carryover)=11 →1, carryover1.\n\nTen-thousands place:\n\nFirst number's ten thousands digit:6.\n\nSecond number's ten thousands digit:7.\n\n6+7=13 +1 (carryover)=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands place:\n\nFirst number:9 (hundred thousands).\n\nSecond number:3 (hundred thousands).\n\n9+3=12 +1=13 →3, carryover1.\n\nMillions place:\n\nFirst number's millions digit is 1 (third digit from left: 2-1-1).\n\nSecond number's millions digit is 4 (third digit from left: 4-4-4).\n\n1+4=5 +1 (carryover)=6 →6, no carryover.\n\nTen millions place:\n\nFirst number:1 (second digit from left).\n\nSecond number:4 (second digit from left).\n\n1+4=5.\n\nHundred millions place:\n\n2+4=6.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nHundred millions:6.\n\nTen millions:5.\n\nMillions:6.\n\nHundred thousands:3.\n\nTen thousands:4.\n\nThousands:1.\n\nHundreds:3.\n\nTens:0.\n\nOnes:1.\n\nSo combining: 6 (hundred millions),5 (ten millions),6 (millions),3 (hundred thousands),4 (ten thousands),1 (thousands),3 (hundreds),0 (tens),1 (ones).\n\nThat's 656,341,301.\n\nWait, so the user's answer is 656,349,301. The difference is in the thousands and hundred thousands place?\n\nWait let me check the hundred thousands place again:\n\nHundred thousands digit:\n\nFirst number's hundred thousands digit is 9.\n\nSecond number's is 3. 9+3=12 +1 (carryover from ten thousands place which was 14, so carryover 1) →13. So 3 in hundr