values:\n\nFirst number (789,658,324):\n\nPositions (from right, units to billions):\n\nPosition 1 (units):4.\n\nPosition2 (tens):2.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):3.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):8.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):5.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):6.\n\nPosition7 (millions):9.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):8.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):7.\n\nSecond number (207,374,479):\n\nPositions:\n\nPosition1 (units):9.\n\nPosition2 (tens):7.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):4.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):4.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):7.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):3.\n\nPosition7 (millions):0 (since it's 207 million, so the millions digit is 7, but in the breakdown, the seventh position from the right is millions. Wait:\n\nWait, for the second number:\n\nPosition7 (millions) is the 7 in 207, so yes, millions digit is 7? Wait, no.\n\nWait, let me re-express the second number's digits:\n\nSecond number:207,374,479.\n\nPositions from right (units):\n\n1:9.\n\n2:7.\n\n3:4.\n\n4:4 (thousands place?).\n\n5:7 (ten thousands).\n\n6:3 (hundred thousands).\n\n7:0 (millions? Wait, the 7 in 207,374,479 is in the millions place. Let's see:\n\nThe number is 207,374,479. Breaking down:\n\n- 2 (hundred millions).\n\n- 0 (ten millions).\n\n-7 (millions).\n\n-3 (hundred thousands).\n\n-7 (ten thousands).\n\n-4 (thousands).\n\n-4 (hundreds).\n\n-7 (tens).\n\n-9 (units).\n\nTherefore, the seventh position from the right (millions place) is 7.\n\nThus, in the second number's digits:\n\nPosition7 (millions):7.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):0.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):2.\n\nSo when adding the millions place (position7):\n\nFirst number has 9 (millions digit).\n\nSecond number has7 (millions digit).\n\nSo 9+7=16? Wait, but in the digit-by-digit approach earlier, when I added position7 (millions):\n\nFirst number's millions digit is9, second's is0 (no, wait second's is7). Wait, confusion arises from how I labeled the positions before. Let me clarify:\n\nIf we are numbering positions from the ri