 7 (millions):1\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):5\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):6\n\nWait, perhaps I need to align both numbers properly. Let me write them vertically:\n\nFirst number (534,280,104):\n\nDigit positions (from right):\n\n1: 4 (ones)\n\n2: 0 (tens)\n\n3: 1 (hundreds)\n\n4: 0 (thousands)\n\n5: 8 (ten thousands)\n\n6: 2 (hundred thousands)\n\n7: 4 (millions)\n\n8: 3 (ten millions)\n\n9:5 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number (651,658,122):\n\n1:2\n\n2:2\n\n3:1\n\n4:8\n\n5:5\n\n6:6\n\n7:1\n\n8:5\n\n9:6\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nStarting at position 1 (ones):\n\n4 + 2 = 6. Write 6, no carryover.\n\nPosition 2 (tens):\n\n0 + 2 = 2. Write 2, no carryover.\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):\n\n1 + 1 = 2. Write 2, no carryover.\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):\n\n0 +8 =8. Write 8, no carryover.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\n8 +5 =13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\n2 +6 =8, plus carryover 1 =9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\n4 +1 =5. Write 5, no carryover.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n3 +5 =8. Write 8, no carryover.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n5 +6 =11. So write 1, carryover 1. But since there are no higher digits, the carryover 1 becomes a new digit (billions place). So total digits now 10.\n\nSo putting all digits together:\n\nStarting from the first digit (billions place:1), then hundred millions (1), ten millions (8?), wait let me see:\n\nWait, the positions after adding:\n\nPosition 1:6\n\n2:2\n\n3:2\n\n4:8\n\n5:3\n\n6:9\n\n7:5\n\n8:8\n\n9:1 (from 5+6=11, so digit is 1 and carryover 1)\n\nBut the carryover adds a new digit (position 10) which is 1.\n\nSo the digits from position 1 to 10 are:\n\nPosition10:1\n\nPosition9:1\n\nPosition8:8\n\nPosition7:5\n\nPosition6:9\n\nPosition5:3\n\nPosition4:8\n\nPosition3:2\n\nPosition2:2\n\nPosition1:6\n\nWait, that\'s a bit confusing. Let me write the digits from left to right (starting from the highest place):\n\nBillions place (position10):1\n\nHundred millions (position9):1 (since 5+6=11, so the 1 is in the hundred millions, but actually, the 5 and 6 are in the hundred millions place, so their sum 11 would put 1 in the hundred millions and carryover to billions. Wait, perhaps I need to think in terms of building the number step by step.\n\nLet me list the digits from the highest to lowest:\n\nAfter adding:\n\n- The billions place: the carryover 1 from the hundred millions addition.\n\n- Hundred millions place: 1 (since 5+6=11, so 1 is written here, plus the carryover? Wait no, the 5 and 6 in hundred millions add to 11, so the digit in hundred millions is 1, and carryover 1 to billions.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:1 (from 11)\n\nTen millions:3+5=8 (no carryover here?)\n\nWait, let\'s go step by step again:\n\nLet me start from the right:\n\nPositions:\n\n1: 4 +2=6\n\n2:0+2=2\n\n3:1+1=2\n\n4:0+8=8\n\n5:8+5=13 (write 3, carryover 1)\n\n6:2+6=8 +1=9 (no carryover)\n\n7:4+1=5\n\n8:3+5=8\n\n9:5+6=11 (write 1, carryover 1)\n\nThen the carryover 1 goes to a new position (position10: billions).\n\nSo the digits from position1 to 10 are:\n\nPosition1:6\n\nPosition2:2\n\nPosition3:2\n\nPosition4:8\n\nPosition5:3\n\nPosition6:9\n\nPosition7:5\n\nPosition8:8\n\nPosition9:1\n\nPosition10:1\n\nSo writing the number from position10 down:\n\n1 (billions), 1 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 8 (millions?), wait let\'s arrange properly:\n\nThe digits are:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:1,\n\nTen millions:8,\n\nMillions:5,\n\nHundred thousands:9,\n\nTen thousands:3,\n\nThousands:8,\n\nHundreds:2,\n\nTens:2,\n\nOnes:6.\n\nWait, arranging the digits in order from highest to lowest:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:1,\n\nTen millions:8,\n\nMillions:5,\n\nHundred thousands:9,\n\nTen thousands:3,\n\nThousands:8,\n\nHundreds:2,\n\nTens:2,\n\nOnes:6.\n\nSo putting this together as a number: 1,185,938,226.\n\nWait, but the user said the correct answer is 1,185,838,226. Hmm, so there\'s a discrepancy here. Where did I